\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Michigan's Primary,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and Economy Drive Choices for Voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bosman \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Voters in the Detroit suburbs said issues including the economy and bodily autonomy were driving their ballot decisions.\par \par \uc2 HAZEL PARK, Mich. -- Ashley Polansky, a 34-year-old retail manager, hurried inside a polling place at a tiny church to vote on Tuesday morning with one issue foremost in her mind.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm three months pregnant,'' she said, describing her worst fears about giving birth without the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''If something goes wrong, the doctors could just let me die.''\par \par \uc2 To Ms. Polansky, re-electing Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  -- a Democrat who has campaigned heavily on defending the precarious right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Michigan -- feels like a literal matter of life or death for her and other women. They fear that if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in the state, complications from a pregnancy would leave their own health vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 As Election Day unfolded, the voters of Hazel Park in Oakland County, a Democratic-leaning stretch of suburbia outside Detroit, framed their motivations in existential terms.\par \par \uc2 Life in Michigan, both Democrats and Republicans said, is weighed down by worries. In Hazel Park, a well-kept town of small bungalows lined by chain-link fences, the concerns are numerous: the preservation of democratic elections, maintaining autonomy over their own bodies and health, affording food for their families.\par \par \uc2 For many voting Republican, economic issues dominate.\par \par \uc2 ''I just wish that things would get better,'' said Bruce Roach, 44, who works construction for a cable company. ''We need to get the party that's in power out.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Roach, like many people in politically diverse suburban Detroit, has toggled back and forth between party affiliations. He voted for former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  twice, and then voted for former President Trump in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 He appreciated the stimulus checks he received under President Biden, he said, but has been struggling mightily to get by as inflation has made the cost of everything soar. Gas prices are too high for him to drive his children to visit their grandparents in Tennessee. At the grocery store, Mr. Roach used to be able to afford the premium Boar's Head brand but now feeds his family prepackaged meat that he calls ''nitrate-filled processed yuck.''\par \par \uc2 Many people have seen their investments crater this year as the stock market has fallen, a development they blame on President Biden.\par \par \uc2 ''I've lost $72,000 since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became president,'' said Robert Thomas, a 62-year-old retired machinist, who arrived at a polling place on his bicycle, a practice he relies on to save money.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Van Stee, 35, a creative director, said she voted Democratic, citing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a primary motivator. While she has not suffered economically -- she has a good job, makes decent money and has owned a house in town since 2019 -- she knows her situation is not the norm.\par \par \uc2 ''Life is hard for a lot of people in this area,'' Ms. Van Stee said. ''My neighbors bring up economic issues all the time. It's a lot of, 'Can you believe how much cereal costs?'''\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday afternoon, voters streamed out of a modern public library across from a Mexican restaurant in the nearby suburb of Ferndale.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Williams, 52, a publishing executive, said she didn't normally vote in primary elections but was determined to cast a ballot this time because there was so much at stake.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, human rights, protection of marriage equality,'' she said. ''I feel like they're all personal issues.''\par \par \uc2 Voters in Detroit who arrived at the polls on Election Day said they were focused on the fundamentals: protecting democracy and exercising a right to vote that they have not always held.\par \par \uc2 ''I just think about those that sacrificed for us to be able to vote,'' John Williams, 70, said as he stood outside Bagley Elementary School on Detroit's northwest side.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Williams, who is retired from law enforcement, said that for him, knowing of Republican efforts to tighten access to the ballot answered the question of whether he should bother voting in a primary.\par \par \uc2 ''It's always been a constant and an emphatic 'yes' over the past few years,'' he said, ''with the direction which I see America going.''\par \par \uc2 If the issues in this election feel insurmountable, maybe the pandemic is still to blame, said Gordon Novak, 61, a retired entrepreneur who lives in Ferndale.\par \par \uc2 ''We're still dealing with the adjustment after the pandemic,'' Mr. Novak said. ''The shortages of stuff, supply chains being backed up. Everything feels out of whack.''\par \par \uc2 Carolyn Gentry, who is 52 and a Democrat, said that there was a time when she could talk about the issues with her Republican friends in Michigan, but in the current political mood in the state, and with so many Republicans believing that the 2020 election was fraudulent, that no longer feels possible.\par \par \uc2 ''I used to engage in tons of discussions, thinking we could come to some reasonable truth,'' she said. ''There's no understanding of what truth is anymore.''\par \par \uc2 Reporting contributed by Sam Easter and Ryan Hooper.\par \par \uc2 Reporting contributed by Sam Easter and Ryan Hooper.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Polansky, who voted in Hazel Park, Mich., is pregnant and concerned about the erosion of \uc2  rights. ''If something goes wrong,'' she said, ''the doctors could just let me die.''; Gordon and Chrissi Novak voted at a library in Ferndale, Mich. Mr. Novak said lingering problems from the pandemic played a role in the election. ''Everything feels out of whack,'' he said. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CYDNI ELLEDGE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220803ei8300048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
