\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Kansas Retains  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights In Critical Post-Roe Referendum\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitch Smith and Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,688 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the first election test after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, voters overwhelmingly rejected an amendment that would have allowed legislators to enact \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 OVERLAND PARK, Kan. -- Kansas voters resoundingly decided against removing the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the State Constitution, according to The Associated Press, a major victory for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement in one of America's reliably conservative states.\par \par \uc2 The defeat of the ballot referendum was the most tangible demonstration yet of a political backlash against the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, the landmark decision that had protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights throughout the country. The decisive margin -- 60 to 39 percent, with about 80 percent of the votes counted as of Tuesday night -- came as a surprise, and after frenzied campaigns with both sides pouring millions into advertising and knocking on doors throughout a sweltering final campaign stretch.\par \par \uc2 ''The voters in Kansas have spoken loud and clear: We will not tolerate extreme bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Rachel Sweet, the campaign manager for Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, which led the effort to defeat the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sweet told supporters that a willingness to work across partisan lines and ideological differences helped their side win.\par \par \uc2 Registered Republicans far outnumber Democrats in Kansas -- and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists made explicit appeals to unaffiliated voters and center-right voters. In interviews last week in populous Johnson County, Kan., a number of voters said they were registered Republicans but opposed the amendment -- a dynamic that almost certainly played out across the state, given the margin.\par \par \uc2 ''We're watching the votes come in, we're seeing the changes of some of the counties where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a huge percentage of the vote, and we're seeing that just decimated,'' said Jo Dee Adelung, 63, a Democrat from Merriam, Kan., who knocked on doors and called voters in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 She said she hoped the result sent a message that voters are ''really taking a look at all of the issues and doing what's right for Kansas and not just going down party lines.''\par \par \uc2 Value Them Both, a group leading the vote-yes effort, said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , ''This outcome is a temporary setback, and our dedicated fight to value women and babies is far from over.''\par \par \uc2 The vote in Kansas, three months before the midterm elections, was the first time American voters weighed in directly on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade this summer.\par \par \uc2 The referendum, watched closely by national figures on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, took on added importance because of Kansas' location, abutting states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already banned in nearly all cases. More than $12 million has been spent on advertising, split about evenly between the two camps. The amendment, had it passed, would have removed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections from the State Constitution and paved the way for legislators to ban or restrict abortions.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the vote, which coincided with primary elections, Scott Schwab, the Republican secretary of state, predicted that around 36 percent of Kansas voters would participate, up slightly from the primary in 2020, a presidential election year, though he later said there were signs turnout would be much higher. His office said that the constitutional amendment ''has increased voter interest in the election,'' a sentiment that was palpable on the ground.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been saying that after a decision is made in Washington, that the spotlight would shift to Kansas,'' said David Langford, a retired engineer from Leawood, Kan., who wanted the amendment to pass, and who reached out to Protestant pastors to rally support.\par \par \uc2 While Kansas has a history of voting for governors of both parties, the state almost always backs Republicans for president -- Lyndon B. Johnson in 1964 was a notable exception. It is a largely white state and many Kansans identify as Christians, with a sizable evangelical constituency. Roman Catholic Archbishop Joseph F. Naumann of Kansas City, Kan., has long been a hero to many conservative Catholics for his ardent opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 The push for an amendment was rooted in a 2019 ruling by the Kansas Supreme Court that struck down some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and found that the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was guaranteed by the State Constitution. That decision infuriated Republicans, who had spent years passing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and campaigning on the issue. They used their supermajorities in the Legislature last year to place the issue on the 2022 ballot.\par \par \uc2 That state-level fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits took on far greater meaning after the nation's top court overturned Roe, opening the door in June for states to go beyond restrictions and outlaw abortions entirely. The Roman Catholic Church and other religious and conservative groups spent heavily to back the amendment, while national supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights poured millions of dollars into the race to oppose it.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the amendment had said repeatedly that the amendment itself would not ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Republican lawmakers were careful to avoid telegraphing what their legislative plans would be if it passed.\par \par \uc2 ''Voting yes doesn't mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be allowed, it means we're going to allow our legislators to determine the scope of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Mary Jane Muchow of Overland Park, Kan., who supported the amendment. ''I think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal, but I think there should be limitations on it.''\par \par \uc2 If the amendment had passed, though, the question was not whether Republicans would try to wield their commanding legislative majorities to pass new restrictions, but how far they would go in doing so. Many Kansans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said they feared that a total or near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would be passed within months\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now legal in Kansas up to 22 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't want to become another state that bans all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason,'' said Barbara Grigar of Overland Park, who identified herself as a moderate and said she was voting against the amendment. ''Choice is every woman's choice, and not the government's.''\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey published last month found that a majority of Americans said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, and that more than half of adults disapproved of the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Kansas has been a focal point of the national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate at least since 1991, when protesters from across the country gathered in Wichita and blocked access to clinics during weeks of heated demonstrations that they called the Summer of Mercy.\par \par \uc2 At times, the state has seen violence over the issue. In 1986, a Wichita \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic was attacked with a pipe bomb. In 1993, a woman who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shot and injured Dr. George Tiller, one of only a few American physicians who performed late-term abortions. In 2009, another anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist shot and killed Dr. Tiller at his Wichita church.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, and especially in the weeks since Roe fell, Kansas has become a haven of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in a region where that is increasingly rare.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Supreme Court's action, nearly half of the abortions performed in Kansas involved out-of-state residents. Now Oklahoma and Missouri have banned the procedure in almost all cases, Nebraska may further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the next few months, and women from Arkansas and Texas, where new bans are in place, are traveling well beyond their states' borders.\par \par \uc2 Kansas voters are generally conservative on many issues, but polling before the referendum suggested a close race and nuanced public opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The state is not a political monolith: Besides its Democratic governor, a majority of Kansas Supreme Court justices were appointed by Democrats, and Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , a Democrat, represents the Kansas City suburbs in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davids's district was once a moderate Republican stronghold, but it has been trending toward Democrats in recent years. Her re-election contest in November in a redrawn district may be one of the most competitive House races in the country, and party strategists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate to play an important role in districts like hers that include swaths of upscale suburbs.\par \par \uc2 Political strategists have been particularly attuned to turnout in the Kansas City suburbs, and are seeking to gauge how galvanizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is, especially for swing voters and Democrats in a post-Roe environment.\par \par \uc2 ''They're going to see how to advise their candidates to talk about the issue, they're going to be looking at every political handicap,'' said James Carville, the veteran Democratic strategist. ''Every campaign consultant, everybody is watching this thing like it's the Super Bowl.''\par \par \uc2 As the election approached, and especially since the Supreme Court decision, rhetoric on the issue became more heated. Campaign signs on both sides have been vandalized, police officials and activists have said. In the Kansas City suburb of Overland Park, vandals targeted a Catholic church, defacing a building and a statue of Mary with red paint.\par \par \uc2 Before the vote on Tuesday, which coincided with primary elections, Scott Schwab, the Republican secretary of state, predicted that around 36 percent of Kansas voters would participate, up slightly from the primary in 2020, a presidential election year. His office said that the constitutional amendment ''has increased voter interest in the election,'' a sentiment that was palpable on the ground.\par \par \uc2 ''I like the women's rights,'' said Norma Hamilton, a 90-year-old Republican from Lenexa, Kan. Despite her party registration, she said, she voted no.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Dias contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Dias contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Celebrating in Overland Park, Kan., on Tuesday after voters rejected a referendum that could have led to the restriction of \uc2  rights. \uc2  is legal in Kansas up to 22 weeks of pregnancy. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ARIN YOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Faye and Rich Bott, who supported the unsuccessful effort to amend the State Constitution, prayed in Overland Park. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHRISTOPHER SMITH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A17) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220803ei8300045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
