\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justice Dept. Sues Idaho Over New  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlie Savage \par \uc2 892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration filed its first new litigation to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court ruling in June that ended the constitutional right to terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Biden administration sued Idaho on Tuesday over a strict state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law set to take effect this month that the Justice Department said would inhibit emergency room doctors from performing abortions that are necessary to stabilize the health of women facing medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, announced by Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2 , is the first Biden administration has filed to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court ruling in late June that ended the constitutional right to terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Mr. Garland noted at a news conference on Tuesday, ''there have been widespread reports of delays and denials of treatment to pregnant women experiencing emergencies.'' The lawsuit argues that a federal law, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, bars states from imposing restrictions that would prevent emergency room doctors from treating those women.\par \par \uc2 ''If a patient comes into the emergency room with a medical emergency jeopardizing the patient's life or health, the hospital must provide the treatment necessary to stabilize that patient,'' Mr. Garland said. ''This includes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when that is the necessary treatment.''\par \par \uc2 The litigation came as voters in Kansas resoundingly decided to uphold a 2019 ruling by the state's Supreme Court interpreting the state's Constitution as protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The ballot initiative is the first referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision in late June.\par \par \uc2 Last month, after the federal \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  put out guidance to ensure access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in certain emergency situations at hospitals that take Medicare funding, Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  of Texas filed a lawsuit challenging the rules.\par \par \uc2 The new case raises similar legal issues about the scope of federal law to protect emergency room doctors who decide abortions are necessary to treat dangerous pregnancy complications that fall short of a direct threat to the life of a patient. This time, however, the federal government is the plaintiff, not the defendant.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is also seeking an injunction barring Idaho from enforcing its strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law on emergency room doctors, nurses and lab technicians who assist with abortions in emergency situations -- including instances in which women face conditions like ectopic pregnancies, severe pre-eclampsia or pregnancy complications threatening septic infections or hemorrhages.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  contained a trigger that would allow it to take effect shortly after any ruling by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturning its Roe v. Wade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights precedent. Because the court issued such a ruling earlier this summer, the Idaho law is set to take effect in about three weeks.\par \par \uc2 The legislation bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except when necessary to save the life of a pregnant woman -- but not to protect her health -- or in cases of rape or incest that were previously reported to the authorities.\par \par \uc2 It allows law enforcement officials to arrest and indict a doctor whenever an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been performed, regardless of the circumstances; it is up to the doctor, as a defense at trial, to prove that one of the narrow exceptions to the ban applied. As a result, critics of the law say that doctors will be afraid to perform abortions under any circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's lawsuit seeks a declaration from a court that Idaho's law is invalid if applied to situations covered by the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act because the U.S. Constitution makes federal law supreme over state law where the two conflict.\par \par \uc2 ''Even in dire situations that might qualify for the Idaho law's limited 'necessary to prevent the death of the pregnant woman' affirmative defense, some providers could withhold care based on a well-founded fear of criminal prosecution,'' the department's complaint said.\par \par \uc2 It continued: ''Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law will therefore prevent doctors from performing abortions even when a doctor determines that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the medically necessary treatment to prevent severe risk to the patient's health and even in cases where denial of care will likely result in death for the pregnant patient.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  of Idaho, a Republican, described the lawsuit as ''federal meddling'' and said the Supreme Court had ''returned the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states to regulate -- end of story.''\par \par \uc2 ''The \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 's interference with Idaho's pro-life law is another example of Biden overreaching yet again,'' Mr. Little said.\par \par \uc2 Appearing alongside Mr. Garland, Vanita Gupta, the associate attorney general and the head of a Justice Department task force on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that her working group had been studying the ''fast-changing landscape'' of state legal restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court's ruling. She suggested that more lawsuits were likely to follow.\par \par \uc2 ''We know that these are frightening and uncertain times for pregnant women and their providers,'' she said. ''The Justice Department, through the work of its task force, is committed to doing everything we can to ensure continued, lawful access to reproductive services.''\par \par \uc2 The suit, announced by Merrick B. Garland, was the first by the government since the Roe ruling. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DREW ANGERER/GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220803ei830003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
