\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Effect Looms In 5 States' Primaries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his election conspiracy theories will play a central role in big races in at least three states that are voting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Voters in five states head to the primaries on Tuesday to decide races that will shape the Republican Party and perhaps America's democratic future this November and beyond, with former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  playing key roles in marquee races in Arizona, Michigan and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Few states have been more rattled by Mr. Trump's baseless claims of election rigging than Arizona and Michigan. On Tuesday, Republican voters in those states will choose standard-bearers for governors' races in November, and in Arizona will also nominate a candidate for secretary of state, the post that oversees elections.\par \par \uc2 Also on the ballot will be the Republican nominations for Senate races in Arizona, Missouri and Washington. Republican voters will also decide the fate of three of the 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach Mr. Trump for inciting the Jan. 6, 2021, attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Here are the key races to watch:\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, Trump is front and center.\par \par \uc2 The former president turned against Arizona's governor, \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , after Mr. Ducey certified \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr\uc2 .'s narrow victory in the state and refused to echo Mr. Trump's lies about a stolen election. The race to succeed Mr. Ducey has been dominated by that issue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's preferred candidate, the former news anchor Kari Lake, has repeated outlandish falsehoods about the 2020 election and embraced provocations like vowing to bomb smuggling tunnels on the southern border. Her main opponent, Karrin Taylor Robson, a real estate developer endorsed by Mr. Trump's vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , is running on conservative themes but not on election denial.\par \par \uc2 On the Democratic side, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , Arizona's secretary of state, is favored to win the nomination, setting up what is expected to be a tight, high-stakes contest this fall.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump again figures in the Republican primary to take on Senator Mark Kelly, a Democrat, this November, a key front in the battle for control of a Senate now divided evenly between the parties. The former president's endorsement of the political newcomer \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  helped vault the quirky technology executive into the lead, but the state's attorney general, \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 , could benefit from the barrage of attack ads aimed at Mr. Masters from another Senate candidate, the solar power executive Jim Lamon.\par \par \uc2 The race for the Republican nomination for secretary of state features Mark Finchem, a state representative and expansive conspiracy theorist who marched on the Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Show me the fate of \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The race to succeed Senator \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2 , the Missouri Republican who is retiring, should have been a gimme for the Show Me State's Republicans, who now dominate statewide office. But the attempted political comeback of \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  has complicated matters. In 2018, Mr. Greitens resigned as governor in disgrace amid an investigation into fund-raising improprieties and an allegation by his former hairdresser that he had lured her to his home, stripped off her clothes, taped her to exercise equipment, photographed her, threatened to make the photos public if she talked and then coerced her into oral sex.\par \par \uc2 Taking a page from Mr. Trump, Mr. Greitens dismissed the allegations as cooked up by his political enemies -- Democrats and ''Republicans in name only'' -- as he plotted a comeback by running for Senate. Prominent Republicans in Missouri and Washington, D.C., split their endorsements between the state's attorney general, \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 , and a conservative House member, \uc2 Vicky Hartzler\uc2 , giving Mr. Greitens a path to the nomination -- and Democrats a plausible shot at the seat.\par \par \uc2 In the closing weeks, affluent donors dumped money into an anti-Greitens super PAC, Show Me Values, which blistered Mr. Greitens with his former wife's accusations of domestic violence against her and one of their young sons. The group's backers were confident another candidate would prevail.\par \par \uc2 Despite \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 .'s backing of Mr. Greitens, his father, the former president, never came through with an endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Missouri Democrats will have a difficult time grabbing the seat even if Mr. Greitens prevails. And a new complication has threatened Democratic unity: The party had largely gotten behind Lucas Kunce, a telegenic former Marine, but his coronation was interrupted by the late rise of Trudy Busch Valentine, the free-spending heiress to the \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2  fortune.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, democracy (and Israel) on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Up and down the state's primary tickets, Michiganders who deny President Biden's clear, 2.8-percentage-point victory in their state are vying to defeat politicians from both parties who accept the results.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Kelley, who was arrested last month by the F.B.I. for his actions at the Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021, is running to unseat Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, though in the most recent polling he trails the conservative media personality Tudor Dixon -- whose views on the 2020 election have wavered -- and the self-funding businessman Kevin Rinke.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon picked up Mr. Trump's endorsement on Friday, but it was unclear whether his supporters in the state would rally behind her after warring for months with Ms. Dixon's chief backer, \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 , and her relatives, the most influential Republican family in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 In the Western Michigan House seat centered in Grand Rapids, a Trump-backed election denier, John Gibbs, is trying to take out Representative Peter Meijer, a freshman Republican who not only accepts the election results but also voted to impeach Mr. Trump for inciting the attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee aired an advertisement in the final days of the campaign lifting Mr. Gibbs, a potentially far weaker candidate in November than Mr. Meijer, by highlighting his conservative credentials for Republican primary voters, a move that infuriated some Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In Eastern Michigan's Detroit suburbs, redistricting pitted two incumbent Democratic House members, \uc2 Andy Levin\uc2  and Haley Stevens, against one another. That race has turned into a battle royal between progressive groups backing Mr. Levin and pro-Israel groups determined to punish him for what they see as a bias toward Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 The impeachers' penultimate stand.\par \par \uc2 Three of the 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach Mr. Trump face their day of reckoning on Tuesday. Their fate will say much about Mr. Trump's power with primary voters. Besides Mr. Meijer, Representatives \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2  and \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2 , both of Washington, are being challenged by Republicans endorsed by Mr. Trump as part of his revenge tour.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Beutler faces the most prominent opponent, Joe Kent, a square-jawed, retired Green Beret whose wife was killed by a suicide bomber in northeast Syria in 2019 while fighting the Islamic State. Mr. Kent has run into an odd problem of his own: accusations from the furthest fringe of the right that he is a deep-state denizen working for the C.I.A. No moderate, Mr. Kent insists that the 2020 election was stolen and that those jailed after the storming of the Capitol are political prisoners.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Newhouse has largely kept his head down since voting to impeach, but he, too, has a Trump-backed challenger, Loren Culp, a retired law enforcement officer who was the Republicans' candidate for governor of Washington in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Of the impeachment 10, so far four have retired; one, Representative Tom Rice of South Carolina, has lost his primary; and one, Representative David Valadao of California, has survived his primary. After Tuesday, just one more awaits a primary: Representative \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  of Wyoming, whose uphill fight will be decided on Aug. 16.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Kansas will be the first since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade to decide for themselves whether to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  or turn the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over to state legislators.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's ballot will include an amendment to the state constitution that would remove an existing guarantee of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow the Legislature to pass laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The returns in Kansas will be closely watched, not only by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and Democrats, for signs of the potency of the issue in the midterm elections, but also by Republican state lawmakers in Kansas and beyond, who felt empowered by the Supreme Court's decision but are unsure how far they should go to bar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their states.\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Democrats see danger ahead.\par \par \uc2 The power of incumbency is proved time and again, but with inflation at a 40-year-high, President Biden's approval ratings well below 40 percent and congressional redistricting taking a toll, holding elective office is no guarantee of keeping it.\par \par \uc2 In Kansas, \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2 , a Democratic governor in a deep-red state, has an approval rating of 56 percent, 23 percentage points higher than Mr. Biden's, but her relative success may not save her tossup race against her expected Republican challenger, Attorney General Derek Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 In the Kansas City, Kan., suburbs, Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2  -- a gay former mixed-martial arts fighter and one of the first two Native American women in the House -- was hailed as a path-breaker after her 2018 victory. But redistricting redrew her seat from a slight Democratic lean to a slight Republican edge.\par \par \uc2 If Amanda Adkins, a businesswoman and former congressional aide, wins the Republican primary on Tuesday, November's race will be a rematch of their 2020 contest, which Ms. Davids won easily. But this time, the circumstances will be more difficult for the incumbent.\par \par \uc2 If the political environment deteriorates further for Democrats, another incumbent in a Tuesday primary, Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  of Washington, could pop up on both parties' radar screens.\par \par \uc2 In the nonpartisan Washington primary, Ms. Murray is expected to cruise, as will the Republican backed by the party establishment, Tiffany Smiley. A nurse and motivational speaker, Ms. Smiley will lean on a biography that includes the blinding of her husband by a suicide bomber in Iraq, a tragedy that drove her to veterans' causes. But her main argument is that 30 years after Ms. Murray first won her Senate seat as a ''mom in tennis shoes,'' it's time for ''a new mom in town.''\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake at a rally hosted by former President Donald J. Trump in Prescott Valley, Ariz., last month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ash Ponders for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220802ei820004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
