\par\par \uc2 SIDEBAR\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Decision, Haunted by Brown v. Board of Education\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The justices cited the landmark 1954 decision banning segregation in public schools 23 times, debating its meaning and methods.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- In the Supreme Court decision that eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the justices engaged in an extended debate over the meaning and legacy of Brown v. Board of Education, the 1954 decision that said the Constitution does not permit racial segregation in public schools.\par \par \uc2 The connection between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and education may seem elusive. But the justices cited Brown 23 times, using it to make points about precedent, about popular opinion and, most tellingly, about how to interpret the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., writing for the five-member majority, invoked Brown as an example of a decision that had properly overruled a precedent. Plessy v. Ferguson, the 1896 decision that said ''separate but equal'' facilities were constitutional, was plainly and egregiously wrong, he wrote, and so Brown had been right to overturn it.\par \par \uc2 The same was true, Justice Alito wrote, of Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that had guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that had reaffirmed Roe's core holding.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 ., in a concurring opinion that would have stopped short of overruling Roe, failed to see the parallel. ''The opinion in Brown,'' he wrote, ''was unanimous and 11 pages long; this one is neither.''\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the three dissenting justices wrote in a joint opinion, ''a bare majority'' of the current court had overruled the two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  precedents.\par \par \uc2 ''The majority has overruled Roe and Casey for one and only one reason: because it has always despised them, and now it has the votes to discard them,'' Justices \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan wrote.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito also cited Brown, which was deeply unpopular in the South, in support of a second point. ''We cannot allow our decisions to be affected,'' he wrote, ''by any extraneous influences such as concern about the public's reaction to our work.''\par \par \uc2 But the most intriguing mention of Brown was made almost in passing in the dissent. It said the court that had decided Brown might not have done so had it used ''the majority's method of constitutional construction.''\par \par \uc2 That method was originalism, which seeks to identify the original meaning of constitutional provisions using the tools of historians.\par \par \uc2 But Brown has always been problematic for originalists. The weight of the historical evidence is that the people who from 1866 to 1868 proposed and ratified the 14th Amendment, which guaranteed ''equal protection of the laws,'' did not understand themselves to be doing away with segregated schools.\par \par \uc2 Yet Brown is generally considered to be a moral triumph and the Supreme Court's finest hour. A theory of constitutional interpretation that cannot explain Brown is suspect, if not discredited.\par \par \uc2 Originalists hate talking about Brown. When Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , an enthusiastic originalist, used to be asked about the case, he was prone to say, ''Waving the bloody shirt of Brown again, eh?''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito's critique of Roe was certainly steeped in originalism. In ruling that there is no constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he focused on the words of the Constitution and ''how the states regulated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the 14th Amendment was adopted.''\par \par \uc2 His approach echoed contemporary criticism of Brown on originalist grounds.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito said that ''the Constitution makes no mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' A 1956 statement by Southern members of Congress who objected to Brown, which came to be known as the Southern Manifesto, made a similar point: ''The original Constitution does not mention education. Neither does the 14th Amendment nor any other amendment.''\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, Justice Alito wrote that ''by the time of the adoption of the 14th Amendment, three-quarters of the states had made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime at any stage of pregnancy, and the remaining states would soon follow.''\par \par \uc2 The Southern Manifesto again echoed the point.\par \par \uc2 ''When the amendment was adopted, in 1868, there were 37 states of the union,'' the manifesto said. ''Every one of the 26 states that had any substantial racial differences among its people either approved the operation of segregated schools already in existence or subsequently established such schools by action of the same lawmaking body which considered the 14th Amendment.''\par \par \uc2 The unanimous opinion in Brown did not really quarrel with the idea that it could not be justified using the tools of originalism. ''At best,'' the opinion said, the historical evidence was ''inconclusive.''\par \par \uc2 Before Justice Scalia died in 2016, he and Justice Breyer, who retired in June, would occasionally appear in public to debate constitutional interpretation. Justice Breyer liked to needle Justice Scalia about Brown.\par \par \uc2 ''Where would you be with school desegregation?'' Justice Breyer asked his colleague in 2009, at an appearance at the \uc2 University of Arizona\uc2 . ''It's certainly clear that at the time they passed the 14th Amendment, which says people should be treated equally, there was school segregation, and they didn't think they were ending it.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Scalia did not give a direct answer. In other settings, he endorsed the decision. ''Though Scalia says that he would have voted with the majority in Brown,'' Margaret Talbot of The New Yorker wrote in a 2005 profile, ''it's hard to see an originalist justification for it.''\par \par \uc2 The majority in the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, noted that both Plessy and Roe had survived about a half-century before being overturned.\par \par \uc2 The three dissenters responded that Plessy might still be on the books if the court in Brown had been committed to originalism.\par \par \uc2 ''If the Brown court had used the majority's method of constitutional construction,'' the dissenters wrote, ''it might not ever have overruled Plessy, whether five or 50 or 500 years later.''\par \par \uc2 George E.C. Hayes, left, Thurgood Marshall, center, and James M. Nabrit, all lawyers who worked on Brown v. Board of Education, celebrated the 1954 Supreme Court ruling that struck down school segregation.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220802ei820003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
