\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Doctor in Indiana Faces Risks as Voice For  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dr. Caitlin Bernard, who provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 10-year-old, says doctors shouldn't be silent. But she finds herself at the center of a post-Roe clash shaking the medical community.\par \par \uc2 Three weeks before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Dr. Caitlin Bernard, an Indianapolis obstetrician-gynecologist, donned her white lab coat, put her infant daughter into a front-pack baby carrier and joined a few colleagues who marched to the State Capitol, hoping to deliver a letter to Gov. \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Signed by hundreds of health professionals, the letter implored Mr. Holcomb, a Republican, not to convene a special legislative session to further restrict abortions. It contained a pointed political message: ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are not popular in our state.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard, who catapulted into the national spotlight for providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 10-year-old rape victim last month, delivers babies and provides contraceptive care, pap smears and other routine obstetric and gynecological care. She is also one of a small number of doctors in her state with specific training in complex reproductive care, including second-trimester abortions.\par \par \uc2 But some of her riskiest work takes place outside her hospital, advocating publicly for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Her outspokenness has extracted a price. Dr. Bernard, 37, has been criticized across right-wing media, faced harassment and is the subject of an investigation by the Indiana attorney general. She's landed at the center of a post-Roe clash that the medical community has been dreading -- one in which doctors themselves are the focus of political and legal attacks.\par \par \uc2 ''Physicians who provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been harassed, they have been murdered,'' Dr. Bernard said on Tuesday in an interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . ''And for too long, I think, because of that, they've had to be silent to protect their families, and it's created an idea that we're doing something wrong or something illegal. And we're not. And I feel compelled to say that.''\par \par \uc2 Threats against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are hardly new. But the overturning of Roe has created a frightening new legal landscape for doctors.\par \par \uc2 In conservative states like Indiana, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is restricted, they face mounting legal requirements that, if not followed, could jeopardize their licenses or land them in jail. A wave of new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions across the nation call for hefty prison terms and fines against doctors who provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 In Indiana, Todd Rokita, the attorney general, is investigating whether Dr. Bernard, an assistant professor of clinical obstetrics & gynecology at the Indiana University School of Medicine, reported the Ohio girl's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Indiana state officials, as required. Records show she did.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to The Times on Tuesday, the attorney general said he would ''see this duty through to the very end,'' and accused Dr. Bernard of using ''a 10-year-old rape victim's personal trauma'' to ''push her ideological stance.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard, in turn, says Mr. Rokita is just another politician engaging in ''state intimidation for their own political ends.'' She has filed a tort claim against him, the first step toward a potential lawsuit for defamation.\par \par \uc2 Medical professionals who work in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  are watching the events in Indiana closely, said Dr. Kristin Lyerly, an obstetrician-gynecologist in Wisconsin who coordinates \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care advocacy in the upper Midwest for the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 . Before Roe was overturned, she said, she was providing abortions at one of four clinics in Wisconsin. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned there under an 1849 law that makes it a criminal offense.\par \par \uc2 ''Those of us who provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care have been trying to do it discreetly and carefully for many years knowing that this is necessary health care for our patients,'' Dr. Lyerly said. ''Now, we feel like we really need to tell the story and be very frank about what we're seeing and experiencing and what our patients are dealing with, while walking that very fine line of protecting patient privacy.''\par \par \uc2 Abortions are only a small part of Dr. Bernard's practice. She handles complex \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases -- those where the life of the mother is in danger -- at the university's medical center. She provides abortions -- both surgical and medication -- several days a month at Planned Parenthood clinics in Indiana and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 The work has long included stressful elements that go well beyond delivering sensitive medical care: In 2020, she said, the F.B.I. informed Planned Parenthood it was investigating a kidnapping threat against her daughter.\par \par \uc2 Her patients describe her as kind and caring; Rebecca Evans, a nurse midwife who sought care from Dr. Bernard after she suffered a miscarriage, called Dr. Bernard a ''full scope'' clinician, who ''does all of these different things, and she's really passionate about all of it.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard's advocacy, she says, is in furtherance of her goal of providing patients the best medical care possible. By limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  options, and requiring her to make certain statements -- such as informing patients that fetuses feel pain during an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the science on that issue is still unclear -- the state is forcing her to practice medicine in a way that is unsafe and not medically accurate, she says.\par \par \uc2 She is the plaintiff in a 2019 lawsuit filed by \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  that sought unsuccessfully to overturn Indiana's ban on nearly all second-trimester abortions. She testifies frequently in the State Legislature. After Roe was overturned, she organized a protest. (She also sports a tattoo on her left foot, showing a wire coat hanger -- a symbol of a dangerous at-home abortions before the procedure was legal -- over the words ''Trust women.'')\par \par \uc2 Indiana currently allows abortions up to 22 weeks. This week, as the Indiana legislature considered a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the legislative session she fought against, Dr. Bernard was not there.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have been leaving hateful messages on her cellphone, she says. She continues to see patients, but has hired a security detail, and her colleagues have started a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  account to help with her mounting legal bills. An in-person appearance in a tense environment at the Legislature might further inflame the situation.\par \par \uc2 ''The politicization of me, and of the work that I do, has definitely made it difficult for me to continue to do the advocacy that I have in the past,'' she conceded.\par \par \uc2 Not long after Roe was overturned, the Indianapolis Star learned of her 10-year-old patient, who had traveled from Ohio, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned after six weeks, even in cases of rape or incest. Dr. Bernard's allies say it is no accident that the 10-year-old child was referred to her; there are very few doctors, they say, who could handle such a sensitive case.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, President Biden cited the case when he signed an executive order designed to ensure access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication. Suddenly, all eyes were on Dr. Bernard.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard on Tuesday refused to discuss any aspect of the case, citing the girl's privacy. In addition to worrying about prosecution, she could face consequences at work. Until Tuesday, her employer, Indiana University School of Medicine, a state-funded institution, and \uc2 Indiana University Health\uc2 , a nonprofit health care system, had been publicly silent about her, except to say she had not violated patient privacy laws.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to The Times, \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 's president, \uc2 Pam Whitten\uc2 , and medical school dean, Dr. Jay Hess, said Dr. Bernard remains ''a member of the faculty in good standing.'' I.U. Health called her a ''valued and respected physician'' and a ''true advocate for the health and well-being of her patients.''\par \par \uc2 In a sense, Dr. Bernard's life has prepared her for this moment. She absorbed her activist streak from her parents, who came of age in the socially liberal 1960s, and lived on a communal farm in upstate New York when their children were little.\par \par \uc2 When she was five, she informed her family that she was going to be a doctor, said her sister, Rebeccah Johnson. When she was 15, she and her sister walked past a phalanx of protesters at a Planned Parenthood clinic to get birth control. Later, she witnessed firsthand the complications women can suffer from pregnancy when she and her father, a carpenter, went to Guatemala to help run health clinics.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps because of that, she said, she was always drawn to obstetrics and gynecology. Early in her career, Dr. Bernard joined a program called AMPATH, led by \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 , which brings American doctors to Kenya, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is largely prohibited.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a third of the patients she saw were suffering from complications from unsafe at-home abortions. ''We'd often see women who had been raped, assaulted, and now pregnant,'' said Dr. Astrid Christoffersen-Deb, her supervisor.\par \par \uc2 After completing medical school and residency at Upstate Medical University in Syracuse, N.Y., Dr. Bernard trained at \uc2 Washington University in St. Louis\uc2 , where she became accredited in ''complex family planning,'' a specialty that qualifies her to handle complicated cases including second-trimester abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''People who need abortions in the second trimester are often facing the absolute worst imaginable situations -- they have a very wanted pregnancy and their baby is not going to survive or is going to have an incredibly difficult life and they are trying to spare their child from that outcome,'' she said, adding, ''Politicians, people who are uncomfortable with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, have usually never been in those situations.''\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Dr. Bernard left St. Louis for Indiana, where she has become the ''go-to'' doctor to speak out for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Dr. Tracey A. Wilkinson, a pediatrician who, along with Dr. Bernard, is involved with Indiana's chapter of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Advocacy Project. Dr. Wilkinson spent all day Monday at the Indiana Capitol, and said she felt Dr. Bernard's absence acutely.\par \par \uc2 ''We do not go knowing that we are going to change the way the votes happen,'' Dr. Wilkinson said. ''We go to put in the record that somebody stood up and said that this was wrong. We go so that our patients hear somebody standing up for them.''\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Indiana's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban advanced out of a Senate committee, drawing critics from across the political spectrum. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates called the measure an attack on women, while several anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists criticized exceptions that would allow for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape and incest; one suggested that Dr. Bernard's 10-year-old patient should have been required to give birth.\par \par \uc2 If the bill passes, Dr. Bernard said, she will likely find herself referring Indiana women to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers out of state. Though she knows it may create further problems for her, she does not intend to be quiet.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the most important things about the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the U.S. is that people don't want to talk about it,'' she said. ''They fear the stigma, providers fear the stigma that they're going to be harassed, targeted, because they have been. So one of the most important things is just being honest about it.''\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith contributed to this story.\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith contributed to this story.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Caitlin Bernard says her \uc2  rights advocacy is part of providing patients with the best care possible. She worked in Kenya, far left, in 2017 and attended an \uc2  rights rally with Dr. Tracey A. Wilkinson at the Indiana Statehouse last month. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY AMANDA ANDRADE-RHOADES FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; DR. CAITLIN BERNARD; DR. TRACEY A. WILKINSON); Indiana state legislators, left, at a session on \uc2  on Monday. Todd Rokita, above, Indiana's attorney general, is investigating whether Dr. Bernard reported a girl's \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KAITI SULLIVAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; OLIVER CONTRERAS/SIPA, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220729ei7t00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
