\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Where  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Are on the Ballot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Schoenfeld Walker, Jason Kao and Marco Hernandez \par \uc2 1,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Now that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  has returned the power to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states, every election has implications for access. The 2022 midterms will bring the issue's first big electoral test since the court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Five states have put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights directly on the ballot with measures to amend their constitutions: California, Michigan and Vermont will ask voters whether to affirm the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Kansas and Kentucky will ask voters whether to reject it.\par \par \uc2 In other states, the outcomes of competitive races for the legislature and the governor's mansion in November could pave the way either for protections or for restrictions and outright bans. Races further down the ballot matter, too, giving Americans more opportunities to vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some with more lasting effects.\par \par \uc2 In most states, the position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is clear, and the midterms are unlikely to affect the status quo. But in at least seven states, highly competitive races within one or more branches of government could tip the balance in support of either access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or further restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Advocates and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are strategizing for wins up and down the ballot. And opponents and conservative groups in particular have been investing heavily in lesser-known races. Here's a guide to the elections they see as most critical.\par \par \uc2 Arizona\par \par \uc2 With Republican control of the State Legislature and the governor's office, Arizona has enacted a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks of pregnancy, set to take effect this fall. The attorney general wants an older ban on nearly all abortions to go into effect. The State Supreme Court has not yet ruled on whether the state's Constitution protects the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates see opportunities to flip control of the State Senate or State House, in which Republicans have just a two-seat margin in each chamber. And they are backing Democrats for governor and attorney general. Voters could reject up to three judges who are up for re-election on the court but could not change its Republican control.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are aiming to maintain the State Legislature's Republican majority and to fill open seats for governor and attorney general with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates.\par \par \uc2 Georgia\par \par \uc2 Georgia Republicans have enacted a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six weeks of pregnancy. Competitive races for governor and attorney general could challenge lawmakers' plans to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are endorsing \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and Jen Jordan, both Democrats who staunchly oppose the state's ban, for governor and attorney general. They are also hoping to flip several legislative seats currently held by Republicans, though the midterms are unlikely to change partisan control of either chamber.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are rallying voters to maintain the status quo. The State Supreme Court, which has a majority of judges appointed by a Republican governor, held elections in May.\par \par \uc2 Kansas\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court ruled in 2019 that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was protected in the State Constitution. That has made it hard for Republicans in the Legislature to enact restrictions, even though they have a large enough majority to override a veto from Gov. Laura Kelly, a Democrat. If voters approve an Aug. 2 ballot measure denying a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would pave the way for bans.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are aiming to defeat the ballot measure and protect two judges who supported the 2019 ruling. Flipping three seats in the State House and State Senate could remove the Republican supermajority.\par \par \uc2 Passing the ballot measure, which would deny the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and could be undone only by passing another ballot measure, is a top priority for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. Voters can choose to reject six of seven judges on the Democrat-led State Supreme Court who are up for re-election.\par \par \uc2 Michigan\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, has blocked the Republican-controlled Legislature's efforts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her re-election effort and several races in the State House and State Senate are some of the most competitive in the country. A pending ballot measure would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with a constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 Passing the ballot measure is critical for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates, who also see opportunities to overturn Republican control of the Legislature by flipping three seats in the House or four in the Senate.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are seeking to defeat the ballot measure and flip the Democratic-majority court, as well as the offices of governor and attorney general.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat, has been a firewall against Republican efforts in the General Assembly to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The governor is not up for election this cycle, but Republicans would need to pick up just a few legislative seats to win a veto-proof majority. The State Supreme Court could lose its Democratic majority with the flip of a single seat.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are rallying voters to maintain the Democratic majority on the court and to keep Democratic seats in the General Assembly to prevent a veto-proof majority for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  see an opportunity to flip the court's Democratic majority and to give Republican legislators the power to override a governor's veto by winning three seats in the House and two seats in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania\par \par \uc2 The state's Democratic governor, \uc2 Tom Wolf\uc2 , has vetoed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions proposed by the General Assembly, but he is not running again. The governor's race is competitive, and a win for the Republican candidate, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , would most likely lead to bans from a Legislature that is likely to keep its Republican majority.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are backing \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , a Democratic candidate for governor who would be a firewall against restrictions. Advocates also see a chance to flip a chamber in the General Assembly for Democrats, though voters would need to overturn five Republican seats in the Senate and 12 in the House.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are rallying voters to strengthen the Republican majority in the General Assembly and elect the Republican candidate for governor.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  and Attorney General \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 , both Democrats, have sued to block a ban on nearly all abortions. They stand in opposition to the Republican-controlled State Legislature, which is a few seats away from a veto-proof majority.\par \par \uc2 Priorities for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates include preventing Republicans from securing a veto-proof majority in the State Legislature and re-electing the governor and the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 For the governor and attorney general races, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are rallying voters to elect Republicans who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Winning at least five more State Assembly seats and one more State Senate seat for Republicans would give the chambers the power to override a veto.\par \par \uc2 Additional work by Christine Zhang and Guilbert Gates. Note: The ballot measure in Michigan is awaiting certification. Sources: Ballotpedia; Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; David S. Cohen, \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 ; Douglas Keith, Brennan Center for Justice; Rebecca J. Kreitzer, \uc2 University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill\uc2 ; Paul Nolette, \uc2 Marquette University\uc2 ; National Conference of State Legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220722ei7m0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
