\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Some Colleges Resist Students' Push for  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anemona Hartocollis and Stephanie Saul \par \uc2 1,694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even in states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, colleges are proceeding with caution.\par \par \uc2 Many colleges pride themselves on providing a range of sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services for their students, including birth control, screenings for sexually transmitted diseases and even insurance coverage to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 What they do not tend to provide is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , either by pill or through a procedure. For that, they refer students to other clinics or doctors' offices.\par \par \uc2 For some students, that standard of care is no longer good enough, not with the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. They want colleges to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reduce the stress and obstacles, and give women more autonomy and control over the process.\par \par \uc2 The biggest thing on many of their wish lists: medication to end an unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Niharika Rao, a senior at Barnard College in New York who uses they pronouns, said students had demanded that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be made available on campus: ''College student access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being stigmatized and politicized,'' they said.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  involves taking two types of pills, federally approved for use within the first 10 weeks of pregnancy. It is different from the morning-after pill, which is more widely available at student health centers and can be taken to block a pregnancy before it begins.\par \par \uc2 A few colleges, including the University of Illinois Chicago, already provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. The \uc2 University of Massachusetts Amherst\uc2  plans to begin offering it in the fall. In California, a new law requires all of the state's public universities to provide medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on campus by January; some campuses, like in Berkeley, have already begun to do so.\par \par \uc2 But because of the fluctuating landscape of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, and because of the complicated politics, persuading more colleges to provide medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be difficult.\par \par \uc2 Colleges in states with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, like Missouri, may not be able to legally offer it. Other universities, especially publicly funded ones in states where laws are in flux, do not want to end up on the wrong side of legislation.\par \par \uc2 Even in states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, colleges are proceeding with caution. Providing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill on campus rather than referring students elsewhere enmeshes colleges in a personal, sometimes emotionally and physically taxing medical decision, according to administrators at several colleges who asked not to be named because of the sensitivity of the issue.\par \par \uc2 Administrators say that colleges must be prepared for students who will feel stressed, as well as those who will feel relieved by the procedure. They must have the ability to provide emergency medical care. And they must consider that protesters may show up at the college health center door, and be prepared to offer security and privacy to the women going inside.\par \par \uc2 ''There are some valid security concerns,'' Dr. Guy W. Nicolette, assistant vice chancellor for \uc2 University Health Services\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , said. In addition, he said, ''not every provider feels competent or comfortable doing this.''\par \par \uc2 But Dr. Nicolette said that on balance, Berkeley's health services believed that offering medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on campus, with counseling, was the sensitive approach. ''Because of our connection to the campus, we try to be the trusted source for health care for students, recognizing that often this is their first time away from home, first time accessing health care away from home,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Cost is also an issue. In California, the new law does not pay for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, and students have to pay through insurance or in cash. Community colleges are exempt from the requirement to provide medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though a 2021 study estimated that those students sought 865 to 1,109 medication abortions per month. Women who have a child while in college are less likely to graduate than those who do not, according to U.S. Education Department data.\par \par \uc2 Women in their 20s account for more than half of abortions, or about 57 percent, according to 2019 data from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some 300 to 500 public university students in California sought medication abortions each month, according to a 2018 study by University of California researchers published in The Journal of Adolescent Health. As many as 62 percent of students in the system were more than a half-hour by public transportation from the closest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. The average cost of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was $604, and the average wait time for the first appointment was one week, which could tip the pregnancy past the 10-week approved window in which the medication can be prescribed.\par \par \uc2 ''This happens a lot -- folks are pushed into another trimester waiting for a referral from the student health center,'' said Cindy Cruz, the Western states program director for \uc2 Unite\uc2  for Reproductive and Gender Equity, an advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 Students who have to travel to a distant \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic may also miss out on classes and work, said Z Zenobia, a graduate student at California State Polytechnic University, Humboldt, a campus in a rural area where access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services is limited.\par \par \uc2 ''Having to travel an hour there and an hour back on the bus, that could be missing an entire lab,'' said Mx. Zenobia, who has also lobbied for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to be more sensitive to transgender students.\par \par \uc2 At Barnard College and Columbia University, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are not at risk, student activists who demanded medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the university's health service were unhappy with the institutional response.\par \par \uc2 Columbia provided them a standard university handout that said: ''Private physicians' offices are usually less crowded, have shorter waiting time, afford more privacy and feel more personal. Clinics or nonprofits such as Planned Parenthood may allow greater anonymity.''\par \par \uc2 Mx. Rao, the Barnard student, said students seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  often go to Planned Parenthood clinics in Lower Manhattan or the Bronx, which are targets of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters who include some fellow students.\par \par \uc2 ''I would argue it is more anonymous to go to your campus clinic,'' Mx. Rao said.\par \par \uc2 Mx. Rao said a meeting with a Barnard health administrator last week had left them feeling a little more optimistic.\par \par \uc2 Barnard said through a spokesperson that it was committed to working with students and local organizations to ''bolster'' \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, including pregnancy termination, and ''to determine what that looks like in a post-Roe environment.''\par \par \uc2 Universities are also getting pushback from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents.\par \par \uc2 Carol Tobias, president of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, said that offering medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on campus created the potential for complications, like in the case of an undiagnosed ectopic pregnancy. ''I just don't think this should be handed out like aspirin on college campuses to young girls who may not be aware of what's happening,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Grace Lake, a senior and nursing major at \uc2 East Carolina University in Greenville, N.C\uc2 ., said the overturning of Roe had empowered anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists on campus. She is secretary of a recently formed group, Students for Life, which is lobbying the university's chancellor for day care services for students who have children.\par \par \uc2 ''From my point of view, there is no reason at all why there should be any available access for students to be able to end the life of their child, especially if it's through the school,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws could become more strict, students are trying to find alternatives. At \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2 , some students worry that their access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state is tenuous because of a 1931 law, currently tied up in court, that criminalizes almost any type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They were not reassured when \uc2 Samuel L. Stanley Jr\uc2 ., the university's president, said in a statement that \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care was a ''basic human right'' while also acknowledging a ''diversity of opinions'' on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''I was like, that's just not enough,'' said Lana Heaney, a rising junior who organized a rally.\par \par \uc2 For now, Ms. Heaney said, women are working on a ''backup plan'' -- a push to distribute contraceptives and Plan B on campus in case the law goes into effect.\par \par \uc2 Activists are also hoping that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doulas can support women taking medication for abortions. This year, Advocates for Youth, a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, helped train 100 people -- three-quarters of them current or recent college students -- to be \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doulas. These doulas will provide information and emotional support to those going through an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The practical support is really increasing -- the rides, the shelter, the funds and all of the different logistical pieces that are needed to make access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care possible,'' said Tamara Marzouk, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access at Advocates for Youth.\par \par \uc2 Caitlyn Bull, who will be a senior at \uc2 Oberlin College\uc2  in the fall, has been part of the Oberlin Doula Collective, which is not formally affiliated with the school, since her freshman year. ''It has been very cool to see the campus community come together around this work,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The college, she said, had sent out messages that supported \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ''but we do live in a hostile state.''\par \par \uc2 In the more volatile landscape after Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates also need to be careful -- just as careful as universities. They are being trained in how to ''share'' information about self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- not to train people in how to do it -- because ''sharing'' is legal, Ms. Marzouk said.\par \par \uc2 ''When we veer into potentially giving medical advice or saying, 'You should do this or do that,' that could be construed as medical or legal advice,'' she said, ''and that veers into a risky area.''\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Niharika Rao, a Barnard senior and \uc2  advocate, participating in a panel discussion, above. At the University of California, Berkeley, top, medication \uc2  is offered on campus. ''Because of our connection to the campus, we try to be the trusted source for health care for students,'' said Dr. Guy W. Nicolette, assistant vice chancellor for University Health Services. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY BEN MARGOT/ASSOCIATED PRESS; SARAH BLESENER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220720ei7k0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
