\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Why Is the Right Forcing Women Who Miscarry to Suffer?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's getting hard to keep track of all the stories of women being denied care for miscarriages and otherwise having their lives endangered because of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported on a woman who had to travel to Michigan after a doctor in her home state refused to end an ectopic pregnancy because of the presence of fetal cardiac activity. (Ectopic pregnancies, in which an embryo implants outside the uterus, never lead to a live birth and are the leading cause of first-trimester maternal death.)\par \par \uc2 In an interview with The Associated Press, a doctor described a patient who was miscarrying in Texas and had developed a uterine infection. She couldn't get the necessary treatment -- an immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- as long as the fetus displayed signs of life. ''The patient developed complications, required surgery, lost multiple liters of blood and had to be put on a breathing machine,'' The A.P. reported, all because, as the doctor said, ''we were essentially 24 hours behind.''\par \par \uc2 A doctor in Wisconsin, Carley Zeal, told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  about caring for a woman having a miscarriage who had been denied treatment at a hospital. By the time she found Dr. Zeal, The Times reported, ''the woman had been bleeding intermittently for days,'' which the doctor said put her at ''increased risk of hemorrhage or infection.''\par \par \uc2 Some in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement insist that the doctors refusing to treat these women are mistaken about what the laws in their states say. ''To the extent that doctors or attorneys are confused about whether necessary women's health care is forbidden under pro-life laws, the fault lies in large part with pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, who have been intentionally muddying the waters,'' tweeted Alexandra DeSanctis Marr, a writer for National Review and the co-author of ''Tearing Us Apart: How \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Harms Everything and Solves Nothing.''\par \par \uc2 If that was the case, one might think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents would be eager to see their laws clarified. After all, the suffering caused by mismanaged miscarriages doesn't serve the cause of fetal life. Ultimately, it will likely be detrimental to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. In Ireland, it was the death of Savita Halappanavar, who developed septicemia after being refused a termination while she was miscarrying, that spurred the successful campaign for legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there. Preventing such deaths should be as urgent a priority for those opposed to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as for those who champion it.\par \par \uc2 But it isn't. Last week, the Biden administration released guidance that under federal law, hospitals must provide abortions when they're necessary to stabilize patients suffering medical emergencies, or transfer them to a hospital that will. Texas is suing to prevent that policy from going into effect, saying it would ''transform every emergency room in the country into a walk-in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.''\par \par \uc2 Idaho's Republican Party recently changed its platform to call for the criminalization of all abortions without exception. According to a blog post by Idaho Reports, a public policy television program, some delegates shared concerns about ectopic pregnancies and proposed an exemption in the platform when a woman's life is in ''lethal danger.'' The exemption proposal was voted down, 412-164.\par \par \uc2 In The Times, the president of Texas Right to Life, John Seago, acknowledged that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans could delay intervention during miscarriages. Doctors, he said, cannot decide that ''I want to cause the death of the child today because I believe that they're going to pass away eventually.''\par \par \uc2 I thought I was sufficiently cynical about the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, but I admit to being taken aback by this blithe, public disregard for the lives of women, including women suffering the loss of wanted pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 I suspect that part of what's happening is the right following its own rhetoric to its logical conclusion. It's common for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to claim that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is never medically necessary. Among conservative elites, this argument relies on semantic trickery, defining the termination of pregnancy to save a woman's life as something other than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hence when the president of \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  testified before Congress, she argued, about the high-profile case of the 10-year-old rape victim, ''If a 10-year-old became pregnant as a result of rape and it was threatening her life, then that's not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 This stance allows some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to avoid reckoning with the consequences of the laws they support. Others, however, see those consequences fully and are fine with them. Scott Herndon, an Idaho Republican who recently unseated an incumbent state senator, was the politician who proposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  criminalization language in his party's platform. A website he runs, Abolish \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Idaho, says, of legislation he pushes, ''Doctors may not intentionally kill the child in their medical attempts to treat the mother.''\par \par \uc2 On the day Roe v. Wade was overturned, Herndon posted a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  video arguing for murder prosecutions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. ''This body inside the mother's body is not her body, and we need to get over the lie that mothers are not accountable,'' he said. Men like him are making laws now. Don't expect mercy.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Christopher Lee for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220719ei7j00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
