\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Returning After 22 Years, Jackson Enters a Maelstrom\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,751 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the 22 years since she worked for Justice \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , whom she replaced last month, the court has become polarized and predictably conservative.\par \par \uc2 Less than two hours after the Supreme Court ended a wrenching term last month, it welcomed a new member. Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , who had been waiting in the wings since she was confirmed by the Senate in April, took two oaths of office -- and joined a court in turmoil.\par \par \uc2 ''She's entering the court at a time of just crazy polarization after a very momentous term and after this huge leak from the spring,'' said David Lat, a legal commentator, referring to the disclosure in May of a draft opinion overturning Roe v. Wade, which closely resembled the decision last month that did away with the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I'm sure her colleagues will be very welcoming to her, but there may just be a lot more circumspection around the building,'' said Mr. Lat, the author of Original Jurisdiction, a newsletter about the law and legal profession. ''It could be a little weird.''\par \par \uc2 In joining the court, Justice Jackson returned to a familiar setting. She had served as a law clerk to Justice \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , whom she replaced, in the term that ended in 2000. But that was a very different time -- and the differences illuminate both the extraordinary transformation of the institution and the challenges its newest member will face.\par \par \uc2 In an end-of-term overview that July, The New York Times's Supreme Court reporter, Linda Greenhouse, asked \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 ., then a prominent lawyer, for his assessment of the court's major decisions.\par \par \uc2 ''Which cases were most visible to the public this year?'' asked Mr. Roberts, who would become chief justice five years later. ''Probably school prayer, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Miranda, and the conservatives lost all three.''\par \par \uc2 The term that ended last month also featured cases on school prayer, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Miranda. This time around, though, the conservatives won all three.\par \par \uc2 The court in 2000 was about halfway through an 11-year stretch without any changes in personnel, the second-longest such period in its history.\par \par \uc2 It was by most accounts a happy place. That too has changed.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not the court of that era,'' Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said at a conference in Dallas two weeks after the leak, adding: ''We actually trusted each other. We may have been a dysfunctional family, but we were a family.''\par \par \uc2 Since Chief Justice Roberts's arrival in 2005, there have been seven new justices. The only current member of the court who was serving in 2000, when Justice Jackson was a law clerk, is Justice Thomas.\par \par \uc2 After she was sworn in last month, Justice Jackson alluded to the fact that her appointment as the first Black woman to serve on the court was a milestone. ''I am truly grateful,'' she said in a statement, ''to be part of the promise of our great nation.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Breyer, in his own statement, said his successor would fit in well at the court. ''I am glad for my fellow justices,'' he said. ''They gain a colleague who is empathetic, thoughtful and collegial.''\par \par \uc2 Those qualities, coupled with her familiarity with the inner workings of the court, may serve Justice Jackson well. But the court's six-member conservative majority seems poised to assertively push the law to the right, and its most junior member will have little ability to slow, much less to thwart, its ambitions.\par \par \uc2 It may, in any event, take some time for Justice Jackson, who spent most of her judicial career as a trial judge, to feel comfortable with the court's work.\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas, who joined the court in 1991, said he had asked his new colleagues how long it would take to feel comfortable. ''To a person, they said it took three to five years under normal circumstances to adjust to the court,'' Justice Thomas said in 1996.\par \par \uc2 That is the usual estimate. ''So extraordinary an intellect as Brandeis said it took him four or five years to feel that he understood the jurisprudential problems of the court,'' Justice Felix Frankfurter wrote of Justice Louis D. Brandeis, who sat on the court from 1916 to 1939.\par \par \uc2 But Justice Jackson's experience as a law clerk may speed her acclimation. Six of the 10 justices who were once Supreme Court clerks sit on the current court: Chief Justice Roberts and Justices Jackson, Elena Kagan, \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, the court Justice Jackson knew in 2000 was a different place, even though then as now, it was dominated by Republican appointees. Indeed, it was in that sense even more lopsided, with seven justices named by Republican presidents rather than the current six.\par \par \uc2 But the justices' partisan affiliations in those days did not reliably predict their votes.\par \par \uc2 Two of the members of the court appointed by Republicans -- Justices \uc2 John Paul Stevens\uc2  and David H. Souter -- were liberals. Another two -- Justices \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  and \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2  -- were moderates who leaned right. Only the remaining three -- Chief Justice William H. Rehnquist and Justices Thomas and \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  -- were committed conservatives.\par \par \uc2 That meant the court's two Democratic appointees -- Justices Breyer and \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  -- were very much in the mix on a court that could be unpredictable. Justice Jackson, though she will be part of a three-member liberal wing, will have less room to maneuver.\par \par \uc2 These days, partisan affiliations are closely aligned with voting patterns in major cases. In decisions issued last month on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns, religion and climate change, all of the six Republican appointees voted with the majority and all of the three Democratic ones were in dissent.\par \par \uc2 Consider the contrasts between the decisions Mr. Roberts noted in 2000 and their 2022 counterparts.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, the court applied principles announced in Roe v. Wade to strike down a Nebraska law that banned a late-term procedure that opponents call partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Justice Breyer wrote the majority opinion in the 5-to-4 decision.\par \par \uc2 Just seven years later, after Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . replaced Justice O'Connor, the court reversed course in another 5-to-4 decision, now sustaining the federal Partial-Birth \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ban Act. After further changes in the court's personnel, including the addition of three justices appointed by President Donald J. Trump, the court last month overruled Roe entirely.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, the court was wary of prayer in public schools, ruling that organized prayers led by students at high school football games violated the First Amendment's prohibition of government establishment of religion.\par \par \uc2 ''The delivery of a pregame prayer has the improper effect of coercing those present to participate in an act of religious worship,'' Justice Stevens wrote for the majority in a 6-to-3 decision. Four Republican appointees voted with the majority.\par \par \uc2 In June, in a 6-to-3 decision divided along partisan lines, the court ruled that a football coach at a public high school had a constitutional right to pray at the 50-yard line, discounting the possibility of coercion.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most surprising decision of the 2000 term was the 7-to-2 ruling reaffirming Miranda v. Arizona, the 1966 decision that required police officers to issue the familiar warning before interrogations. The decision had been widely criticized, and Congress tried to overturn it.\par \par \uc2 But Chief Justice Rehnquist, himself a longtime critic of Miranda, wrote for the majority that the warnings had ''become embedded in routine police practice.'' Since the Miranda decision had ''announced a constitutional rule,'' he wrote, a statute that sought to overrule it was itself unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 In June, in a 6-to-3 decision that divided along the familiar lines, the court ruled that police officers may not be sued under a federal civil rights law for failing to administer the warnings. ''A violation of Miranda does not necessarily constitute a violation of the Constitution,'' Justice Alito wrote for the majority.\par \par \uc2 Justice Jackson may have hoped that she would have the summer to settle in and prepare for the major cases next term, which starts in October. But there will be activity on what critics call the court's shadow docket before then.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the court is considering an emergency application from the Biden administration to undo a trial judge's ruling blocking the administration's approach to immigration enforcement. She will probably cast her first vote in that case.\par \par \uc2 Important cases await Justice Jackson in the next term. In October alone, the court will hear arguments on the role race may play in redistricting under the Voting Rights Act, on the scope of the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 's authority to enforce the Clean Water Act and on whether Andy Warhol committed copyright infringement by relying on a photographer's image of the musician Prince.\par \par \uc2 Later in the term, the court will hear arguments on whether state legislatures should have an outsized role in setting the rules for federal elections and on whether a web designer may refuse to provide wedding-related services to same-sex couples.\par \par \uc2 The court will also hear challenges to race-conscious admissions programs at Harvard and the University of North Carolina. Justice Jackson, who served on one of Harvard's governing bodies, has said she will recuse herself from the Harvard case. The two cases have been consolidated, but the court is likely to decouple them to allow Justice Jackson to hear the one against U.N.C.\par \par \uc2 Justice Jackson's judicial record gives few hints about how she will rule in any of the coming cases, though it will be a surprise if she strays far from the approach of Justice Breyer, her mentor, or the two liberal members of the current court, Justices Sotomayor and Kagan.\par \par \uc2 Her arrival, though, may nonetheless be a welcome reset. As Justice Byron R. White liked to say, every time there is a new justice, ''it's a different court.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson is one of six current Supreme Court members who are former law clerks for the court. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES); A crowd in April, left, celebrated the confirmation of Justice Jackson, the first Black woman on the Supreme Court. Anti-\uc2  advocates prayed in front of the court last month. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES; SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220718ei7i00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
