\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Pro-Life and Pro-Choice Americans on Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Kelley, Vanessa Mobley and Adrian J. Rivera \par \uc2 3,762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 THE Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last month. In the weeks since, the debate around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only intensified, with old questions taking on new life and new questions arising every day. For how much longer will it remain legal to travel out of state to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? In Texas -- where, last year, the state Legislature passed a trigger law that made performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony, exceptions only allowed to save the life of the pregnant patient or if there's a risk of 'substantial impairment of major bodily function -- does a pregnant driver get to use the high-occupancy vehicle lane? We at Times Opinion wanted to learn how everyday Americans were thinking about questions like these, so we decided to run two focus groups: one with self-described pro-life voters, and one with self-described pro-choice voters. In two separate discussions held on the same night, we asked participants in both groups the same questions.\par \par \uc2  As expected, we often heard very different answers. But across both groups, few participants were familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, which accounted for more than half of recent abortions performed in the United States and which many women, even in conservative states, are expected to use post-Roe. In the pro-life group, some were clearly moved by the claims of lawmakers who have worked to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity can be detected, starting around six weeks of pregnancy. Most medical experts say there is no real heartbeat at six weeks, and many women don't know they're pregnant at that stage. And in the pro-choice group, most vastly overestimated the major complication rate for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (less than a quarter of 1 percent). In an opinion for the majority, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  wrote that the decisions in Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey had ''enflamed debate and deepened division.'' It's clear that the court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization has done nothing to settle the debate around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In fact, it has perhaps only highlighted the promise and perils of our constitutional system. As two respondents in the pro-choice group noted, '' I really think, honestly, with a lot of things, it would help if [the law] was uniform across the United States. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gun laws, marriage, marijuana -- why are all these states different?'' ''I think because we're so different. That's why I think it would be cool if everyone went to a state that aligned with their beliefs. We're never all going to agree on any of those things.''\par \par \uc2 How would you describe your position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group:\par \par \uc2 Adrienne: I believe people should do what they want to do.\par \par \uc2 Bridget: I'm conflicted. Even though I don't think I could get one, I don't know if I want to impose that on everyone else. I do think it's murder. If you're going to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , own it. You are murdering a baby. But I still don't think it should be outlawed.\par \par \uc2 Anne: I think it's more complicated than just the right to life. I think that Roe protected women's bodily autonomy. So I think it's more about controlling and subjugating women than about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself, and that's just what I think. Personally, I would never have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I just couldn't do it, but I don't think it's about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I think it's about a whole other thing in that the political sides are just using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an excuse.\par \par \uc2 Michael J: I'm firmly on the side of women's right to choose. I look at the situation, and the people that are making these decisions are usually men. And if you're not able to give birth and such, you shouldn't be making these decisions, not like this.\par \par \uc2 adrienne: What makes this issue important to me is I feel that it's an infringement on rights. I feel like a person's right to make whatever choices for themselves is being invalidated.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group:\par \par \uc2 anthony: I feel like the choice should be left up to women. It does take a toll on them, financially, physically, emotionally. So it's their choice.But I just don't believe in taking a life.\par \par \uc2 Eileen: I think that If you're going to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it should be done before six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Christina: I'm more pro-life unless it's through something you can't control, like incest or rape.I think sex is made for people that are responsible. And if you're responsible, then you should know the outcomes and face the consequences of what could happen.\par \par \uc2 Gwendolyn: I'm pro-life. But educate, educate, educate. I'm a mother of three and I wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point. But from my own research, at four weeks, there's a heartbeat. I couldn't do it. Who is going to speak up for that baby at five weeks, six weeks, seven weeks ? You're killing someone. In the case of incest or rape, you're going to find out you're pregnant before you get that baby to three weeks, four weeks. You can dissolve that clot of blood. But after four weeks, there's a heartbeat so I don't think that women should have that right. Where's the right for the kid?\par \par \uc2 Sue: After a certain period of time, people make conscious choices, and there are consequences to those choices. It shouldn't be taken out on the fetus. Within the first six weeks, those kinds of decisions can be made. But after that, they're a baby, and they deserve to be protected, too.\par \par \uc2 Gwendolyn: Four weeks. Not six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Sue: I get your four weeks, but I think a lot of people don't pay attention till after their menstrual cycle, and sometimes it takes them a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Which tends to be more physically dangerous to the woman, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or childbirth?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group: 6 say childbirth is more dangerous, 2 say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group: 6 say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more dangerous, 2 say childbirth is more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 What have you heard about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group:\par \par \uc2 Michael J.: I've heard of it.\par \par \uc2 Rildon: I've never heard of it.\par \par \uc2 Anne: I've heard of it, but I don't know anything about it.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Michael J., what's your knowledge of it? As far as you know, how many abortions do you think are done that way?\par \par \uc2 michaelj: I would probably say maybe about 30 percent because you get it in the mail.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group:\par \par \uc2 Gwendolyn: Not enough testing has been done to say that it's safe. And that it's just a way for people not to be responsible adults.\par \par \uc2 Eileen: I don't really know much about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication. I would say half of them are probably done with this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 Recently, the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade, which established a constitutional right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now it's up to the states to decide their own laws. What is the first word that comes up for you when you think about this ruling?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group:\par \par \uc2 Adrienne: Freedom.\par \par \uc2 Michael J: Betrayal.\par \par \uc2 Bridget: Chaos.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group:\par \par \uc2 Gwendolyn: Pray.\par \par \uc2 Christina: Challenging.\par \par \uc2 Eileen: Yeah, challenging, I would say.\par \par \uc2 Dwyarrn: Complicated.\par \par \uc2 Sue: Frustrating.\par \par \uc2 moderator2: Were you all surprised by the decision?\par \par \uc2 lindsey: Yeah. I remember waking up and seeing it on my phone, and I didn't really think that it was real. I guess I had heard in the news that it was like something that people were talking about. But it just seemed like something that could never really happen in the US -- a place that defines itself on freedom that would be taking away people's right to choose,seemingly so easily.\par \par \uc2 michaelj: Nobody here should have been surprised because it was leaked that this was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 moderator2: And what about the reaction to the decision?\par \par \uc2 michaelj: The reaction, it was typical, and the only thing that can happen now is the people that are out there protesting and everything like that -- they just basically have to get out there and vote. If Republicans take over both houses of Congress, things will get worse.\par \par \uc2 PROLIFE\par \par \uc2 eileen: I would have been more surprised if the document hadn't been leaked a leak two months ago. But otherwise, yeah. I am surprised. The people who are for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a lot more vocal than the people who aren't.\par \par \uc2 What's the impact of this ruling on your families, personally? And how about the impact on women overall?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group:\par \par \uc2 Anne: My niece has had two pregnancies that have been a severe health risk to her. She had pre-eclampsia, and she ended up having to have c-section quite early so the baby -- it was a threat to the baby's life as well. She can't get pregnant again. And fortunately, she lives in a state that, as of yet, is not going to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if the Supreme Court went this far, are they going to take it even further and mandate that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal? I don't know. It's very scary.\par \par \uc2 Bridget: Depending on how far this goes, our kids could be the generation of unwanted kids, and that's going to bring along a lot of consequences with it -- probably a lot more crime, probably a lot more mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 anne: The straight white men want to keep their majority position and their money and their power and not let anyone else have a say or opinion. They want to legislate things that should be left to God.\par \par \uc2 moderator2: So I'm curious who you all think is to blame for the decision that came down overturning Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 michaelj: As much as I hate to say anything bad about \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  or the Democrats, they actually had a chance to codify this when he had a supermajority while he was president. But because they did not think it was something that needed to be done, it didn't get done\par \par \uc2 bridget: I have to say I think it falls squarely on Ruth Ginsburg. I think she was power hungry. She didn't retire under Obama, where he could have appointed a new justice. And so Trump got that opportunity because she waited till she died. So greed is really, what caused it to tip to that way.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group:\par \par \uc2 Marcia: It really doesn't affect me personally. Well, I shouldn't say that. I do have a young daughter. She and I did talk about it, and she asked me what was my stance on it. And I did tell her I was more pro-life than pro-choice. And she, being 13 -- she's, I guess, leaning more pro-choice. It's like this -- I respect people's choices and decisions. Even though I'm mostly pro-life, I do feel like there should be some allowances for abortions, such as rape or if a woman's life was in jeopardy -- her or the child -- that type of situation. But I just feel like we just have to accept that it's been overturned and it's going to be for the life.\par \par \uc2 Dwyarnn: In my household, I have two girls. If you get pregnant by being irresponsible, maybe you should have the baby and give it up for adoption. Now, I think the women are going to lose their minds. Some think, hey, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives them an excuse to be more irresponsible sometimes.\par \par \uc2 Gwendolyn: Mm-hmm. It's all about morals and ethics, morals and ethics because, you know what? At the end of the day, you're going to be accountable for it.\par \par \uc2 moderator: If you were describing someone who feels differently than you on this issue, how would you characterize them?\par \par \uc2 sue: It's a complicated topic. It's an emotional topic. I don't think badly of them. But I get concerned that maybe they don't understand the full impact of the consequences of the decision that they're saying should be allowed to be made.\par \par \uc2 moderator: And Eileen, how would you characterize someone who feels differently than you on this issue?\par \par \uc2 eileen: I assume that they are probably Democratic and --\par \par \uc2 gwendolyn: Not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 eileen: But I try not to judge people based on their opinions about things like this.\par \par \uc2 moderator: OK, Christina, how would you characterize someone who feels differently than you on this issue?\par \par \uc2 christina: I might still be friends with them. But every time I'd see them, I'd think, oh, they're baby killers.\par \par \uc2 How many think the Supreme Court may now re-examine other decisions, like the ones allowing gay marriage, birth control, sexual intimacy or interracial marriage?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group: 9 say yes, 6 say no.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey: I think it's really scary, just as a young person who's grown up in the age of everyone being able to marry whoever they wanted to.\par \par \uc2 Michael J: Justice Thomas hinted at a bunch of rulings that could be affected by this, even though Judge Alito said that this had nothing to do with those.\par \par \uc2 Rildon: One thing leads to another. If they can do this on this issue, what else are they going to try to change and try to alter and make us follow what they want us to do? They start infringing on our freedom to express ourselves and our freedom to decide to assemble and try to march. And all those things can easily start being taken away from us.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Adrienne, tell me why you think that might not happen.\par \par \uc2 adrienne: I think it's more about hot topics. And I don't think those are topics that they're going to revisit right now.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group: 6 say yes, 9 say no.\par \par \uc2 Christina: It's constantly going to be like -- well, why is that law OK and this one's not?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are now up to the states. What is happening in your state? Are you following it? What are the rules where you are?\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group:\par \par \uc2 Michael J: Well, I live in New York, so it's definitely pro-choice. That's not going to change.\par \par \uc2 Bridget: In Missouri, I think it's banned except if the mother's life is in severe danger.\par \par \uc2 moderator: And how does that make you feel that that's the rule in Missouri?\par \par \uc2 bridget: I'm honestly OK with it. The states get to decide. And Missouri is considerably a red state. So if that's what the majority of people in Missouri want, then I think that should be the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 moderator: How would you feel about your state passing a law that would protect the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in your state?\par \par \uc2 anne: I would be relieved.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Bridget, if Missouri passed a law protecting the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Missouri, how would you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 bridget: I would not be opposed to that.\par \par \uc2 moderator: You would not be opposed to it. So you're OK with either banning most abortions or protecting the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . You're OK with either type of law.\par \par \uc2 bridget: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 moderator2: What about a law that would prevent someone from crossing state lines to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a different state?\par \par \uc2 michaelj: If you honestly believe that the majority of your people in your state are good with not having \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be legal, that's one thing. But to prevent somebody from going to another state where it is legal is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 lindsey: Having a state next to you where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal and in your state it's not -- that disproportionately affects people who are underserved, people in poverty, people who are not as advantaged as those who have the resources to have the money for gas, rent a hotel, all of those things.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group:\par \par \uc2 Eileen: I'm in Missouri, and with the trigger law, it immediately made abortions illegal.\par \par \uc2 marcia: I would just like them to make it so that it's only permissible for a woman who may have been raped or incest or real early term.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Vincent, when I say, pass a law protecting the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in your state, give me your reaction.\par \par \uc2 vincent: I'm in agreement with it.: People have their rights. As far as religious purposes and different things, that's between them and God. When it comes to the law and religion, you have to separate that. When it comes down to morals and emotions, people have their rights -- whether you agree or disagree.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Make sure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws have exceptions for the health of the woman: What's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 sue: I think you have to have exceptions because sometimes it's not till just before the birth that they know that there has to be a decision between the life of the mother and the life of the baby.\par \par \uc2 vincent: I agree, and not just physically. There's also mental health.\par \par \uc2 sue: Well, I just want to clarify -- physical health of the mother, not mental health, not other things. There's too much room for making up reasons. It has to be related to the physical health.\par \par \uc2 moderator: That actually brings me to my next question: what do you think about making sure that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws have exceptions for the health of the woman, including if the woman is suicidal?\par \par \uc2 eileen: She needs mental help sooner than an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I don't think jumping to getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the answer.\par \par \uc2 9. In one word, describe how you feel about the Supreme Court right now.\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice group:\par \par \uc2 Rildon: One-sided.\par \par \uc2 Anne: Unsafe. Disgusted\par \par \uc2 Adrienne: I'd say inconsiderate.\par \par \uc2 Michael J: Tyrannical.\par \par \uc2 Bridget: I feel cautious.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life group:\par \par \uc2 Sue: Courageous.\par \par \uc2 Marcia: It does take courage to make decisions. Courageous.\par \par \uc2 Dwyarrn: Disappointed.\par \par \uc2 Eileen: I would say, difficult.\par \par \uc2 Gwendolyn: Disappointed.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Eileen, you said, difficult. Can you tell me more about that?\par \par \uc2 eileen: I think that they are just in a difficult position.\par \par \uc2 moderator: Gwendolyn, you said, disappointed.\par \par \uc2 gwendolyn: Man is in control of the Earth. I'm just going to stick to my religion and what I believe in. I wouldn't want the blood on my hands. So I wouldn't dare to make decisions.\par \par \uc2 sue: I don't think they made their decision based on public opinion. They tried to make it based on the letter of the law. That's really what their job is supposed to be.\par \par \uc2 moderator: How much does it matter if the court is in sync with public opinion?\par \par \uc2 michael j: You have polls every day that said that the majority of people in this country think that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal. Not just that, but the other decision that they made about the guns -- totally the opposite of where the country is going. They're totally out of step.\par \par \uc2 adrienne: I think that they should align somewhat with the people.\par \par \uc2 christina: I think they should definitely put into consideration the people that they're actually making the law for. So I think the people are what you're hired for anyway.\par \par \uc2 gwendolyn: Exactly. I agree.\par \par \uc2 dwyarrn: It's important that the government is in sync with the public opinion, but I don't think they are. If you're going to make drastic changes like that, you need to have more public opinion involved before you make those decisions.\par \par \uc2 anthony: I would just say that the Supreme Court needs to follow the law. That's what they were trained to do. But if the laws are doing things that would harm people or otherwise enforce corruption or damage the country as a whole, then maybe it's time to rewrite the laws and look at it differently.\par \par \uc2 If you agreed with a candidate on<br>most things but disagreed with that candidate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raise your hand if you could still vote for that person.\par \par \uc2 PROCHOICE GROUP:\par \par \uc2 2 raise their hand\par \par \uc2 PROLIFE GROUP:\par \par \uc2 8 raise their hand.\par \par \uc2 PROCHOICE\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and voting a little bit. How important is that issue going to be to you personally at the polls?\par \par \uc2 rildon: So for me, it's definitely going to be -- having a daughter, that's something that's going to be weighing on my mind when it comes time to voting. Again, my belief, I wouldn't have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But by the same token, I'm not one to be telling somebody what to do with their body. If they have an issue or they have a medical condition or they were a victim of rape or incest I definitely, would not be against that individual choosing to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 bridget: It's really not important to me.\par \par \uc2 PROLIFE\par \par \uc2 christina: I think it's very important. If you leave it up to somebody, they're just going to take advantage of it. And like a couple other people said, they're just going to use it as a form of birth control if they change it to pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 moderator2: ''Roe v Wade'' was the law for 50 years. 50 years from now, what do you think the debate around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be?\par \par \uc2 marcia: It'll probably be the same thing -- the right of a woman, if she has the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regardless of the reason. And they will probably want to overturn it again so that we will be more pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 eileen: Well, I think we'll have more data. So a lot of people are throwing out numbers right now about back alley abortions and stuff like that. ButI think we'll just be better educated, so we can have a more scientific discussion about it.\par \par \uc2 moderator2: Thank you. And Sue, do you think we'll still be debating this subject 50 years from now?\par \par \uc2 sue: I think there's always going to be people that are going to want to have a discussion about it because there's always going to be people who are in disagreement.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220717ei7h00082\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
