\par\par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The G.O.P. Is Threatening the Right to Travel\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamelle Bouie \par \uc2 1,182 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, it did more than just supercharge the assault on the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It also opened up a corresponding attack on the right to travel.\par \par \uc2 That attack is a straightforward consequence of giving states the power to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Ohio, for example, does not actually end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It simply pushes it underground or, for those who have the means, out of state. This, in fact, is what happened with a 10-year-old rape victim, who was recently taken out of state to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after she was impregnated as a result of the assault.\par \par \uc2 It is important to say that the Supreme Court has recognized a right to travel between states on multiple occasions in cases stretching back to the 19th century.\par \par \uc2 In Crandall v. State of Nevada, decided in the late 1860s, the court invalidated a Nevada law that imposed a $1 tax ''upon every person leaving the state by any railroad, stage coach or other vehicle engaged or employed in the business of transporting passengers for hire.'' Americans, wrote Justice Samuel Miller in his majority opinion, have a right to movement that is ''in its nature independent of the will of any state over whose soil he must pass in the exercise of it.''\par \par \uc2 The court affirmed this right a second time in Williams v. Fears in 1900. ''Undoubtedly,'' wrote Chief Justice Melville Fuller, ''the right of locomotion, the right to remove from one place to another according to inclination, is an attribute of personal liberty, and the right, ordinarily, of free transit from and through the territory of any state is a right secured by the 14th Amendment and by other provisions of the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 More recently, in Saenz v. Roe in 1999, a majority of the court recognized that, as Justice William Brennan put it in 1969, ''the nature of our Federal Union and our constitutional concepts of personal liberty unite to require that all citizens be free to travel throughout the length and breadth of our land uninhibited by statutes, rules or regulations which unreasonably burden or restrict this movement.'' The right to travel, wrote Justice \uc2 John Paul Stevens\uc2  in his majority opinion, quoting Justice Potter Stewart, ''is a virtually unconditional personal right, guaranteed by the Constitution to us all.''\par \par \uc2 There is nothing in the Supreme Court's reasoning in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health that would explicitly threaten the right to travel between states. In his concurrence with the majority's ruling, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  even says that in his view a state may not ''bar a resident of that state from traveling to another state to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 But that's exactly where some Republican-led states want to take the law.\par \par \uc2 Missouri lawmakers have introduced a ''bounty'' bill similar to the one now in operation in Texas, which would allow private citizens to sue anyone who helps a resident obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state. Another bill would apply Missouri's laws to abortions that occur in other states.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of Texas, a group of State House lawmakers who call themselves the Texas Freedom Caucus hope to ''impose additional civil and criminal sanctions on law firms that pay for abortions or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel,'' regardless of where the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurs.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization led by Republican state lawmakers has been exploring ''model legislation that would restrict people from crossing state lines for abortions.''\par \par \uc2 ''Just because you jump across a state line doesn't mean your home state doesn't have jurisdiction,'' Peter Breen, vice president of the Thomas More Society, told The Post. ''It's not a free \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  card when you drive across the state line.''\par \par \uc2 And in Washington, congressional Republicans have rejected an effort to affirm the right to travel. ''Does the child in the womb have the right to travel in their future?'' asked Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2  of Oklahoma, objecting to a Democratic bill that would bar restrictions on women traveling to another state to get a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There are few, if any, modern precedents for laws that limit the right of Americans to travel between states. To the extent that there is a history here, it lies in the legal conflicts over both fugitive slaves and free Black individuals in the decades before the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 ''As the North began emancipating those slaves whom slaveholders brought into free jurisdictions, slave states strove to strengthen a slaveholding power structure thought to be under Northern attack,'' the historian Edlie L. Wong writes in ''Neither Fugitive Nor Free: Atlantic Slavery, Freedom Suits, and the Legal Culture of Travel.'' Slave states, she writes, enacted increasingly punitive restrictions ''that prohibited free Blacks from traveling into slave jurisdictions.''\par \par \uc2 On the other side, slaveholders sought to use the legal system to restrict the movement of enslaved Americans out of the South. If the Northern state governments would not recognize the existence of slave property, then federal courts would.\par \par \uc2 The federal government was also reluctant to grant free Northern Black people the documents necessary to secure their freedom beyond the states in which they lived. The result was a world in which Black Americans were deprived of freedom of movement. This was true even after the Civil War when, in the wake of Reconstruction, ''redeemed'' Southern states put limits on the right of Black Americans to use public transportation and other forms of transit.\par \par \uc2 As Wong notes, the landmark case Plessy v. Ferguson in 1896 -- which affirmed Jim Crow segregation -- was ''fought over equal access to the technologies of intrastate railway travel.'' In his famous lone dissent, Justice John Marshall Harlan reminded the court that '''personal liberty' as it has been well said, 'consists in the power of locomotion, of changing station, or removing one's person to whatsoever place one's own inclination may direct; without imprisonment or restraint.'''\par \par \uc2 There is no direct parallel between the travel rights of women, girls and others who can give birth under anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and the travel rights of Black Americans under various forms of legalized unfreedom. But there is an echo of a question that relates to both situations: What happens to the rights of citizens when their bodies become property under the law?\par \par \uc2 And make no mistake: When a state claims the right to limit your travel on account of your body -- when it claims one of the most fundamental aspects of your personal liberty in order to take control of your \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- then that state has rendered you little more than another form of property.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY CAROLYN DRAKE/MAGNUM ) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220717ei7h0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
