\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Zingers Unleashed In  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Battle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melena Ryzik \par \uc2 1,535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lizz Winstead, the ''Daily Show'' co-creator, has marshaled her contacts in the standup world to help supporters turn protest into action.\par \par \uc2 ''Things are awesome -- never better!'' joked Lizz Winstead, the comedian, producer and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activist. ''Sleeping well; no diarrhea. Things are awesome.''\par \par \uc2 Things are decidedly not awesome, but comic misdirection might as well be oxygen for Winstead, who has banked her career on satirizing politics and media and calling out hypocrisy, as the co-creator of ''The Daily Show'' and a host and director on the now-defunct left-wing radio network Air America. For most of the last decade or so, though, she has been singularly, steadfastly focused on one issue, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her preferred method for delivering her message is the variety show: a little schtick, a little song, a little taboo talk.\par \par \uc2 ''Don't be ashamed of having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' the comedian Joyelle Nicole Johnson said onstage at ''Bro v. Wade,'' a benefit show in Brooklyn that Winstead organized recently with her group \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Front. ''Maybe be ashamed of how you got pregnant. I got pregnant the classy way: On the floor. On an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train. In the handicapped restroom, babeeey!''\par \par \uc2 Joking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nothing new; George Carlin went there, among many others. But Winstead's goal is sharper: with righteous fervor and a Rolodex of comic all-stars, she leads a nonprofit that uses unexpected tools -- like humor and men -- to advocate for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as health care and as a fundamental human right.\par \par \uc2 She told her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  story on a \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2  special in 1992, and in the decades since, has been warning, on stages across the country and in social media campaigns, that \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  were in jeopardy. Long before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Front was preparing. Now, it is mobilizing as never before: On Sunday it will host ''Operation Save \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' a livestreamed daylong training session, with more than 60 partners and 25 panelists from local and national care, funding and policy organizations, and secure ways for viewers to plan direct, on-the-ground action. It will be capped off by a set from Johnson, a board member and ride-or-die touring performer, who lately has become accustomed to delivering punch lines to an audience that has spent the day weeping.\par \par \uc2 ''Politicians aren't going to save us,'' Winstead, 60, said. Laughter won't either. But with her network of grassroots advocates, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and entertainers, she hopes to change the narrative around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , eliminate the shame and give newly fired-up supporters the tools to get involved. ''If people have to march one more time, and rage and feel helpless and hopeless, they win,'' she said of her anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. ''We need to give people who are, like, 'What can we do?' an answer,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 That includes the people responsible for 50 percent of a pregnancy -- men. On the eve of Father's Day last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Front produced a ''Dads for Choice'' video starring W. Kamau Bell, the comedian, CNN host and commentator, and inviting men to consider who bears the monetary costs of contraception: ''Nobody ever got pregnant from a vibrator!''\par \par \uc2 [Video: Watch on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .]\par \par \uc2 ''The more complicated the issues are, the more humor can break things down to their basic points, and clarify things,'' Bell said. Especially for topics that have traditionally been deemed uncomfortable, ''humor can invite people in.''\par \par \uc2 Why might comedy be an especially effective tool now? ''Well, the other stuff hasn't worked,'' said David Cross, who was part of the all-male ''Bro v. Wade'' lineup. ''Look where we find ourselves.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Front performances feature sketch comedy; music; standups like Sarah Silverman, Michelle Buteau, Jenny Yang, Aida Rodriguez and Negin Farsad and notables like the writer Dan Savage and filmmaker Mark Duplass; and on the road, conversations with local \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, to highlight their needs. Even pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights crowds are often edified and galvanized, according to audience surveys collected by a researcher from the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''To give you the joy and then the information and then give you something to do, that trifecta of an evening is magic,'' Winstead said. Beginning in 2016, the showcases toured annually across dozens of cities, including in states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of the group's messages is that everybody can find a way to contribute; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters need not march in every street protest or continually reach into their overstretched wallets. ''If you have 10 minutes a month to give, I can give you something to do that's meaningful,'' Winstead said. ''And I don't want you to feel bad that that's all you can give. Life is too messed up right now.''\par \par \uc2 At one clinic, in Huntsville, Ala., \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Front and its volunteers planted hedges out front, to help block anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters. In Detroit, at the request of another clinic, they threw a block party as a gesture of welcome and gratitude to the community.\par \par \uc2 ''For a lot of these folks, in the only clinic in their state, they feel really isolated,'' said Amy Elizabeth Alterman, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  scholar, ethnographer and public health researcher at U.C.L.A. Out of safety concerns or for social reasons, ''many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers don't tell friends and family what they do.''\par \par \uc2 Winstead's organization, which has a full-time staff of 10 and many volunteers, served as a much-needed balm. ''When a band of feminists explodes out of a van, wearing pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  swag and saying, 'Thank you for what you do. What can we do and how can we celebrate you?' it's often very emotional,'' Alterman said. ''Sometimes providers cry.''\par \par \uc2 Winstead and the group are not trying to reach across the aisle to change anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  evangelists' minds. Since she became outspoken on the issue, she has personally experienced a backlash. ''My parents, when they were alive, got calls constantly saying, your daughter's a baby murderer,'' she said. They were Catholic -- ''it really scared them.'' Her shows were boycotted; old employers were called in efforts at intimidation. She ''paid a lot of money,'' she said, to erase her personal data from the internet.\par \par \uc2 Now, ''there's no place I can get fired from -- come at me, I don't care,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Fomenting any cultural shift requires real dedication, said Dean Obeidallah, the comic and radio host, who was on the ''Bro v. Wade'' bill and performed at Winstead's first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights benefit a decade ago. ''I can tell you from years of doing comedy, and trying to dispel stereotypes about Arab Americans, it's never a light-bulb moment,'' he said. ''For people on your side already, you have to make them feel like they're in the right place. For people who aren't on the right side, or even have hate, it's chipping away.''\par \par \uc2 For those in the Minnesota-born Winstead's orbit, it's not surprising that she rose from a politically minded standup to an activist leader. ''She's just one of those -- you meet them throughout life -- boundless energy, high-strung, talk very quickly, gesticulate wildly, kind of people,'' said Cross, a friend for more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Johnson, the comic who has been with the organization since it started, said, ''I think she's a non-somniac, like Obama's a non-somniac, to be able to do all the things she does. Her brain is constantly worrying -- since 2016, her hair has turned white.''\par \par \uc2 Even for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Front, whose allies long knew that reproductive care and women's rights were under attack, the weeks since Roe v. Wade was overturned have been, as Winstead said, gut-churningly surreal and destabilizing. ''I've always felt unsafe in this country,'' said Johnson, who is Black, ''but now it's almost a slapstick level of unsafe. It's chaos.''\par \par \uc2 Winstead said, ''This is almost our last shot, because we're burned out -- and that's by design.''\par \par \uc2 But this moment has also sharpened activists' focus, and expanded their tent. Since Roe was overturned, ''I talk about it every chance -- you're going to hear \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of Joyelle's mouth,'' Johnson said. ''I do it for the women in the audience who are not as liberated as me, those women who cannot tell their closest family members. I hope it liberates some people.''\par \par \uc2 Viva Ruiz, a performer and activist whose group, Thank God For \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is involved in the training session Sunday, said, ''Everybody needs to use their way -- the more variance there is, the more tactics there are, the more successful we can be.'' She added, ''The thing is, to just not stop. To keep showing up.''\par \par \uc2 Together, Winstead agreed, ''we are more motivated to fight and stay in the fight. And be relentless.''\par \par \uc2 Clockwise from top: Lizz Winstead started \uc2  Access Front to change the narrative around the procedure; the crowd viewing a ''Bro v. Wade'' show; David Cross performing. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY NINA WESTERVELT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (C5) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220716ei7g0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
