\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 'Proper Action' Was Taken in Girl's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Lawyer Says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A document obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  shows that the Indiana obstetrician reported the child's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the state.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the Indiana obstetrician who has drawn national attention for providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care to a 10-year-old rape victim said on Thursday that the doctor had taken ''every appropriate and proper action'' in the case -- including filing necessary forms to report the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with state authorities, contrary to the assertions of Indiana's attorney general.\par \par \uc2 The case of the rape victim, who crossed state lines from Ohio to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Indianapolis, has become a hot-button issue in the nation's roiling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. Many on the right -- including the Ohio attorney general -- initially doubted the veracity of the story when it was shared by Dr. Caitlin Bernard, the Indiana obstetrician. The story was confirmed after the arrest of the man accused of raping the girl. Yet Dr. Bernard remained under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 The Indiana attorney general, Todd Rokita, told Fox News on Wednesday that he intended to investigate Dr. Bernard, and suggested she failed to file required reports.\par \par \uc2 ''We're gathering the information, we're gathering the evidence as we speak, and we're going to fight this to the end, including looking at her licensure if she failed to report,'' Mr. Rokita said in an interview, in which he assailed Dr. Bernard, who practices in Indianapolis, as an ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist acting as a doctor, with a history of failing to report.''\par \par \uc2 But a document obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  appears to contradict Mr. Rokita's assertion. The document, a ''Terminated Pregnancy Report'' filed with the \uc2 Indiana Department of Health\uc2  and the Department of Child Services, states that Dr. Bernard provided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication -- a two-pill combination that can be taken at home or in any location and is authorized for use in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy -- to a 10-year-old patient on June 30. The report was dated July 2, which puts it within Indiana's three-day reporting requirement.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard's lawyer, Kathleen DeLaney, said in the statement issued Thursday that she is ''considering legal action against'' Mr. Rokita and others, adding that ''the facts will all come out in due time.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. Rokita said on Thursday night that his office was continuing to gather evidence, and that ''our legal review of it remains open.''\par \par \uc2 The Ohio girl's story, which first appeared in The Indianapolis Star, cast a sharp spotlight on the difficulties the most vulnerable have obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in the wake of the Supreme Court's decision to overturn the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enshrined in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Ohio law bans almost all abortions after fetal cardiac activity can be detected, usually about six weeks of pregnancy. According to a doctor familiar with the girl's case, and testimony from a court hearing, her family took her to Indiana to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the procedure is legal up to 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden cited the Ohio girl as an example of the cruelty of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in some states. ''Raped, six weeks pregnant. Already traumatized. Was forced to travel to another state,'' Mr. Biden said last week while signing an executive order on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents and some right-leaning outlets cast doubt on whether the story was even true. A Wall Street Journal editorial with the headline ''An \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Story Too Good to Confirm'' asserted that the president had put his ''seal of approval on an unlikely story from a biased source.''\par \par \uc2 But the speculation ended on Wednesday when it was revealed that a 27-year-old Ohio man had been arrested and charged with the rape. (The Journal later published an editor's note atop its editorial.)\par \par \uc2 The form filed by Dr. Bernard lists the approximate age of the father as 17. Doctors often make their best guess when the pregnancy is the result of abuse, and little may be known about the perpetrator.\par \par \uc2 The case has also exposed the vulnerabilities of medical professionals, like Dr. Bernard, who continue to care for patients amid a patchwork of state laws regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dr. Bernard herself struck back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 ''My heart breaks for all survivors of sexual assault and abuse. I am so sad that our country is failing them when they need us most,'' she wrote. ''Doctors must be able to give people the medical care they need, when and where they need it.''\par \par \uc2 Under Indiana law, the doctor who performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a patient younger than 16 must report it to both the \uc2 Indiana Department of Health\uc2  and the Department of Child Services within three days, though the statute does not specify whether that might change if the patient is referred from out of state.\par \par \uc2 ''These laws are designed to trap us,'' said Dr. Katie McHugh, an OB-GYN in Indiana and board member of the group Physicians for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. McHugh said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, particularly those in states led by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers, have long been wary of the precise reporting requirements laid out in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations.\par \par \uc2 ''These \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporting requirements are seeking to criminalize the provider instead of the actual criminals,'' she said. ''The A.G.'s comments are not about protecting children; they are about targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. DeLaney, the lawyer, said Dr. Bernard had ''followed all relevant policies, procedures, and regulations in this case, just as she does every day to provide the best possible care for her patients.\par \par \uc2 ''She has not violated any law, including patient privacy laws, and she has not been disciplined by her employer.''\par \par \uc2 The Indiana attorney general, Todd Rokita, has questioned a doctor's care of an Ohio girl. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DARRON CUMMINGS/ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220715ei7f0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
