\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Administration Tells Pharmacists Not to Withhold  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 1,152 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 New Biden administration guidance warned that failing to dispense such drugs ''may be discriminating'' on the basis of sex or disability, citing other conditions that they can treat.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Biden administration warned the nation's 60,000 retail pharmacies on Wednesday that they risk violating federal civil rights law if they refuse to fill prescriptions for pills that can induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the second time this week that it has used its executive authority to set up showdowns with states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now illegal.\par \par \uc2 In four pages of guidance, the federal \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  ticked off a series of conditions -- including miscarriage, stomach ulcers and ectopic pregnancy -- that are commonly treated with drugs that can induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It warned that failing to dispense such pills ''may be discriminating'' on the basis of sex or disability.\par \par \uc2 The guidance came two days after \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , President Biden's health secretary, instructed hospitals that even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now illegal, federal law requires doctors to perform abortions for pregnant women who show up in their emergency departments if they believe it is ''the stabilizing treatment necessary'' to resolve an emergency medical condition.\par \par \uc2 The back-to-back actions make clear that while Mr. Biden's authority to preserve access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited after the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to the procedure last month, he will push those limits where he can. Legal experts on both sides of the issue agreed in interviews that the administration was trying to assert that federal law pre-empts that of states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move that would almost surely be challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 ''They are trying to identify federal statutes that in some way will supersede state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and bans,'' said Lawrence O. Gostin, an expert in public health law at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . Of the guidance for pharmacists, he said, ''The obvious goal is to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in stock to treat a range of medical conditions and to be available for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Yet the new guidance is cautiously written, and steers clear of telling pharmacies that they have to provide the drugs for the purpose of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is banned or restricted in certain states. Nor does the guidance address how a provision in federal law called the Church Amendments would apply. That measure allows health care providers, including pharmacists, not to perform or assist in abortions if they have religious or moral objections.\par \par \uc2 At issue are three drugs -- mifepristone, misoprostol and methotrexate -- that are often prescribed for other conditions but can also induce abortions. Experts said the administration was reacting to reports that women of childbearing age are being denied the drugs after the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is used to manage certain patients with a hormonal disorder called Cushing's syndrome, and misoprostol is prescribed for ulcers. But they are also authorized by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  as a two-drug combination that can be taken to terminate a pregnancy during the first 10 weeks, and can also be used in combination following miscarriages. Methotrexate is used to treat autoimmune disorders, such as rheumatoid arthritis, as well as cancer.\par \par \uc2 ''These are very legitimate issues in terms of people being concerned about having access to the basic medications that they have been receiving for years, just because those medications have the capacity to end a pregnancy,'' said \uc2 Alina Salganicoff\uc2 , the director of women's health policy at the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 . ''It doesn't sound like they are blocking this for men.''\par \par \uc2 The administration's moves will almost certainly be challenged in court, and advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights concede that it could be a losing battle. If legal challenges work their way up to the Supreme Court, the administration will have to make its case before the same conservative supermajority who voted to overturn Roe v. Wade, the landmark legal case that established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973.\par \par \uc2 ''They're trying to mandate the stocking of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs and the performance of abortions across the nation using tools that don't grant the federal government that authority,'' said \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2 , who ran the \uc2 Office of Civil Rights\uc2  within the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  when Donald J. Trump was president. ''They are trying to shoehorn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into laws that clearly weren't designed to address \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's action could put pharmacists in a thorny position. The \uc2 National Community Pharmacists Association\uc2 , which represents 19,400 independent pharmacies across the country, said pharmacists ''acting in good faith in accordance with their state's laws'' lacked ''a clear pathway forward'' and needed more guidance from states.\par \par \uc2 ''States have provided very little clarity on how pharmacists should proceed in light of conflicting state and federal laws and regulations,'' B. Douglas Hoey, the organization's chief executive, said in a statement. ''It is highly unfair for state and federal governments to threaten aggressive action against pharmacists who are just trying to serve their patients within new legal boundaries that are still taking shape.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , one of the nation's largest pharmacy chains, said the company would review the guidelines; he had no further comment.\par \par \uc2 During a background call with reporters, an official from the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  said that when state and federal laws conflicted, federal law took precedent.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has been under intense pressure from Democrats and advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take bold steps to preserve the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. Among other things, they have been pushing for him to declare a public health emergency -- something his administration seems unwilling to do.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's guidance was issued by the health department's \uc2 Office of Civil Rights\uc2 . Monday's guidance for hospitals was accompanied by a letter to health care providers, delineating their responsibilities under the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, known as EMTALA, a 1986 law that requires anyone coming to an emergency department to be stabilized and treated regardless of insurance status or ability to pay.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Severino argued that the guidance to hospitals ''flips EMTALA on its head,'' because the law defines an emergency as a condition in which the absence of immediate medical attention ''could reasonably be expected to result in placing the health of the patient, or (in case of pregnancy, the unborn child) in serious jeopardy.'' But Mr. Gostin took the administration's position, saying that in the case of a pregnant woman in distress, the law permitted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''if it was necessary to save her life and there was no other way to stabilize her.''\par \par \uc2 A misoprostol pill, which can be used in combination with another drug to terminate early pregnancies. (PHOTOGRAPH BY George Frey/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220714ei7e0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
