\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Louisiana Judge Blocks Ban On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  for Second Time\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 744 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For the second time, a judge temporarily blocked the state's trigger bans, which went into effect after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Abortions became legal again in Louisiana on Tuesday after a Baton Rouge judge granted a temporary restraining order blocking the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans from being enforced.\par \par \uc2 The judge's decision was the latest in a series of legal maneuvers that have jolted the legal status of abortions in the state, leaving women and providers scrambling to adapt. One of the few remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the state said it would resume providing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is among a number of conservative states that passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions or bans in anticipation that the Supreme Court would overturn Roe v. Wade, the landmark decision that since 1973 had guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the court did so last month, triggering those state bans, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups and providers sued, arguing that state constitutions protected the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana plaintiffs were among the first to file after the overturning of Roe, and a judge in New Orleans granted a temporary restraining order that stopped the state's three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans from taking effect. But on Friday a different judge said that the New Orleans court did not have the authority to issue the restraining order, and that the groups had to file in Baton Rouge, the state capital.\par \par \uc2 That decision immediately outlawed abortions starting at conception, with an exception for a threat to the life of a pregnant woman, but with no exceptions for rape or incest. Under one Louisiana law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers face possible jail time of 10 or 15 years, depending on when the pregnancy was terminated.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the court said abortions could resume at least until Monday, when it will hold a hearing to consider the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers' argument to put the bans on hold more permanently.\par \par \uc2 ''Two separate Louisiana judges have now rightly found that irreparable harm will occur if the trigger bans are enforced,'' Joanna Wright, a lawyer for the plaintiffs, said.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2 , the state's attorney general, posted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that it was disappointing ''to have the judiciary create a legal circus'' after Louisianans had spoken at the ballot box and through their elected legislators.\par \par \uc2 ''The rule of law must be followed, and I will not rest until it is,'' he said. ''Unfortunately, we will have to wait a little bit longer for that to happen. Any society that places themselves before their children (the future) does not last.''\par \par \uc2 The clinic that joined the lawsuit, Hope Medical Group for Women, said it would again start providing abortions in Shreveport. It had stopped providing them on Friday, but remained open to see patients for ultrasounds and ''options counseling,'' said Kathaleen Pittman, the clinic's administrator. The clinic had redirected patients to providers in other states, but surrounding states have also severely restricted or banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It is insane,'' Ms. Pittman said on Tuesday. ''It is stressful. But more so for our patients than staff.''\par \par \uc2 Jenny Ma, a lawyer for the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called it ''an incredible relief for people who need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care right now in Louisiana.''\par \par \uc2 ''Every hour and every day that a clinic can still provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care fundamentally changes the lives of people for the better,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana's State Constitution is unusual in that it explicitly says it does not provide a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning the Legislature has the right to pass laws restricting it. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state argue that the trigger laws violate due process provisions in the Constitution and are ''void for vagueness'' because they do not provide specifics about what is legal or not under the law.\par \par \uc2 The fast-changing status of Louisiana's trigger laws is emblematic of the chaotic national landscape that has unfolded in the weeks since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Litigators for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups rolled out lawsuits challenging trigger bans in a dozen states just after the court's ruling. Judges rejected those challenges in Ohio and Mississippi, but other cases, including in Oklahoma, continue.\par \par \uc2 Ava Sasani contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ava Sasani contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 A protest in New Orleans in June after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Kathleen Flynn for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220713ei7d0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
