\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Health Plans Get Difficult On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tara Siegel Bernard \par \uc2 2,099 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Supreme Court ruling, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefits will depend even more on where a woman lives and who provides her insurance.\par \par \uc2 Even before the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was struck down last month, health insurance coverage for it was spotty. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefits largely depended on where a woman lived or whom she worked for.\par \par \uc2 Those factors have become even more crucial now that the Supreme Court has left it to the states to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The existing patchwork of coverage is likely to become more restrictive, and the gulf will widen between states that had already banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related benefits and those that mandate coverage.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of large employers are attempting to fill the gaps by paying expenses for workers who need to cross state lines for a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though there are still questions about how much risk companies are taking on and how far an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state -- or an aggressive prosecutor -- could go to stop them.\par \par \uc2 For women without that extra support or coverage, the new logistical challenges and the added costs may be impossible to overcome.\par \par \uc2 ''People with financial resources will find ways to get the treatment they want or need,'' said Susan M. Nash, a benefits lawyer and partner at \uc2 Winston & Strawn\uc2  with expertise in health care. ''But the people who can't travel or have limited means to access treatment outside of the health plan will be adversely impacted here.''\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at how the decision, Dobbs v. Jackson, may affect insurance coverage across the country:\par \par \uc2 How much does an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cost?\par \par \uc2 The median cost to a patient for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which involves two drugs, generally taken up to 10 to 12 weeks of pregnancy -- was $560 in 2020, according to a recent study at the \uc2 University of California San Francisco\uc2 's Advancing New Standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  program. A procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was $575 during the first trimester and $895 during the second trimester. That doesn't include travel costs and other expenses, such as child care and time off from work, which will be increasingly necessary for women in a growing number of states. And costs varied substantially by region.\par \par \uc2 Most patients pay out of pocket, research has found, in large part because their insurance doesn't cover the procedure. Even before the Dobbs decision, 11 states restricted the type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage private health insurance plans could cover, and 26 states barred all plans in their state's health insurance exchange from covering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , researchers found.\par \par \uc2 Will Medicaid cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Just like before the ruling, that largely depends on where you live.\par \par \uc2 Medicaid, a public health program largely for low-income households that is administered by the states, is financed by federal and state money. Even before the Dobbs decision, federal law -- known as the Hyde Amendment -- didn't allow federal funds to pay for abortions, except in limited circumstances: if the pregnancy was the result of rape or incest or caused a life-endangering condition for the woman. States could choose to use their own money to pay for abortions beyond those situations, and 16 states had such policies last year, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  (though nine were ordered by courts to have them).\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of states don't pay for anything beyond those limited circumstances -- and South Dakota, in violation of federal law, covers abortions only in the case of life endangerment, according to a 2019 study by the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Like South Dakota, a growing list of states that ban abortions -- including Alabama, Arkansas, Louisiana and Missouri -- make exceptions only when the woman's life is endangered. That puts them in conflict with federal law that also requires \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services said it would notify states when they were out of compliance with federal requirements, and added that the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  was taking steps to expand access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in those limited circumstances. Details on how that will happen are still vague.\par \par \uc2 What about marketplace plans?\par \par \uc2 The health insurance marketplace created under the Affordable Care Act has similar restrictions. Plans offered within the marketplace are not required to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and federal money -- including premium subsidies in the form of tax credits -- cannot be used to pay for them. Here, too, there are exceptions for rape, incest and life endangerment, but they are not universal.\par \par \uc2 There are 26 states that ban marketplace plan coverage of abortions, said \uc2 Alina Salganicoff\uc2 , director of women's health policy at Kaiser. But a few states don't make exceptions for rape or incest, and some states make no exceptions at all, she added.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, insurers in seven states are required to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage in all plans sold on the marketplace, according to Kaiser, but no federal dollars are used.\par \par \uc2 For example, in states like New York, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal under state law, policy holders with subsidized marketplace plans have $1 of their monthly premium held separately to be used for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other services.\par \par \uc2 But if an individual with a marketplace plan lives in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned, it's likely their policy won't provide coverage in their own state or across state lines.\par \par \uc2 I'm covered by my employer's plan. Will my benefits change?\par \par \uc2 That will depend on where you live, the type of insurance plan your employer uses and their stance on coverage.\par \par \uc2 Basically, if a company pays for its employees' health care from its own coffers, workers, even those in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, may have broader access to benefits. But employers that buy insurance policies for workers could be further restricted.\par \par \uc2 Large employers are often self-insured, which means they collect a share of their workers' premiums and pay for their health care (though an insurer or administrator usually processes claims). These plans generally follow federal rules under the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, known as ERISA, which provide broad flexibility in designing a health care plan.\par \par \uc2 Other employers buy insurance on behalf of their workers, and the insurer is responsible for costs. Health insurers are regulated by the states and must follow their rules -- if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned there, you're unlikely to receive any coverage, even if you travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 Many larger employers are providing travel benefits for workers who would need to cross state lines for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is often an extension of existing policies. Typically these plans have offered travel benefits for people seeking cancer treatments, transplants or other specialized therapies if they don't have access to a provider in their state or have to travel a certain number of miles to reach one, benefits lawyers said.\par \par \uc2 But there are still concerns about employers' criminal and civil liability, particularly in states with laws that would call for criminal prosecution of anyone in the state who ''aids and abets'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if it occurs in another state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 Health plans governed by federal ERISA regulations may have additional protections against legal actions brought under state law, benefits experts said, as long as the services are legal in the state where they are provided. Benefits lawyers also point to Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's concurrence in Dobbs, in which he said states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans could not stop women from seeking the procedure elsewhere. But while ERISA regulations often supersede state laws that may apply to plans, that doesn't extend to state criminal laws.\par \par \uc2 ''This issue will likely be the subject of continuing litigation and debate,'' according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  task force at Morgan Lewis, a law firm in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Will my employer coverage work differently if I travel out of state?\par \par \uc2 That depends on your plan.\par \par \uc2 But when an insured person travels out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or related medication, it's more likely the providers will fall outside the plan's network, which generally costs the insured person more or means expenses will be reimbursed at a lower rate, said Sarah Raaii, a senior associate at \uc2 McDermott, Will & Emery\uc2 . Some employers could decide to ease that burden by making up the difference and covering certain out-of-network procedures and services at the in-network rate, she added.\par \par \uc2 What does the ruling mean for telehealth coverage?\par \par \uc2 Women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans may consider getting prescriptions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related drugs through a telehealth visit from a provider located in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted.\par \par \uc2 It may not be easy. Even before the Dobbs decision, some states banned telehealth visits from prescribing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing medications or had other rules that limited the feasibility of remote visits anyway. And six states had laws that banned the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs, according to Kaiser.\par \par \uc2 With broader \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the rules around telehealth visits and insurance coverage may become trickier. But one aspect is relatively clear: The location of the patient during the telehealth visit will determine whether it's legal to prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication at that time, said Marshall E. Jackson Jr., a partner with \uc2 McDermott Will & Emery\uc2  who focuses on digital health care.\par \par \uc2 For example, a patient living in Missouri, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned, could not have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication prescribed during a telehealth consultation while she was in her home state. But if the telehealth visit occurred while she was working from her employer's office in Illinois -- and the medication was mailed there -- that would be generally permitted, he said.\par \par \uc2 How much do I have to worry about privacy if I use insurance?\par \par \uc2 That will also depend on your location.\par \par \uc2 ''If there is a criminal statute in the state you live in, you need to be concerned,'' said \uc2 Amy M. Gordon\uc2 , a partner and benefits lawyer at \uc2 Winston & Strawn\uc2 . ''It depends on how aggressive prosecutors will be prosecuting those outside of their state. Therefore, the risks are still open questions.''\par \par \uc2 Let's say you live in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned but you travel to another state to receive a prescription for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs or to have the procedure. When plan benefits are used to pay, that information is generally protected under the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, known as HIPAA, the federal rule that governs the privacy of a patient's health records.\par \par \uc2 But medical and billing records that are typically kept private can be released without the patient's written permission in response to a warrant or subpoena.\par \par \uc2 ''An employer that sponsors a group health plan can argue this is protected health information, and therefore it should not be turned over,'' Ms. Gordon said. ''However, this is not bulletproof. There are provisions in HIPAA that permit disclosure to law enforcement seeking to enforce the law.''\par \par \uc2 Are there resources for people without insurance?\par \par \uc2 Yes. The federal government is encouraging people to visit reproductiverights.gov, which includes links to other resources that can help users find \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, which can provide financial assistance.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, there are more than 80 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds that help patients seeking the procedure or medication. Other groups, including the Brigid Alliance, help provide travel, lodging and logistical support to patients.\par \par \uc2 Is coverage for infertility treatments or contraceptives affected?\par \par \uc2 Contraceptive coverage is not affected by the ruling. Most private health plans, including plans in the health insurance marketplace, must cover contraceptive methods and counseling, including emergency contraceptives, as prescribed by a health care provider, according to Ellen Montz, director of the Center for Consumer Information and Insurance Oversight at the Center for Medicare & Medicaid Services.\par \par \uc2 These plans must cover these services without charging a co-payment or coinsurance when provided by an in-network provider -- even if someone hasn't met the deductible, she added.\par \par \uc2 But there are fears that some types of infertility treatments, which are increasingly covered by employer-provided insurance, could be restricted, depending on the legal language included in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts say new rules don't pose an immediate threat to infertility patients, their health care providers and embryos created in labs, but that could quickly change, depending on how the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are enforced.\par \par \uc2 ''That could be the next frontier,'' said Ms. Raaii of \uc2 McDermott, Will & Emery\uc2 , ''through which states try to enforce these laws against patients, providers, employers, payers or others.''\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Melek Zertal FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220713ei7d0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
