\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 House Democrats Set Votes For Bills on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Karni \par \uc2 1,461 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats in Congress are under pressure to act after the Supreme Court's decision. But with Republicans and at least one Democrat opposed in the Senate, the measures are all but certain to fail.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Democrats in Congress, under pressure to take quick action in response to the Supreme Court's decision striking down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, are planning to hold votes this week on legislation that seeks to preserve access to abortions in the post-Roe v. Wade era.\par \par \uc2 The bills are all but certain to fail in the evenly divided Senate, where broad Republican opposition means they cannot muster the 60 votes to move forward. But Democrats are pushing forward amid a groundswell of pressure from progressives in their ranks who were outraged by the Supreme Court ruling and irate that their leaders appeared to have no plan to respond.\par \par \uc2 In the immediate aftermath of the decision last month that overturned the nearly 50-year-old precedent that established \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, as protesters gathered outside the Supreme Court demanding action, House Democrats convened on the steps of the Capitol across the street, singing ''God Bless America'' to celebrate passage of a gun safety bill.\par \par \uc2 The gesture was widely derided by activists on the left, who denounced the lack of a cogent response from President Biden or from Congress to a ruling that had been anticipated for weeks.\par \par \uc2 Democrats on Capitol Hill are now moving forward with a plan that is aimed at framing the issue for the midterm elections. Lacking the votes to force action, Democrats are using the debate to show voters where they stand and portray Republicans as out of step with a majority of Americans, who polls consistently show support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The first measure, which Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  plans to bring to a vote this week, would protect the right to travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. A second measure, a version of which passed the House last year, would explicitly give health care providers the right to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and their patients the right to obtain them, invalidating a variety of restrictions that were enacted in the aftermath of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats tried and failed in May to take up that legislation, the Women's Health Protection Act. But Republicans and one Democrat -- Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2  of West Virginia -- opposed it, blocking a debate and leaving the measure short of even the simple majority that it would need to clear Congress.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats also plan to bring up more legislation in the coming weeks that would protect the privacy of patients' health records, amid concern that law enforcement officials could try to use the data to track pregnancies and enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions or bans.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Steny H. Hoyer\uc2  of Maryland, the majority leader, said in a letter to colleagues that leadership would look into more legislative steps ''to respond to the Dobbs v. Jackson decision and protect the rights, health and safety of Americans under a court determined to ignore precedent and eager to legislate.''\par \par \uc2 On Friday, reacting to pressure to respond more forcefully to the Supreme Court decision, Mr. Biden also issued an executive order aimed at ensuring access to contraception and other women's health services. ''A 10-year-old girl shouldn't be forced to give birth to a rapist's child,'' said Mr. Biden, who grew visibly angry.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, as a follow-up to the president's order, the Biden administration instructed hospitals that, even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, federal law required doctors to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a pregnant woman who showed up in the emergency department if they believed it was ''the stabilizing treatment necessary'' to resolve an emergency medical condition.\par \par \uc2 ''Under the law, no matter where you live, women have the right to emergency care -- including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,'' \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , Mr. Biden's health secretary, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Becerra made his position known in a letter to health care providers, giving them official guidance on their responsibilities under the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, also known as EMTALA, a 1986 law that requires anyone coming to an emergency department to be stabilized and treated, regardless of insurance status or ability to pay.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill, Democrats plan to debate measures that would preserve as much access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as possible.\par \par \uc2 One bill, sponsored by Representative Lizzie Fletcher, Democrat of Texas, would prohibit any state from enacting or enforcing a law restricting travel to another state to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Texas, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, about 1,400 women are leaving each month seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, Ms. Fletcher said. Planned Parenthood health centers in neighboring states have experienced a nearly 800 percent increase in patients seeking abortions since Texas instituted its ban, according to the organization.\par \par \uc2 ''We are hearing legislators in our state already talking about preventing women from leaving the state to obtain abortions, and it's not just Texas,'' Ms. Fletcher said in an interview. ''The idea that you can't leave the state to get \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in a state where it's legal makes this is an essential place to start.''\par \par \uc2 The Senate, however, remains the main blockade to any legislative efforts to codify Roe v. Wade into law. All but two of the chamber's Republicans oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, leaving little hope that any bill could move forward; with Mr. Manchin also opposed, passage would be all but impossible.\par \par \uc2 Aware of that reality, Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Democrat of New York and the majority leader, has turned his focus to the confirmation of circuit judges, unwilling to use his remaining floor time before the midterms on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related bills that have no chance of enactment.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Kaine, Democrat of Virginia, who is a practicing Catholic, and Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , Democrat of Arizona, have been working with Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  of Alaska, the only two Republican supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the Senate, to introduce legislation that would codify the framework of Roe and related cases. But Democrats dismissed an alternative proposal, arguing that it was toothless and lacked clear guidance about what states could and could not do.\par \par \uc2 Looking for some path to channel the anger felt by many on the left, Senate Democrats are planning to hold hearings in the coming weeks. Senator \uc2 Richard J. Durbin\uc2 , Democrat of Illinois and the chairman of the Judiciary Committee, has scheduled a hearing on Tuesday on the legal consequences of the Dobbs decision and ''to explore the grim reality of a post-Roe America.'' Witnesses include Dr. Colleen P. McNicholas, a Planned Parenthood \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Missouri and Southern Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , the chairwoman of the Health, Education, Labor and Pensions Committee, has scheduled another hearing for next week with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and physicians.\par \par \uc2 Many progressives say the spate of hearings and votes come too late.\par \par \uc2 ''It's inexcusable for Democratic leadership to have known for months that this was coming and muster such an anemic, milquetoast response,'' said Stephanie Taylor, a founder of the Progressive Change Campaign Committee. ''It's one thing to not show up with the necessary outrage for this kind of news; it's another not to show up with a battle plan.''\par \par \uc2 Elected Democrats have also urged Mr. Biden to do more, including declaring a public health emergency to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for all Americans; directing federal agencies to increase the accessibility of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; providing vouchers for travel and child care for people seeking access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care out of state; and using federal property to increase access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers also want to focus on the Senate confirmation process for Supreme Court nominees, arguing that the Dobbs decision exposed how broken it is. Representatives \uc2 Ted Lieu\uc2 , Democrat of California, and \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , Democrat of New York, called on Mr. Schumer to formally establish a position on whether Justices \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  lied under oath during their confirmations, misleading senators by saying that Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey were established precedent and implying that they would not support invalidating them.\par \par \uc2 ''We must call out their actions for what they were before the moment passes,'' Mr. Lieu and Ms. Ocasio-Cortez wrote in the letter, ''so that we can prevent such a mendacious denigration of our fundamental rights and the rule of law from ever happening again.''\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Lai and Sheryl Gay Stolberg contributed reporting from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Lai and Sheryl Gay Stolberg contributed reporting from Washington.\par \par \uc2 A bill by Lizzie Fletcher of Texas bars limits on travel. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNIE MULLIGAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220712ei7c0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
