\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 States That Ban  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Risk Losing Business\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexander Burns \par \uc2 1,859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade has prompted Democratic governors to pitch their states as better alternatives for business.\par \par \uc2 As a group of conservative states enacted severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions last year, Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois sent letters to a handful of corporate executives with close ties to Texas.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pritzker, a Democrat, urged executives to rethink basing their companies in ''a state that strips its residents of their dignity.'' Most workers, he wrote, did not want to live under a rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 There was no immediate response to his overture. Companies thriving in Texas' freewheeling business environment were not about to flee because of legally contested \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations that were not certain to be enforced.\par \par \uc2 Ten months later, the political and legal landscape is radically different. And a Supreme Court decision that abolished the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now threatening to reshape the lines of economic competition between conservative and liberal states.\par \par \uc2 For companies anchored in economically vibrant conservative states like Texas, Tennessee and Georgia, the rollback of women's rights is no longer a hypothetical scenario but an immediate challenge. It represents a potential disruption to the calculus that has made Republican-led Sun Belt states a draw for big companies, which have tended to embrace the reduced taxes and regulations while treating local social policy as something of a sideshow.\par \par \uc2 That bargain may have grown more difficult in states that have imposed punitive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, banning the procedure altogether or limiting it nearly to the point of elimination.\par \par \uc2 Some of the country's biggest businesses, including \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 AT&T\uc2  and the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2 , have already announced they would take steps to help employees who need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access but cannot obtain it in their home states. There have not yet been major announcements about companies canceling expansions or relocating offices out of jurisdictions where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned.\par \par \uc2 In states like Texas and Georgia, Republican lawmakers are effectively wagering that the local business environment will remain appealing enough to overcome concerns about women's rights. And to some conservative politicians, the risk of alienating business investment is a price worth paying to eradicate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The fallout of the Supreme Court's ruling could be more dangerous for states that share Texas' economic and social policies but lack its longstanding status as an economic powerhouse -- states like Arkansas and Oklahoma, which have passed some of the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country.\par \par \uc2 In places where pitched battles over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are now underway, some business leaders are urging politicians to proceed with caution.\par \par \uc2 ''An extreme response is not in the state's competitive interest,'' said Sandy Baruah, president of the Detroit Regional Chamber, an influential business association in Michigan. ''To the extent that the data shows today that young professionals care about this issue, I don't want to give young professionals a reason not to come to Michigan to work for Michigan companies.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baruah's group responded to the Supreme Court's decision with a statement imploring state officials not to do anything that could make Michigan less hospitable for business recruitment. The state's Democratic governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and Republican-controlled Legislature are currently clashing over the fate of a nearly century-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that had been voided by Roe v. Wade but never formally repealed.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baruah acknowledged that some states, like Texas, might be so well established as business havens that they could shrug off concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and talent recruitment. Of his own state, Mr. Baruah said: ''Michigan needs every advantage it can get.''\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat, gave a similar warning on Wednesday as he issued an executive order reinforcing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state. Mr. Cooper, who has vowed to veto any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban approved by the Republican legislature, said any such measure ''would have a negative effect on economic growth here in our state.''\par \par \uc2 Some Republican-controlled states that have already imposed limits on abortions, like Florida and South Carolina, are weighing whether to tighten them even further. In Florida, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , perhaps the most prominent conservative governor, has enacted a 15-week ban, but some state legislators are pushing for a so-called heartbeat bill that would block all but the earliest abortions.\par \par \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 , the nation's commerce secretary, said in an interview that the states imposing rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were all but certain to suffer economically.\par \par \uc2 ''I've spoken to C.E.O.s who are rethinking those states,'' said Ms. Raimondo, a Democrat. ''I think the cumulative effect will, over time, develop to be quite significant.''\par \par \uc2 Big companies are fighting a ''war for talent,'' Ms. Raimondo said, and particularly for female talent given that women make up a growing majority of new college graduates. A former governor of Rhode Island, Ms. Raimondo predicted that companies would find it difficult to recruit skilled workers in states where women's rights and medical services were sharply curtailed.\par \par \uc2 There is data to back up that view: A survey published this month by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found that more than 3 in 5 people with college and postgraduate degrees disapproved of the Supreme Court's decision, along with nearly 70 percent of people under 30. Sixty-two percent of women disapproved of the decision, according to the survey.\par \par \uc2 A young and educated cohort of workers has been pushing companies in recent years to be more vocal in speaking out on issues of broad social concern, including gay rights and racial justice, bringing some major corporations into conflict with the conservative-leaning states they call home.\par \par \uc2 Some liberal states are already depicting their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights policies as a business advantage, reinforcing the appeal of the wealthier and more progressive states that many businesses opt to call home in spite of their taxes.\par \par \uc2 In Massachusetts, Gov. \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2 , a Republican, suggested that companies should consider his state's ironclad \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protections as an inducement to expand there. Mr. Pritzker, a billionaire venture capitalist before entering politics, said in a recent interview that his administration was including information about Illinois's permissive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in its recruitment pitch to companies, particularly since the court's draft opinion leaked in a Politico article in early May.\par \par \uc2 As a political matter, Mr. Pritzker urged Democrats to lean into the message that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions were bad for the economy.\par \par \uc2 ''A Democratic president and vice president, Democrats more broadly, should be talking about this first as an issue of individual rights and freedom, and second as an economic issue,'' he said. ''Workers care about this.''\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time in recent years when right-wing social policies have thrown state-level business competition into turmoil. In 2016, Republican lawmakers in North Carolina triggered a multiyear business boycott by passing a law -- known as the ''bathroom bill'' -- requiring transgender people to use facilities that matched the gender they were assigned at birth. Two years earlier, the business community in Arizona mobilized in force against so-called religious freedom legislation that could have opened the door to discrimination against L.G.B.T.Q. people.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, a 2012 attempt by far-right legislators to mandate that women receive invasive ultrasounds before obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused outrage among moderate voters and corporate leaders, helping vault a pro-business Democrat into the governorship the next year. In 2011, the Mississippi business community rallied to defeat a proposed state constitutional amendment that would have granted legal rights to a fertilized egg, defying the Roe v. Wade decision that was then in place.\par \par \uc2 Yet even amid that drumbeat of controversy, a handful of big, conservative-leaning states like Texas, Florida and Georgia have defied predictions that anti-gay policies or laissez-faire gun laws would deter new investment. Indeed, Republican governors have recently grown more willing to defy big business: When \uc2 Disney\uc2  recently expressed disapproval of a Florida law limiting discussion of sexual orientation and gender identity in schools, Mr. DeSantis responded with a blistering attack.\par \par \uc2 Scott Reed, a Republican strategist who served as a top official at the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , questioned whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws would do much to undermine the economic appeal of conservative states.\par \par \uc2 ''These red states with low taxes, low regulation, investments in education and roads -- that's the real contrast with these older blue states that are kind of falling apart,'' Mr. Reed said.\par \par \uc2 There is no recent precedent, however, for a rightward lurch in state-level policy on the scale of the crackdown on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is now underway across much of the United States. The full impact of new restrictions is still uncertain in many states, and some bans are riddled with questions about how they will be enforced. A few, like Oklahoma's total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appear to be so sweeping that they could imperil other kinds of reproductive care, including certain fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, the biggest and most prosperous state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now illegal, business leaders are expressing guarded optimism that the state's economy can weather any backlash. The equipment manufacturer \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  announced in mid-June that it was relocating its headquarters from Illinois to Texas, even though the court's draft opinion had been published at that point.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Hamer, president of the \uc2 Texas Association of Business\uc2 , said the state's core appeal to big corporations was still intact.\par \par \uc2 ''The fundamentals of the Texas economy and the Texas work force remain very strong and I would expect continued strong growth well into the future,'' said Mr. Hamer, whose organization has not taken a position on the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott, a Republican, has professed no concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions deterring business investment. In an email, Renae Eze, a spokeswoman for the governor, boasted of the state's ''unmatched competitive advantages,'' including ''no corporate or personal income taxes, a predictable regulatory climate and a young, growing and skilled work force.''\par \par \uc2 Rachel Smith, the president of the Seattle Metropolitan Chamber of Commerce, said the patchwork of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would strain big companies with employees in multiple jurisdictions and clashing local laws. Ms. Smith, whose organization represents companies including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , said many recruits viewed medical care -- including top-flight reproductive medicine -- as a ''foundational'' concern in choosing their workplace.\par \par \uc2 ''Was it taken for granted in a lot of places? Sure -- I'm sure it was,'' Ms. Smith said. ''Is access to more comprehensive health care now at the top of the list for more people? I'm sure it is. The changes are causing people to be more deliberate.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. J.B. Pritzker of Illinois, a Democrat, wrote letters to executives urging them to rethink basing their companies in Texas. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Gina Raimondo, the commerce secretary, said states with \uc2  bans will suffer economically. Gov. Greg Abbott said he isn't worried. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES; ALLISON DINNER/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES) (A17) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220712ei7c0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
