\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Protesters Call on Biden to Protect  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephanie Lai and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 527 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The rally on Saturday, in which some demonstrators risked arrest, reflected activists' growing frustration with the administration.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- One day after President Biden issued an executive order designed to ensure access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication and emergency contraception, more than a thousand protesters gathered in front of the White House, with hundreds risking arrest by sitting at the building's gates, to urge Mr. Biden to do more.\par \par \uc2 Despite rainy weather and the threat of flash flooding in the area on Saturday, the protesters sat on the wet concrete wearing green bandannas that read ''bans off our bodies'' and chanting ''Disobey!'' and ''My body, my choice!''\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators lined up along the gate of the White House, spanning the length of the building. Some tied their bandannas to the gate, while several others tied themselves to it. Beau Loges, a transgender man from Vienna, Va., was one of them. He said he was raped as a child and was given \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at age 11. Now 20, Mr. Loges said he did not think he would have finished school if he had not had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''I'm here for everyone'' who cannot get one, he said.\par \par \uc2 To many activists and progressive lawmakers who had called on the White House to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last month, Mr. Biden's order on Friday fell short. And the event on Saturday reflected activists' growing frustration.\par \par \uc2 Rachel O'Leary Carmona, the executive director of the Women's March, which organized the protest, said in a statement that Mr. Biden should declare a public health emergency on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , codify \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  through executive action and expand the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , the secretary of health and human services, has been instructed by the president to identify potential actions that could protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and report back within 30 days. He has said that the administration has ''no magic bullet'' that can restore access.\par \par \uc2 Although the organizers had warned participants that they might be arrested, they disbanded peacefully by early afternoon. Still, Ms. O'Leary Carmona said the possibility of arrest was a calculated and necessary risk.\par \par \uc2 ''It's an escalated tactic for an escalated moment,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Anne Nicieza of Greenville, S.C., and Suzy Schmalbeck of Raleigh, N.C., two longtime friends, said they left at 8 a.m. on Friday and drove 10 hours so they could attend the rally. They wanted to make a difference where their message would be received, Ms. Nicieza, 27, said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Schmalbeck, 29, said she had weighed the impact of not attending.\par \par \uc2 ''If I'm not out here, that's one less body in the street,'' she said. ''That's one less person showing up for the cause.''\par \par \uc2 The protest, organized by the Women's March, began Saturday at Franklin Square in Washington and ended at the White House.; Some of the protesters tied bandannas that read ''bans off our bodies'' to the gates. The crowd left peacefully in the afternoon. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220710ei7a0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
