\par\par \uc2 THE EDITORIAL BOARD\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 How the Biden Administration Could Protect  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 1,266 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Barely two weeks have passed since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, and already chaos reigns. Several states are moving to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost completely, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights proponents are challenging those bans in court and in some cases, judges have been asked to issue injunctions that could, for a time, prevent the bans from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 Doctors across the country, including in places where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, are confused about what is and isn't allowed -- even when the life of a patient may be at stake. Patients are equally confused and panicked, and in some cases, their health is being put in danger.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, responding to harsh criticism from his own party that his administration has not done enough to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, on Friday signed an executive order to help protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. In his remarks, Mr. Biden made a plea for Americans to elect a Congress that would vote to codify the rights once guaranteed by Roe v. Wade. He is correct that it will be a long political fight to restore the constitutional right that the Supreme Court took away with its decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the Biden administration has an immediate duty to protect public health and the rights Americans still have. There are several steps the federal government can take now, with laws that are already on the books, to fortify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in states where they are legal, support doctors facing legal and logistical quandaries and help women who may need to cross state lines or secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online. The executive order signed Friday does little more than direct the health and human services secretary to look for ways to better enforce these existing laws, and report back.\par \par \uc2 The need for action is clear, from years of research on how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access affects women's health. One recent study estimated that denying abortions in the United States would lead to an increase in deaths of pregnant women by more than 20 percent overall, and by more than 30 percent for Black women -- and that's not counting the deaths that can result from unsafe abortions. While not all abortions will be denied in the new landscape, and how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills will be regulated could play an important role, the public health consequences of overturning Roe could be severe.\par \par \uc2 The most urgent step for the federal government to take is to ensure access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most common method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to preliminary data from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . In a strong public statement in defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  underlined his commitment to ''work with other arms of the federal government that seek to use their lawful authorities to protect and preserve access to reproductive care.'' He noted, in particular, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's authority over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs. States may not ban these drugs ''based on disagreement with the F.D.A.'s expert judgment,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. should follow the attorney general's lead and say, unequivocally, that the agency's approval of and regulatory decisions around prescription drugs, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, pre-empt any state-level regulations or statutes. That argument will almost certainly be challenged in court, but there is precedent to support it. When Massachusetts tried in 2014 to ban Zohydro, an F.D.A.-approved prescription painkiller, the move was struck down in a federal district court based on the F.D.A.'s pre-emptive powers.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A.'s pre-emptive authority should be acknowledged by states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as well. At least 30 states, including some Democratic-led ones, have chosen to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills more tightly than the F.D.A. does. Some require a physician to dispense the pills, even though the F.D.A. allows other qualified health professionals to do so. Others require in-person appointments, even though the F.D.A. allows telehealth consultations. The F.D.A. should insist that those strictures be removed, and that guidelines be consistent with federal ones.\par \par \uc2 The agency should also take immediate steps to loosen constraints on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that experts have long said are unnecessary. For example, the two-drug cocktail commonly used for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is approved for up to 10 weeks of pregnancy in the United States, but the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has deemed it safe for up to 12 weeks. And as reporting by The Atlantic has noted, the pills have been used safely even later than that.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, the F.D.A. requires pharmacies to obtain certification to dispense one of the two pills, a policy that isn't used in other countries and impedes access. To this end, federal officials should also support the drugmaker GenBioPro in its Mississippi lawsuit challenging other restrictions; the F.D.A.'s public support would help, as would the Justice Department's involvement in the case.\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services should also make clear, publicly, that all hospitals receiving federal funds for Medicare and Medicaid (meaning nearly all hospitals and clinics) are required to provide all F.D.A.-approved drugs and that emergency departments are bound by the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, which requires medical professionals to provide the necessary treatment in a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 Both the Medicaid agency and Health and Human Services should make clear -- again, unequivocally -- that the emergency treatment law applies to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when a pregnancy is life-threatening and that they will enforce it. (The Hyde Amendment, a legal provision that prohibits the use of federal funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also has a provision for rape, incest and medical emergencies that still applies to federally funded health centers.) Doctors faced with an ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage -- which can turn deadly if fetal tissue remains in the body for too long -- might be less afraid to act if they were reminded that inaction is still a crime, and if they knew that the federal government would support them.\par \par \uc2 Other federal agencies also have roles to play. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  can aggressively monitor and publicize the illnesses and deaths in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer available. As America's leading public health agency, the C.D.C. has an obligation not only to continue studying the harm to public health caused by limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access but also to speak publicly about the intention to do so. The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  can make clear that anyone who tries to sell fake \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications, online or anywhere else, will be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 It's true, as administration officials have said, that there are no easy solutions to the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that Americans are now engaged in; even among those who favor \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is little consensus about the best solution. It's also true that any action the administration takes may prompt litigation. But given the grave threat to public health, failing to act is not an option.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ELISA WELLS, JUSTIN SULLIVAN, HULTON ARCHIVE, SCIENCE  LIBRARY, LALOCRACIO, AND MIRAGEC, VIA GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220710ei7a00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
