\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Allows Trigger Ban On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  In Louisiana\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani and Kate Zernike \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The fast-changing status in the state is emblematic of the chaotic national landscape that has unfolded in the two weeks since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 A judge in Louisiana allowed state laws banning nearly all abortions to take effect on Friday, lifting an earlier court decision that had temporarily blocked them.\par \par \uc2 Abortions were immediately outlawed starting at conception, with an exception for a threat to the life of a pregnant woman, but with no exceptions for rape or incest. Under one Louisiana law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers face possible jail time of 10 or 15 years, depending on when the pregnancy was terminated.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is among a number of conservative states that passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions or bans in anticipation that the Supreme Court would rescind the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it did last month. The decision triggered those state bans, which quickly went into effect. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, however, sued in state courts and in some cases won temporary restraining orders to block the bans, allowing abortions to continue.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, Judge Ethel Julien of the Orleans Parish Civil District Court ruled on Friday that the court did not have the authority to extend a temporary restraining order issued on June 27 because the case should have been filed in the state's capital, Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers representing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers of Louisiana said the decision was a temporary setback. The case is expected to be transferred to a Baton Rouge court, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates plan to ask for another suspension of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban while litigation moves forward.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Louisiana had argued in the initial case that the state's trigger laws violated the State Constitution, were ''void for vagueness'' and did not provide enough specifics about banned actions -- such as what exceptions existed for medical workers trying to save a pregnant woman's life.\par \par \uc2 ''The case continues on, the work continues on,'' said Joanna Wright, one of the lead lawyers representing a group of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and a Louisiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. ''This was a decision on a technicality that had nothing to do with the merits of our case.''\par \par \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2 , attorney general of Louisiana, celebrated the end of the temporary restraining order and vowed to continue litigation in Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 ''We certainly intend to continue to defend the laws of the state and to enforce the laws,'' Mr. Landry said.\par \par \uc2 The clinic, Hope Medical Group for Women, which had continued to provide abortions after the initial court order blocked Louisiana's trigger laws from enforcement, said it would pause performing the procedure in light of Friday's decision.\par \par \uc2 Clinic staff members canceled \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointments and redirected patients to other providers, but the clinic planned to remain open to see patients for ultrasounds and ''options counseling,'' said Kathaleen Pittman, the administrator of Hope Medical Group.\par \par \uc2 The fast-changing status of Louisiana's trigger laws is emblematic of the chaotic national landscape that has unfolded in the two weeks since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Litigators for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups rolled out lawsuits challenging trigger bans in a dozen states just after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the landmark decision that since 1973 had guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Judges rejected those challenges in Ohio and Mississippi, but other cases, including in Oklahoma, continue. According to the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is litigating the suit in Louisiana, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now unavailable in nine states.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, the last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in the state -- Jackson Women's Health Organization, which brought the suit that the Supreme Court used to overturn Roe -- closed on Thursday when a legal challenge failed and the state's trigger law took effect.\par \par \uc2 ''We asked the Supreme Court to return \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaking to the people,'' said Lynn Fitch, the state's attorney general. ''Today, in Mississippi, for the first time in many years, the will of the people as expressed through their elected legislators is no longer held up in a court and will go into effect.''\par \par \uc2 An Arizona court heard oral arguments on Friday in a challenge to a state law defining life as beginning at conception. The law had been held up by the challenge soon after it passed in 2021, but clinics in the state had stopped providing abortions following the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe, out of fear that the state law would take effect immediately and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most of the legal challenges nationwide are seeking to establish that the State Constitution protects a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Louisiana, however, voters recently amended the Constitution to say that it did not guarantee that right, making the legal challenge steeper.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, La. A judge in Louisiana allowed the state's trigger laws banning nearly all abortions to take effect on Friday.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Emily Kask for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220712ei790000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
