\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden, Responding to Reversal, Signs Order on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael D. Shear and Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 1,223 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court's ruling, some of the president's supporters have wanted him to push harder to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. But details about what his new order will do remain unclear.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Under pressure to do more to respond to the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, President Biden issued an executive order on Friday designed to ensure access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication and emergency contraception while preparing for legal fights to come.\par \par \uc2 But the order is vague about how the president hopes to accomplish those goals, leaving the details largely to \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , his secretary of health and human services, who has said the administration has ''no magic bullet'' that can restore access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Biden's order stops far short of demands from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates, who have criticized him for failing to move quickly to take action after the court's decision two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters at the White House before signing the document, Mr. Biden condemned the court's decision as ''terrible, extreme and, I think, so totally wrongheaded.'' The order would help protect some access to reproductive services, he said, but the only real way to protect access was to elect more lawmakers who support those services.\par \par \uc2 ''For God's sake, there's an election in November. Vote. Vote. Vote. Vote,'' the president said, noting that the court's majority in the decision ''practically dares'' women to assert their political power to put in place laws that restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''Consider the challenge accepted, court.''\par \par \uc2 In his remarks, Mr. Biden decried news of a 10-year-old girl in Ohio who became pregnant after being raped and was forced to leave her state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Still, he all but acknowledged his powerlessness, saying that ''the fastest route available'' to restoring a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 ''It's my hope and a strong belief that women will, in fact, turn out in record numbers to reclaim the rights'' the court had taken from them, he said.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, he said, ''I'm signing this important executive order.''\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights criticized the president's order, accusing the administration of trying to find ways to increase abortions despite the court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''In obvious defiance of the Supreme Court's decision and to surrender to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists, President Biden is futilely attempting to maintain his grip on an issue that is no longer a federal one,'' said Chelsey Youman, the national legislative adviser for Human Coalition Action, which pursues anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 But advocates, such as Mini Timmaraju, the president of \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , said the executive order ''is an important first step in restoring the rights taken from millions of Americans by the Supreme Court.''\par \par \uc2 Others have urged Mr. Biden to take bolder action, even if he risks being overturned in court. They want him to declare a public health emergency, a move that they say would enable the administration to move quickly to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including by ensuring that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills can be prescribed in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal.\par \par \uc2 ''You need to be willing to take some risks -- even if the anticipation is it might not work,'' Andrea Miller, the president of the National Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said before the announcement. ''Because we are facing an immediate crisis.''\par \par \uc2 The president's order directs Mr. Becerra to develop a report ''identifying potential actions'' to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but does not say what those actions will be. Mr. Becerra will also identify ''steps'' to ensure that pregnant women have access to emergency care, it says, by ''considering updates to current guidance on obligations specific to emergency conditions and stabilizing care.''\par \par \uc2 The order does not specify what those updates will be but directs Mr. Becerra to report back to Mr. Biden in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to prepare for legal challenges from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, the order directs the attorney general and the White House counsel to ''convene a meeting of private pro bono attorneys, bar associations, and public interest organizations'' to encourage robust legal representation.\par \par \uc2 In response to concerns that those seeking abortions could be prosecuted using personal information in emails, text messages or web searches, the order seeks to safeguard patient privacy regarding data about women's health services.\par \par \uc2 It asks the chair of the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  to ''consider actions'' to protect consumers' privacy when patients seek information about reproductive care. It also directs Mr. Becerra to ''consider actions'' to protect patients' privacy.\par \par \uc2 The order pledges that the administration will ''ensure the safety of patients, providers and third parties'' who are delivering or receiving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. But it does not say how agencies will ensure that amid heightened tensions after the Supreme Court's decision.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials have said that the president's hands are largely tied in part because of the Hyde Amendment, a provision in federal law precluding the government from paying for abortions except in cases of rape or incest or to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Becerra has said his agency will work with the Justice Department to ensure women have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- two drugs taken 24 to 28 hours apart and authorized for use during the first weeks of pregnancy -- in places where state law conflicts with the judgment of the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , which approved the drugs for use and determined that they are safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 Jen Klein, the executive director of the White House Gender Policy Council, which led the effort to draft the executive order, said Friday that the details are yet to be worked out by Mr. Becerra and others.\par \par \uc2 ''So what the executive order asked the secretary to do is come back within 30 days with a plan,'' she told reporters. ''So part of this is, as you are noting, are things the president has talked about and what the secretary has already started to work on. And what this asks for is a very deliberate plan and process.''\par \par \uc2 But the president's attempt to shift responsibility has not gone over well among some of Mr. Biden's core supporters, who have repeatedly called on the White House to push to find novel ways around the court's decision and the subsequent limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the ruling automatically imposed in more than a dozen states with Republican-led state legislatures and Republican governors.\par \par \uc2 Some activists and lawmakers, for example, have urged the president to establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services on federal land or on Native American territories, where state laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services might not be enforceable. The White House has dismissed those ideas as legally unworkable and potentially more dangerous for women seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden signed the executive order on Friday before leaving for a weekend at his beach house in Delaware. The fact sheet did not include any of the more far-reaching suggestions about using federal resources to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 President Joe Biden condemned the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade as ''terrible'' before he signed the executive order on Friday at the White House. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM BRENNER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220709ei790003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
