\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Need to Scare Voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Marie Cox \par \uc2 1,308 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the Democrats need to lean into the politics of fear.\par \par \uc2 They face a base that feels betrayed and a set of wealthy, moderate voters in purple states who may not realize that their own rights are also on the line. Democrats need both of these groups to stave off defeat in the fall, and fear can drive them to the polls. What should the Democrats tell them to be afraid of? A national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 America after the fall of Roe v. Wade might feel as if we're living in the worst-case scenario, but anyone who values reproductive freedom has reason to panic about what could happen if Republicans take back power in Washington. G.O.P. Congress members have already introduced bills that would criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in various ways. They are only more emboldened now.\par \par \uc2 Last month, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  called for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. More to the point, Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the minority leader, has called it ''possible.'' Recently, he hedged the prediction by stating an obvious fact: ''Neither side on this issue has had 60'' votes to pass such a ban. Is that a phrase you feel comfortable hanging your future on?\par \par \uc2 Plenty of progressive voters don't need to be made aware of the danger. They are already terrified for the future. But everyone should be, not just those who might want or need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future. The conservative legal theories (and the conservative jurists) that brought down Roe threaten marriage equality, privacy in the bedroom, even contraception. A law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by recognizing fetuses as humans with constitutional rights could criminalize in vitro fertilization. The life-or-death pregnancy scenarios now playing out in red states could be repeated right next door, no matter where you live.\par \par \uc2 To meet the urgency of the moment and save their razor-thin and often nonexistent hold on the Senate, Democrats must talk about that future, giving voters across the country, in every state, a reason to vote. Lives are on the line. At the same time, Democratic leaders have to understand that the politics of fear can run both ways.\par \par \uc2 The party needs to scare voters and show that they, too, are scared: scared of the voters themselves. Democratic politicians watched Republicans roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for decades -- and when Roe fell, they had no plan. Now, they need to demonstrate that they are willing to put themselves at the mercy of those they failed -- making specific promises and letting the voters know that if they fail again, it will be more than a fund-raising opportunity. It will be a reckoning.\par \par \uc2 Politicians have never shied away from making campaign promises, but the Republicans are better at creating the circumstances for voters to exact revenge. \uc2 Grover Norquist\uc2 's Taxpayer Protection Pledge held sway over hundreds of Republican candidates for over a decade. For the G.O.P., the pledge was a neat way to synchronize the interests of the very rich and the defiant antigovernment streak of the white middle class, and the key to making it work, for as long as it has, has been simple: letting voters know they expect to be held accountable. If elected officials buck the tax pledge, voters have a specific reason to send them home, and the politicians have no excuses.\par \par \uc2 A promise to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would not have the same kind of moneyed network behind it that the tax pledge does. But it would carry the same emotional and political weight. In the end, all politicians have to answer for their records.\par \par \uc2 Say that Democrats committed, for once, to prioritizing action over whatever solution Washington considers reasonable. Call it the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Pledge and turn running against the threat of a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban into a chance to run for something, too.\par \par \uc2 Don't quibble, don't be distracted by debates over whether or when a ban will happen. Instead, decide on a handful of action items around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and get everyone who wants a vote or a dollar to say it out loud: ''I will support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in these ways and if I don't follow through, you should kick me out.''\par \par \uc2 I am honestly unsure if it matters what those action items are; I do know Democrats will have to throw out any concern for the appearance of moderation. Right now, all the ideas about bridging the gap to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access sound extreme. But so did the tax pledge at one point. So did overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Take allowing abortions on federal land. Biden could declare the policy so. Candidates would only have to pledge to support it. Yes, the policy would invoke an avalanche of untested legal theories and complicated jurisdictional questions. But Democrats who want to save the lives of those in need of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't fall back on ''it's complicated'' as an excuse to not even try.\par \par \uc2 If you want something less complicated -- something that would also help roll out abortions on federal lands -- make a pledge not to vote for any appropriation bill that carries the Hyde Amendment, which bans federal funding for most abortions. On its own, abolishing the Hyde Amendment would not greatly expand access outside states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. But combined with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on federal property, the government could act even more directly to help those seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Stonewalling Hyde-burdened budgets could lead to a government shutdown, but if you think that ruins a party's reputation forever, well, you are probably a current Democratic office holder.\par \par \uc2 Embracing a politics of fear on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  unites two of the constituencies the Democrats need to edge out the G.O.P. in key narrow races (Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, Georgia). First, hammering home the danger of a national ban may sufficiently alarm moderate voters in the suburbs, convincing them to abandon Republicans. Second, addressing the widespread sense of betrayal among progressive voters will help keep them activated. The threat of a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is also a national message. Democrats can make it clear that the party can't risk a single loss, no matter how lopsided the polls are. And then there is the simple truth underpinning this entire strategy: Protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is popular.\par \par \uc2 This plan -- where Democratic leaders take real political risks -- is untried. But it's worth the gamble. Bold action is the only way to assure voters that they are prepared to do whatever it takes when other rights are on the chopping block. Most significantly, prioritizing responsiveness to voters would reset the entire framework of Democratic campaigns.\par \par \uc2 The only way this strategy could truly backfire is if Democrats run on a promise and then break it, again. And expect to be re-elected, again. Of course, that is what Democrats are doing right now. ''Protect Roe'' was their rallying cry; it turns out that's all it was.\par \par \uc2 Fear often divides, but it can also unite. When you have a common threat, there is an opportunity for a common mission. This threat is no longer beyond the horizon -- it's at the door. Now, Democrats, decide on a mission.\par \par \uc2 Ana Marie Cox (@anamariecox) was the founding editor of the political blog Wonkette. She is the author of ''Dog Days,'' a novel, and a forthcoming memoir.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Pete Gamlen FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220709ei7900032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
