\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 South Carolina Weighs a Stricter  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Fausset and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,173 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state legislature was the first to step into a post-Roe world, clearing the path for full bans in other conservative states.\par \par \uc2 COLUMBIA, S.C. -- Nearly two weeks after the Supreme Court overturned the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established by Roe v. Wade, South Carolina lawmakers became the first to consider more restrictive legislation.\par \par \uc2 The state had already decided to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy. But on Thursday its legislature, now empowered to consider even greater restrictions, offered the first glimpse of a state taking early steps into a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 Many conservative states had passed tight restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including outright bans -- in anticipation of the Supreme Court decision. But now more are poised to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans at conception, limits on out-of-state abortions and shutting down access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 The fall of Roe started a frenzy of legal and legislative activity across the nation, with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups lobbying for a federal ban, and the most strident edge of the movement pushing to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  homicide and prosecute women who have the procedure.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are suing to stop existing state bans from being enforced and fighting to strengthen state laws that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois has said he plans to call a special session to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Special legislative sessions meant to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected in Nebraska and Indiana. In Kansas, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed up to 22 weeks of pregnancy, voters will go to the polls next month to decide whether to amend the State Constitution to say it does not include a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republicans in firm control of South Carolina's legislature and executive branch, a new proposal offering something closer to an outright ban seems quite likely, although the details have yet to be hammered out. On Thursday, the exploration of a new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law began with a meeting of a 14-member, Republican-dominated House committee. But a bill introduced in the House has been left intentionally vague, with just a few lines of text -- including a statement that state law would be changed ''so as to prohibit abortions in the state of South Carolina.''\par \par \uc2 Courtney Milbank, an Indiana lawyer, urged the committee to adopt model legislation she had helped draft for the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 . The draft legislation bans all abortions except to prevent the death of the pregnant woman, and calls for criminal prosecution of anyone ''conspiring to cause, or aiding or abetting, illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' including anyone who takes a minor to another state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The legislation would not punish women in civil or criminal court.\par \par \uc2 A bill to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  already introduced in the South Carolina Senate contains similar provisions.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats say Republican leaders may not be willing to push too far past public opinion, which shows overall support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with some limits.\par \par \uc2 The first-term governor of South Dakota, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , has twice announced plans to call a special session of the State Legislature. South Dakota already has a complete ban on abortions with no exceptions for rape or incest. But a special session could allow Republican lawmakers to impose additional restrictions, like attempting to prohibit residents from traveling out of state to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet Ms. Noem, a Republican who is facing re-election in November, has not called a special session.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say the governor is worried that a session to strengthen the state's already sweeping ban could reveal that public opinion is divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A representative for Ms. Noem said the special session was not ''in the immediate future.''\par \par \uc2 ''I think that the governor is realizing she is out of step with the people of South Dakota,'' State Senator Reynold Nesiba, a Democrat, said.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota voters rejected two ballot initiatives, one in 2006 and another in 2008, that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  long before Roe v. Wade seemed vulnerable to reversal. An effort is underway for a new ballot initiative that would enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , a Republican who is up for re-election in November, has said he welcomed the House committee's work on a new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill. But he may face a difficult political decision if he is presented with a bill that does not include exceptions for rape and incest, which are in the current law.\par \par \uc2 In late June, he told reporters, ''I look forward to the day that we don't have any abortions in South Carolina, and we won't need exceptions.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday at the South Carolina State House, Jenny Black, the president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, criticized the current six-week ban, saying that at that point, ''most people don't even know they are pregnant yet, and it is often logistically impossible for many to arrange for safe legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in time.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Black said Planned Parenthood's two South Carolina clinics, in Columbia and Charleston, continued to offer abortions within the scope of the new law. (An independent clinic in Greenville is also continuing to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, a worker there said on Thursday.)\par \par \uc2 Committee members on Thursday gave little indication of what kinds of new restrictions they might adopt, giving over the bulk of the hearing to residents who wished to speak on the topic. Some quoted Scripture. Some spoke of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a woman's right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 Former U.S. Representative \uc2 Joe Cunningham\uc2 , the Democratic nominee for governor, told the committee that it was robbing women of ''the right to decide what happens to their very own bodies.''\par \par \uc2 Craig Scott, a pastor from Abbeville, spoke in favor of greater restrictions. He told the committee that he had been diagnosed, in the womb, with birth defects, and that doctors had told his parents he would be a ''vegetable'' after birth. Mr. Scott noted that he had graduated from college and currently leads a church.\par \par \uc2 ''All I'm asking today is to give these children a chance -- a chance to flourish, the chance to succeed and a chance to live,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina on Wednesday, Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat, signed an executive order instructing state agencies to ''protect people or entities who are providing, assisting, seeking or obtaining lawful \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care services in North Carolina.'' The order recognized that North Carolina has begun attracting people from nearby Southern states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been restricted or banned.\par \par \uc2 ''Your ZIP code should not determine your rights,'' Mr. Cooper said in a news conference. ''But because of the Supreme Court's outrageous decision, that's the reality right now.''\par \par \uc2  rights and anti-\uc2  demonstrators in Columbia, S.C., on Thursday, as state legislators consider an outright ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SEAN RAYFORD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220708ei780004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
