\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Louisiana,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinics Stay Open on Borrowed Time\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Kask \par \uc2 432 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Abortions were expected to cease in Louisiana when the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade on June 24, but a state judge gave clinics a reprieve.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana's ''trigger law,'' similar to those in a dozen other states, was temporarily delayed in taking effect. For now, the Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport is continuing to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, 40 women had appointments for abortions at the clinic.\par \par \uc2 The phones rang constantly with potential patients from Louisiana, Texas and Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruling left patients and staff uncertain about what the future might look like. ''Some women had been waiting weeks for their appointment,'' said Kathaleen Pittman, the clinic's administrator. ''A lot canceled because they were afraid they would be canceled anyway.''\par \par \uc2 For now, the clinic is trying to accommodate as many patients as possible.\par \par \uc2 A protester stood outside the clinic on Tuesday, trying to dissuade women from receiving abortions.\par \par \uc2 Clinic escorts ushered patients from their cars to the door to ensure their safety and privacy.\par \par \uc2 A woman sat in the waiting room while her 17-year-old daughter received an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They had traveled more than 300 miles from the Houston area.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the only choice for us,'' her mother said. ''If we couldn't have gotten in here, I had heard of some person I could have bought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill off of.''\par \par \uc2 If the Louisiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is allowed to take effect, the clinic will be forced to shut down. ''I don't know what I'm going to do next,'' Ms. Pittman said. ''I never imagined I'd have to be starting over at 65.''\par \par \uc2 The judge will hold a hearing on July 8. If he allows the law to go forward, it will ban anyone from performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or providing drugs to interrupt a pregnancy. It will allow exceptions only to prevent serious injury or death of a pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 If Louisiana's clinics shut down, women in the state who need abortions might have to travel as far as Illinois or New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 If the Louisiana \uc2  ban is allowed to take effect, the Hope clinic will be forced to close. ''I don't know what I'm going to do next,'' Kathaleen Pittman, below, its administrator, said.; The Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, above and bottom right, is continuing to offer \uc2  services under a state judge's reprieve. A protester outside the clinic last month. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY EMILY KASK) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220707ei7700050\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
