\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As U.S. Clamps Down, Rules Ease Elsewhere\b0\par\par\uc2 By The New York Times \par \uc2 1,783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly led to bans in at least eight states, a shift toward criminalization that runs counter to recent easings in countries that had longstanding bans.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's elimination of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after nearly a half-century, has made the United States one of the few countries actively strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned in at least eight states, a shift toward criminalization that runs counter to the longstanding policies of some close allies, like Canada, and to recent easings in several nations that had long imposed bans, like Ireland, Mexico and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 But no nations share the same history regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , nor does any part of the world have uniform laws: Women seeking abortions everywhere must navigate distinct rules, in a variety of health care systems, if access is available at all. The following examples, while not comprehensive, illustrate the diversity of those laws -- and how they're changing.\par \par \uc2 Canada\par \par \uc2 No laws restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Canada, where it is covered by provincial and territorial public health care systems as an essential medical procedure within 20 weeks of conception and, under some circumstances, after that point, such as when a pregnancy threatens the mother's life. Access and exceptions vary by province, and sometimes by hospital.\par \par \uc2 Until 1988, criminal laws allowed abortions only if approved by committees of physicians. That year, the Supreme Court struck down the laws in a landmark case. Most legal scholars agree that if the issue were to reach the court again, it would make the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  explicit.\par \par \uc2 -- Ian Austen\par \par \uc2 Mexico\par \par \uc2 Before a court ruling last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was largely restricted, with Mexico City and only three of 31 states permitting the procedure up to 12 weeks of fetal gestation. But the Supreme Court ruled unanimously in the fall that penalizing women who undergo abortions was unconstitutional, and in the months since, five more states have moved to legalize abortions.\par \par \uc2 The justices did not specify how far into a pregnancy \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was permitted, leaving the details to the states. For the states that still ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , legislatures will need to change laws to permit the procedure.\par \par \uc2 -- Maria Abi-Habib\par \par \uc2 Nicaragua\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is completely illegal and punishable by jail for the woman and the doctors.\par \par \uc2 The Legislature eliminated all exceptions in 2006, ending a century of law that allowed abortions in cases of life-threatening complications or pregnancies caused by rape. President Daniel Ortega, a strong supporter of criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has received support from Evangelical leaders in Nicaragua and the United States.\par \par \uc2 -- Yubelka Mendoza and Maria Abi-Habib\par \par \uc2 Britain\par \par \uc2 Abortions have been legal in England, Scotland and Wales for more than 50 years, protected by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act of 1967. Abortions can be legally performed up to the 24th week of pregnancy and must be medically approved by two doctors.\par \par \uc2 The 1967 law allows some exceptions for later-term abortions, including when the pregnancy endangers the woman's health or if a prenatal scan reveals a fetus abnormality. A provision of the law allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the fetus carries significant risk of serious disability was at the center of a court case last year.\par \par \uc2 In rare circumstances, such as when a woman, without doctors' consent, takes medicine intended to terminate a pregnancy, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be prosecuted as a criminal act.\par \par \uc2 The 1967 law did not cover Northern Ireland, which for decades prohibited almost all abortions.\par \par \uc2 British lawmakers overturned that ban in 2019, legalizing ''unconditional termination'' of pregnancy within the first 12 weeks. But with resistance coming from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and church groups, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services remain limited.\par \par \uc2 -- Saskia Solomon\par \par \uc2 Ireland\par \par \uc2 A 1983 constitutional amendment banned nearly all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reflecting the Roman Catholic Church's deep influence in the country. That influence had waned by 2018, when a referendum to end the ban was approved by 66 percent of voters.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers then legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Health Act of 2018, allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason up to the end of the first trimester. The law provides exceptions beyond 12 weeks in cases of fetal abnormalities considered fatal after birth or a potential risk to the woman's health.\par \par \uc2 -- Saskia Solomon\par \par \uc2 Poland\par \par \uc2 Soviet-era Poland offered some of Europe's broadest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and it became a destination for women seeking abortions. But after the Soviet Union's collapse, and under the influence of the Catholic Church, the Polish Parliament in 1993 passed one of Europe's strictest bans, asserting that ''every human being shall have an inherent right to life from the moment of conception.''\par \par \uc2 It allowed three categories of exception: danger to the mother's health or life; rape or incest; severe fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 Despite mass protests, the ban was tightened last year by the nationalist Law and Justice Party, eliminating the most-used exception -- fetal abnormalities -- which accounted for almost all of the roughly 1,000 legal abortions a year. An estimated 100,000 to 150,000 illegal abortions take place every year in the country, activists say.\par \par \uc2 The remaining exceptions are problematic for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers. Rape victims face a deadline of the 12th week of pregnancy, and they require a certificate from a prosecutor, which takes a long time to acquire. And the definition of what constitutes a ''serious'' risk to a woman's health is too vague for doctors to always act decisively. In a small number of cases, women have died of sepsis after doctors refused to intervene while the fetus's heart was still beating.\par \par \uc2 Women cannot be punished for taking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill or undergoing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abroad.\par \par \uc2 Anyone deemed to have aided or abetted an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces up to eight years in prison.\par \par \uc2 -- Katrin Bennhold\par \par \uc2 India\par \par \uc2 A total ban was eased in 1971 with the Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act, which made some abortions legal.\par \par \uc2 But activists continued to seek further easing, to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of fetal anomaly or pregnancy caused by rape. In 2021, the federal government amended the law, expanding the criteria for legal abortions and adding a privacy clause to protect women who went to clinics.\par \par \uc2 The law allows women to terminate pregnancies until 20 weeks. Between 20 and 24 weeks, a woman needs two doctors to approve an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and after 24 weeks, abortions are allowed only when the woman's health is at risk. Women can now terminate unwanted pregnancies caused by contraceptive failure regardless of marital status; before the amendment, only a married couple could do that.\par \par \uc2 Still, abortions done in violation of the law are punishable by up to seven years in prison for the woman and medical personnel.\par \par \uc2 -- Sameer Yasir\par \par \uc2 China\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been legal in some form since 1953. By the 1970s, as the ruling Chinese Communist Party grew increasingly worried about overpopulation, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became more widely accessible, and the one-child policy led to some forced abortions.\par \par \uc2 Sex-selective abortions are illegal, meant to counter the widespread preference for boys over girls. In response to recent concerns over declining birthrates and an aging population, families may now have three children without penalty. Given the government's invasive family planning policies, some women fear it will restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. The authorities last year said they intended to reduce ''medically unnecessary abortions,'' without explaining how.\par \par \uc2 Access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services varies by region, with some requiring women to produce certificates of medical necessity. In Jiangxi Province, women who are more than 14 weeks pregnant need three signatures from medical personnel.\par \par \uc2 Scholars, activists and some foreign governments have accused the authorities of using family planning policies to suppress ethnic minorities, which the government denies.\par \par \uc2 -- Vivian Wang\par \par \uc2 Kenya\par \par \uc2 Under the 2010 Constitution, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted if a trained health professional determines a need for emergency treatment, or if the pregnancy endangers the life or health of the mother. In other circumstances, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers can face up to 14 years in prison under Kenya's penal code.\par \par \uc2 In practice, many women who could obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot because of poverty, lack of access to health services or a lack of information about their rights.\par \par \uc2 -- Matthew Mpoke Bigg\par \par \uc2 Benin\par \par \uc2 Last fall, Benin joined South Africa and Mozambique as one of the few African countries to broadly legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Under the new law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be allowed ''when the pregnancy is likely to aggravate or cause a situation of material, educational, professional or moral distress incompatible with the interest of the woman and/or the unborn child.'' In doing so, Benin became the third country in Africa, along with Ethiopia and Zambia, to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on the social or economic needs of the woman.\par \par \uc2 The previous law authorized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if the pregnancy endangered the woman's life or was the result of rape or incest, or if the fetus was malformed. The new law will take effect after the authorities detail how it will be applied, which could take months.\par \par \uc2 -- Elian Peltier\par \par \uc2 Egypt\par \par \uc2 A strict anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law has been on the books for 85 years, derived from the French Penal Code of the colonial era that bans the procedure under nearly any circumstances. The woman and doctor face imprisonment if convicted.\par \par \uc2 It does have one loophole, which is based on the medical code of ethics: Doctors are allowed -- but not legally obligated -- to terminate a pregnancy if it puts the woman's life at risk and she signs a document saying the procedure was lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 Though convictions are uncommon, the law and social stigma have pushed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices out of public sight, where the woman's safety cannot be protected and the procedure can be prohibitively expensive.\par \par \uc2 -- Nada Rashwan\par \par \uc2 Turkey\par \par \uc2 Since 1983, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been legal up to 10 weeks after conception. Married women need spousal consent. The law allows exceptions after 10 weeks when the pregnancy threatens the life of the woman, if the doctor concludes the fetus has a grave disability, and in cases of rape.\par \par \uc2 Violators face prosecution and prison -- for both the woman and the doctor\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a divisive issue. In 2012, Recep Tayyip Erdogan, now the president and then prime minister, called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  murder and urged more restrictions. After popular outrage, the law was never amended, but in practice, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services are unavailable in many state hospitals.\par \par \uc2 -- Safak Timur\par \par \uc2 Activists clashing outside the U.S. Supreme Court on June 27, days after it eliminated the constitutional right to \uc2 . The ruling contrasted with recent decisions by high courts in Mexico and Colombia, which have moved to decriminalize \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY Shuran Huang for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220706ei7600030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn