\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Behind the Scenes of the Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Leonhardt \par \uc2 1,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Getting a sense of the mood after a momentous term.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks ago, I asked Adam Liptak -- The Times's Supreme Court correspondent -- to preview the major cases that would make up the end of the court's term. Adam was prophetic, correctly forecasting every big ruling. Today, he returns to the newsletter, answering my questions about the behind-the-scenes atmosphere at the court.\par \par \uc2 David: The last few months have been among the most unusual in the Court's modern history -- a major leak followed by an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision that, as you've written , will change American life in major ways. Inside the court, do you think things also feel different?\par \par \uc2 Adam: The Supreme Court's building has been closed to the public since the beginning of the pandemic. Then, not long after the leak in early May of a draft of the opinion that overruled Roe v. Wade, the courthouse was surrounded by an eight-foot fence. Always cloistered and remote, the court is now impenetrable.\par \par \uc2 The release of the decision in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case highlighted another way in which the court has withdrawn from public scrutiny. For unexplained reasons, the justices have stopped announcing their decisions from the bench, abandoning a tradition that is both ceremonial and illuminating. In the old days, the author of the majority opinion would give a quick and conversational summary of the ruling that could be extremely valuable for a reporter on deadline and, by extension, for members of the public trying to understand a decision.\par \par \uc2 More important yet were oral dissents, reserved for decisions that the justices in the minority believed were profoundly mistaken. In ordinary times, one or more of the three liberal justices who dissented in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case would have raised their voices in protest. These days, the court makes do with posting PDFs of its decisions, robbing the occasion of ceremony, drama and insight.\par \par \uc2 So the lawyers who argued the cases and the reporters covering the court find out about decisions the same way everybody else does -- by refreshing their browsers. But the justices have returned to the courtroom for arguments, haven't they?\par \par \uc2 Yes, they have taken a different approach with arguments. After hearing them by telephone for much of the pandemic, the justices returned to the bench in October. Reporters with Supreme Court press credentials were allowed to attend and the public could listen to live-streamed audio on the court's website. It is not clear why opinions could not be announced in similar fashion.\par \par \uc2 I haven't been to the courthouse since the last argument of the current term, on April 27, when Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  grew emotional in saying farewell to a retiring colleague, Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . But there is every reason to think that the leak, the investigation it prompted, the controversy over Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's failure to recuse himself from a case that intersected with his wife's efforts to overturn the election and the justices' very real security concerns have made the court an unhappy place.\par \par \uc2 In remarks in May, not long after the leak, Justice Thomas reflected on how things had changed at the court since an 11-year stretch without changes in its membership before the arrival of Chief Justice Roberts in 2005. ''This is not the court of that era,'' Justice Thomas said, adding: ''We actually trusted each other. We may have been a dysfunctional family, but we were a family.''\par \par \uc2 A less collegial court seems like it could be especially problematic for the three liberal justices. There are now five Republican-appointed justices who are even more conservative than Roberts . If the court is a less collaborative place, I would imagine it gives the justices in the minority -- both the liberals and, in some cases, Roberts -- less ability to shape decisions.\par \par \uc2 Yes, though it's possible to overstate the power of collegiality. Justices cast votes based on the strength of the relevant arguments and the desired outcomes, not on how likable their colleagues are.\par \par \uc2 The justices say there is no vote-trading across cases, and I believe them. On the other hand, there are certainly negotiations within cases. It seems tolerably clear, for instance, that Justices Breyer and Elena Kagan shifted positions in one part of the 2012 case that upheld a key portion of the Affordable Care Act to make certain they would secure Chief Justice Roberts's vote on another part.\par \par \uc2 Justices may well be prepared to narrow or reshape a draft opinion that seeks to speak for a five-justice majority in exchange for a vote. But once the author has gotten to five, the value of another potential vote plummets. It is that dynamic that must worry the court's liberals.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Justice Breyer officially retired and helped swear in his replacement, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . How do the justices typically welcome a new member?\par \par \uc2 When a new justice joins the Supreme Court, tradition requires the second-most junior justice to arrange a little party. In 2006, for instance, when Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  came on board, that task fell to Justice Breyer, who knew his new colleague to be a \uc2 Phillies\uc2  fan. Before dessert was served, Justice Breyer introduced a special guest: the Phillie Phanatic, the team's mascot.\par \par \uc2 This year, Justice Amy Coney Barrett is the second-most junior justice and will presumably be in charge of the welcoming celebration for Justice Jackson.\par \par \uc2 And now that the court is on a break until October, what do the justices usually do?\par \par \uc2 They often teach courses in exotic places. In 2012, for instance, after voting to uphold the Affordable Care Act, Chief Justice Roberts left for Malta to teach a two-week class on the history of the Supreme Court. ''Malta, as you know, is an impregnable island fortress,'' he said. ''It seemed like a good idea.''\par \par \uc2 More about Adam Liptak: He started his Times career as a copy boy in 1984, fetching coffee for editors and occasionally writing. After law school and a stint at a Wall Street law firm, he returned to the paper in 1992, joining its corporate legal department before moving to the newsroom as a reporter a decade later. He reads a lot and plays a lot of poker.\par \par \uc2 More on the court\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's latest term was its most conservative since 1931, as these graphics by Alicia Parlapiano demonstrate.\par \par \uc2 Dysfunction in Congress has given the court inordinate power, Adam writes.\par \par \uc2 NEWS\par \par \uc2 The Latest\par \par \uc2 Russia claimed to have seized Lysychansk, a prize city in Ukraine's east, and blamed Ukraine for explosions that rocked a Russian border town. Here's the latest.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian men volunteered to protect their homes. Now, many of these untrained soldiers are dying on the other side of the country.\par \par \uc2 For months, Russia has pummeled Ukrainian civilians -- and offered excuses to dodge responsibility.\par \par \uc2 The investigation into Russian war crimes, by Ukrainian and international agencies, may be the largest in history.\par \par \uc2 The rising price of fuel is hitting poorer countries especially hard, with many residents struggling to keep the lights on or cook food.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 The school police chief in Uvalde, Texas, resigned from the city council amid continued outrage over officers' slow response to the school shooting.\par \par \uc2 A cohort of young women -- many of them Christian, some of them progressive -- are fighting against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Holiday headache: Thousands of flights were delayed as travel reaches prepandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 Tropical Storm Colin is bringing heavy rain to the Carolinas.\par \par \uc2 FROM OPINION\par \par \uc2 Restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access will hurt rich women, too, says Elizabeth Spiers.\par \par \uc2 The far right and far left both think women don't count, Pamela Paul argues.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Renkl fears for American democracy. She's reclaiming the flag anyway.\par \par \uc2 The Sunday question: Is Roe's fall transforming the midterms?\par \par \uc2 Commentary's Noah Rothman has doubts, arguing that crime and inflation remain voters' top concerns. CNN's Harry Enten thinks the ruling could lift Democrats in state-level races, whose winners will shape whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Mango man: After 82 years, his days are spent taking care of the tree he loves.\par \par \uc2 Other worlds: The powerful new James Webb Space Telescope will search for signs of life in the universe.\par \par \uc2 Frozen: A baby woolly mammoth, preserved in the ground for more than 30,000 years.\par \par \uc2 Culture wars: Solveig Gold is proud to be the wife of a ''canceled'' Princeton professor.\par \par \uc2 Sunday routine: Anthony Almojera, a paramedic, cooks a family meal at Station 40 in Sunset Park, Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Advice from Wirecutter: The best beach gear.\par \par \uc2 A Times classic: The un-divorced.\par \par \uc2 BOOKS\par \par \uc2 Fiction in the city: Contemporary authors shared their favorite New York City novels.\par \par \uc2 By the Book: Alice Elliott Dark ruins books by reading them in the bathtub.\par \par \uc2 Our editors' picks: The history of the bicycle, and nine other new books.\par \par \uc2 Times best sellers: Riley Sager's ''The House Across the Lake'' settles in this week on our hardcover fiction best-seller list. See all our lists.\par \par \uc2 The Book Review podcast: Gabrielle Zevin discusses her new novel, ''Tomorrow, and Tomorrow, and Tomorrow,'' about two friends who design a video game.\par \par \uc2 THE SUNDAY TIMES MAGAZINE\par \par \uc2 On the cover: Moderate Democrats are going extinct.\par \par \uc2 Rituals: How do you prepare for a school shooting?\par \par \uc2 Recommendation: The \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account @b_a_l_l_h_a_u_s will change how you see basketball.\par \par \uc2 Diagnosis: He could barely walk and had to give up golf. What was wrong?\par \par \uc2 Eat: In Hawaii, mac salad isn't just a side dish; it's a condiment unto itself.\par \par \uc2 Read the full issue.\par \par \uc2 THE WEEK AHEAD\par \par \uc2 What to Watch For\par \par \uc2 A law banning nearly all abortions in Mississippi is set to take effect on Thursday. Here's our tracker of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws across the country.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will award the Presidential Medal of Freedom to 17 people on Thursday, including Simone Biles, Denzel Washington and \uc2 John McCain\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Much of the country is in for a hot and humid next couple days.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow Independence Day is celebrated in the U.S., and millions of Americans will be traveling. Expect delays.\par \par \uc2 What to Cook This Week\par \par \uc2 If you've had your fill of burgers and hot dogs, Emily Weinstein recommends Pati Jinich's Sonoran carne asada tacos.\par \par \uc2 NOW TIME TO PLAY\par \par \uc2 Here's a clue from the Sunday crossword:\par \par \uc2 113 Across: Space heater\par \par \uc2 Take the news quiz to see how well you followed the week's headlines.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Spelling Bee. Here's today's Wordle. After, use our bot to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your weekend with The Times. -- David\par \par \uc2 Claire Moses, Ian Prasad Philbrick, Tom Wright-Piersanti and Ashley Wu contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Protests ensued after the court limited the government's ability to regulate power plant emissions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNA ROSE LAYDEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220705ei7500031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
