\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Voices of Men Affected by  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alisha Haridasani Gupta \par \uc2 2,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In light of the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, advocates from all sides of the issue have called for men to be part of the conversation. The Times heard from hundreds who wanted to share their stories.\par \par \uc2 To hear more audio stories from publications like \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , download Audm for iPhone or Android .\par \par \uc2 When Quenton Albertie, 29, found out his college girlfriend was pregnant, he was surprised at first -- and then elated.\par \par \uc2 He was 23, she was 19 and they had been dating for roughly five months while attending \uc2 Mercer University\uc2  in Georgia. ''I called my mom the next day to tell her and she was excited, too,'' Mr. Albertie said. ''She always goes on about how she wants a grandchild.''\par \par \uc2 But two months later, his girlfriend got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without telling him. She informed him after the fact, saying she couldn't manage school and the pregnancy at the same time, Mr. Albertie recalled.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Albertie, who considers himself in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, understood her perspective, but was torn because he had been looking forward to becoming a father -- and thought his opinion should have carried some weight. However, he said, he recognized that ''you can't tell anybody what to do with their own bodies.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Albertie's internal conflict is representative of a broader question about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America: What place do men's opinions and feelings have in the matter?\par \par \uc2 Brian Nguyen, an assistant professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  and the founder of Emerge Lab, a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research organization that seeks to engage men in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights conversation, said that men have been minimized, if not completely overlooked, in the conversation. That fosters ''a societal expectation that they shouldn't speak about it even among their closest network of friends and family,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Men who are opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't feel heard either, said \uc2 Bradley Mattes\uc2 , the president of Life Issues Institute, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization. ''Men are often told, 'It's my body, my choice. Be supportive or get out of the way.' That needs to change.''\par \par \uc2 The fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies have been largely dictated by men in positions of power has created a perception that men are speaking out -- and that men and women have diametrically opposed views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In reality, public opinion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more likely split along party lines than gender.\par \par \uc2 An estimated one in five men in the United States have been involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (meaning their partner's pregnancy ended in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), according to a recent analysis of data between 2015 and 2017 from the National Survey of Family Growth. ''Men really need to consider what losing access to safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  means for them,'' said Joe Colon-Uvalles, an organizer at the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the procedure is already restricted or illegal in over 20 states. In light of the shifting landscape, The Times asked men who have grappled with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their own lives to share their stories. Hundreds of respondents revealed a range of emotional reactions, including fear and frustration, happiness and hopelessness.\par \par \uc2 The Reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Some men said they viewed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an abstract philosophical concept until they came face to face with a woman who was thinking about having one.\par \par \uc2 Paul Noble, 57, a retired high school teacher in Illinois, grew up in a ''very white, very Catholic'' community in the suburbs of Chicago. He said he learned from those around him that he should be ''pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 His perspective changed during his sophomore year of college in Wisconsin. He was a resident assistant in his dorm at the time, and a young woman came to him for advice. ''She sat down and immediately started to cry. She said, 'I don't know what to do. I'm pregnant,''' Mr. Noble said. She explained to him that she had broken up with her boyfriend and that having a baby was not an option.\par \par \uc2 ''I was just kind of agog listening to her talk,'' Mr. Noble said. ''This feeling washed over me -- I don't know if it was shame or humility -- and I remember thinking to myself: 'Why did I think I had a right to have an opinion on this subject?'''\par \par \uc2 Two days later, Mr. Noble borrowed a friend's car and drove a few hours to bring her to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Rockford, Ill. There were protesters outside the entrance, he said, so he put his arm around her and helped push past them.\par \par \uc2 A Romantic Idea of Fatherhood\par \par \uc2 For men whose partners had abortions, there is often a sadness about what life could have been like for them as fathers, said Jennifer Reich, a sociology professor at the University of Colorado Denver who has studied the male discourse on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Albertie said his girlfriend's pregnancy had seemed like an opportunity to be the kind of father he had wanted in his own childhood. ''I had grown up in a single-parent home with my mother, and I thought this is my chance to do something different,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 He is still hoping to become a father at some point. ''I feel like there are a lot of guys out there who, like me, want to have kids and build stable homes,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Reich said that for many of the men she interviewed for her research, pregnancies represented ''something aspirational.'' ''Men are more likely to talk about parenting in somewhat romantic ways -- about being a role model or playing catch in the yard,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 That was how Theo Purington, 37, a salesman in Florida, felt back in college when his girlfriend at the time told him she was pregnant. He wanted her to keep the fetus, he said, and planned to take on a job while finishing school so that he could support a family. She wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I said that I would take full custody of the child and I even had my mom talk to her,'' Mr. Purington said. ''She just wasn't having it.''\par \par \uc2 The experience had left him feeling helpless, he said. ''The night before the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I tried one more time to see if she would change her mind,'' Mr. Purington said. ''She said no, so I kissed her stomach and I said, 'Daddy loves you and I'll see you in heaven,' and I left.''\par \par \uc2 He turned to his Catholicism, seeking solace, and now runs two crisis pregnancy centers in Florida and New Mexico. Crisis pregnancy centers are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations that try to persuade women not to get abortions. ''We provide adoption options and help the women who choose to keep the baby with classes, supplies or whatever else they need,'' he said. ''We don't just say 'choose life' and send them on their way.''\par \par \uc2 A Rigid Idea of Masculinity\par \par \uc2 For some men, the emotional complexity of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- feelings of loss, relief or guilt -- is further compounded by broader societal expectations of them, experts said. They may feel compelled to remain stoic in the face of adversity, acting as pillars of support for their partners. When Dr. Nguyen conducted a survey of over 200 men who had accompanied their partners to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, roughly 40 percent would not have terminated the pregnancy if the choice had been up to them. But, he said, ''they almost universally wanted to be supportive of their partners.''\par \par \uc2 ''In in-depth interviews, men seldom discussed personal concerns about and preferences regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Dr. Nguyen's research paper said, possibly indicating that men had ''prioritized their relationship and the well-being of their partner over their own concerns.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Reich said many of the men she interviewed told her she was the first person they had ever talked to about their experiences. ''It is really challenging for men to acknowledge that there may be an emotional component that they're grappling with,'' she said. Even in particularly tragic circumstances, men said they felt as if they needed permission to grieve.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, Matthew Markman, a software salesman in California, and his wife, who was 20 weeks pregnant, learned that their son had a rare heart defect. If his wife carried the fetus to term, he would be unlikely to survive after birth, their doctor told them.\par \par \uc2 The news was crushing for Mr. Markman and his wife; they had been trying to have a baby for over a year and had used in vitro fertilization multiple times. After three rounds of implantation, one embryo stuck, but resulted in a miscarriage. This pregnancy had been their fifth embryo. They had even settled on a name, Elijah, ''because my grandfather's name starts with an E and he had recently passed,'' said Mr. Markman, 37, who considers himself in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 When the couple made the difficult decision to terminate the pregnancy, Mr. Markman felt that because his wife was the one carrying the fetus and who had to go through the procedure, he had to be the stronger one in that moment of despair. They cremated the remains and spread the ashes on Muir Beach in Northern California.\par \par \uc2 ''I personally had to take leave from work for a couple of months because it was emotionally a very difficult period,'' he said. ''It took me a while to realize that it was OK that the experience was hard on me as well.''\par \par \uc2 Life After \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Another recurring theme in the responses from men who wrote to The Times was the belief that they would not be where they are today without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is a vast body of peer-reviewed research that connects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to a woman's emotional, physical and financial outcomes, including the landmark Turnaway study, which followed women who had been denied abortions for five years and found that they were more likely to be living in poverty or be unemployed than women who were able to get abortions. But experts noted that only a few researchers have explored the long-term consequences of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a man's life trajectory.\par \par \uc2 One study, published in 2019 in the Journal of Adolescent Health, found that men whose partners had abortions while they were in college were more likely to graduate and earn higher incomes than men whose partners did not.\par \par \uc2 Nam Phan, a 30-year-old engineer in Massachusetts and a father of two, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  his wife got when they were dating as teenagers helped them to eventually become better parents. At the time, they were not financially equipped, nor did they feel mature enough to look after a baby. ''I don't think either of us could even manage taking care of ourselves at that point,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Their first child, who is now 5, was also an unplanned pregnancy, but they felt far more prepared for parenthood when they found out about him; they had graduated from college, settled into their jobs, gotten married and were about to buy a home.\par \par \uc2 ''It isn't lost on us that having a kid back then would have really changed our lives significantly,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 When Kevin Barhydt was 19, the woman he was seeing became pregnant. Immediately, he was overcome with ''panic and enormous fear.''\par \par \uc2 ''There wasn't a 'gee, let's do a pros-and-cons list' moment,'' said Mr. Barhydt, now a 60-year-old analyst and an author in New York. By that point, he had already had a rough run at life. He had been abused, he had dropped out of high school and he was struggling with alcohol addiction. They were not in a place to look after a newborn, and he did not even have money to pay for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Barhydt's second experience with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place about a year or so later with another woman, when he was still grappling with his addiction. He described that time in his life as ''terrible.''\par \par \uc2 ''The idea of having a child then just seemed insane,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Both abortions, Mr. Barhydt said, nudged him toward ''a trajectory of healing.'' He went to college and found a stable job. He got married and had two sons, and he has now been sober for over three decades. Those memories, though, are still painful.\par \par \uc2 ''Do I pray for forgiveness? Yes, I do,'' Mr. Barhydt said. ''Do I wish there had been a way to have kept my children? Yes. Do I regret my decision at the time? Not at all.''\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Kate Winslett.\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Kate Winslett.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY RICHARD BEAVEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; PEYTON FULFORD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; IAN C. BATES FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JAMES JACKMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; VANESSA LEROY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220704ei740004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
