\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Strategic Shift In Bid to Regain  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision to reverse Roe, far from settling the matter, instead has launched court and political battles across the states likely to go on for years.\par \par \uc2 Attempting to recover from their staggering loss in the Supreme Court, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have mounted a multilevel legal and political attack aimed at blocking and reversing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in courts and at ballot boxes across the country.\par \par \uc2 In the week since the court overturned Roe v. Wade, litigators for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have rolled out a wave of lawsuits in nearly a dozen states to hold off bans triggered by the court's decision, with the promise of more suits to come. They are aiming to prove that provisions in state constitutions establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the Supreme Court's decision said did not exist in the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Advocates of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are also working to defeat ballot initiatives that would strip away a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to pass those that would establish one, in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is contingent on who controls the governor's mansion or the state house.\par \par \uc2 And after years of complaints that Democrats neglected state and local elections, Democratic-aligned groups are campaigning to reverse slim Republican majorities in some state legislatures, and to elect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to positions from county commissioner to state supreme court justices that can have influence over the enforcement of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''You want all the belt and suspenders that you can have,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which litigated Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the case the Supreme Court used to overturn Roe. While the Supreme Court said it wanted to end five decades of bitter debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its decision has set up a new fight, one that promises to be long and equally bitter.\par \par \uc2 Although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters say their strategy is promising, the path ahead is slow and not at all certain. Polls show that Americans overwhelmingly say that the decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be made by women and their doctors rather than state legislatures. But Republican-controlled state legislatures have passed hundreds of restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last decade, and legislative districts are heavily gerrymandered to protect Republican incumbents. Litigation in state courts will be decided by judges who in many cases have been appointed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups say their cases relying on state constitutions offer a viable path forward to establish Roe-like protections in states. Even in conservative states such as Oklahoma and Mississippi, they see an opportunity to overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and establish a constitutional backstop against further restriction.\par \par \uc2 But in other places, the goal of the litigation is to at least temporarily restore or preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, now that the court's decision stands to make it illegal or effectively so in more than half the states, which include 33.5 million women of childbearing age.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, for example, though the state constitution expressly says there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the legal challenge has allowed three clinics to continue serving women whose plans to end their pregnancies were thrown into disarray by the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''We have to take these things in steps,'' said Joanna Wright, a partner at Boies Schiller Flexner who, with the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is leading the Louisiana case. ''A lot can change in a day, a month and six months. Time will tell the rest, but this is the fight right now.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision has flipped the dynamic of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy that has prevailed for the half-century since Roe, when anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups chipped away at legal access by electing like-minded state legislators and passing increasingly strict laws, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups could rely on Roe to prevent the most severe bans from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 Now, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and congressional Republicans discuss federal legislation that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country after 15 weeks of pregnancy, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have begun climbing the steeper and narrower path state by state.\par \par \uc2 ''Democracy is a collective action,'' said Ms. Wright, ''and what we've seen from the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is an ability to mobilize all the pieces of that,'' which culminated, she added, with the overturning of Roe.\par \par \uc2 By Friday, the groups had temporarily blocked bans from taking effect in Utah, Kentucky, Louisiana, and Florida; judges have set hearings over the next several weeks to consider permanent injunctions. But they lost bids to hold off bans in Ohio and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups had argued for decades that the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left up to states, not to unelected judges in Washington. Within hours of the court's decision, Republican politicians and law enforcement officials announced that bans, once held up in court, were now in effect, and would be prosecuted to the full extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 They decried their opponents' strategy in the courts.\par \par \uc2 ''To say that the State Constitution mandates things like dismemberment abortions, I just don't think that's the proper interpretation,'' Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican, said after Florida's ruling temporarily blocking a law prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The legal challenges argue that the Supreme Court's decision has thrown \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and patients into chaos, subjecting them to state laws that are often unclear, contradictory or confusing. Women have shown up for appointments only to be told that their pregnancies have now progressed too far to be eligible for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under new laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. In Montana, Planned Parenthood clinics said recently that they would require proof of residency for women seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, because of fears that prosecutors in other states might prosecute anyone who helped their residents get abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have not given up on hopes of federal action to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : They are pushing President Biden to use a declaration of a public health emergency to allow the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to authorize out-of-state providers to prescribe and provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to women in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have made them illegal.\par \par \uc2 They are also pushing the Senate to suspend its filibuster and pass the Women's Health Protection Act, which would establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability, as was provided in Roe. Mr. Biden reversed himself on Thursday to say that he supported lifting the filibuster, though he also told a group of Democratic governors that there were not enough votes in the Senate to do so.\par \par \uc2 But by necessity, the groups are focused first on state action.\par \par \uc2 While the Supreme Court's opinion, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , declared that it was returning the regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation ''to the people and their elected representatives,'' its decision has delivered the issue to other courts, those in the states.\par \par \uc2 ''If the Supreme Court and Justice Alito and the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates thought this was going to settle the question, they are going to see just how wrong they are,'' \uc2 Anthony Romero\uc2 , executive director of \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , said in a news conference Friday alongside lawyers and leaders from the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Planned Parenthood. ''The proliferation of litigation that will embroil the states in our country for years to come is going to underscore that this is not settled in the public's mind.''\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits argue that state constitutions offer more protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the federal constitution, either by quirk of state tradition or history. Some, such as Florida's, include an explicit right to privacy. In Kentucky, lawyers argue their constitution provides a right to ''bodily autonomy'' as well as privacy. The Roe decision in 1973 declared that the U.S. Constitution afforded a right to privacy that included a woman's right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; while the Supreme Court overturned that decision, it generally cannot overturn what states say in their own constitutions.\par \par \uc2 The suit in Utah, one of the most conservative states in the country, seeks to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under a provision of the state constitution -- adopted in 1896 -- that provides that ''both males and female citizens of this state shall enjoy equally all civil, political and religious rights and privileges.''\par \par \uc2 Largely because of the influence of the \uc2 Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints\uc2 , the constitution also ensures that state residents have the right to plan their own families; the lawsuit argues this includes the right to choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even in states where lawsuits have been successful, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups say they are playing Whack-a-Mole. In Utah, as soon as the court put a temporary injunction on the state's trigger law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a legislator declared that the state's law against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 18 weeks, which courts had upheld while Roe was in effect, was now the operative law.\par \par \uc2 ''We're in a chess game and we haven't gotten checkmate,'' said Karrie Galloway, the chief executive of Planned Parenthood in Utah. ''We're doing check, check, check, check. Unfortunately, we're doing check, check, check with pregnant people and their families' lives.''\par \par \uc2 In Kansas, a state Supreme Court decision in 2019 found a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the constitutional provisions for ''equal and inalienable natural rights, among which are life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.'' But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups put an initiative on the primary ballot this August that seeks to amend the constitution to explicitly say that it does not include a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the Legislature has the authority to pass further restrictions.\par \par \uc2 That vote will be the first indication of how much the outrage seen in response to the Supreme Court's decision translates into support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in elections.\par \par \uc2 Historically, voters who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been more driven to vote on the issue than those who support a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But polls taken since the leak of a draft of the Supreme Court's decision in May and the final decision in late June show that those who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- largely Democrats -- now cite it as one of their top concerns, and that the court's decision has motivated them more to vote in elections this fall.\par \par \uc2 Vote Pro Choice is attempting to turn out women, especially Black and Latina women, to vote in races including county commissioners, judges and sheriffs, particularly in states such as Texas and Georgia with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws -- positions responsible for enforcing anti-harassment laws outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, and deciding whether to give government money to crisis pregnancy centers, which anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have used to steer women away from abortions.\par \par \uc2 Democrats need to learn from the successes of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and Republicans, said Sara Tabatabaie, the group's chief political officer.\par \par \uc2 ''We have been out-raised, out-organized and out-funded for 50 years, and that is across the board,'' she said. But she is encouraged by the number of people who say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will guide their votes in November: ''In moments of tragedy, I am hopeful that there comes solidarity and increased clarity.''\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, \uc2  rights groups rolled out a wave of lawsuits in nearly a dozen states to hold off bans triggered by the decision. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A17) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220704ei740003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
