\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Young Women Who Fight  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Graham \par \uc2 1,909 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DALLAS -- The rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has been received by many American women with a sense of shock and fear, and warnings about an ominous decline in women's status as full citizens.\par \par \uc2 But for some women, the decision meant something different: a triumph of human rights, not an impediment to women's rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I just reject the idea that as a woman I need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be successful or to be as thriving as a man in my career,'' said Phoebe Purvey, a 26-year-old Texan. ''I don't think I need to sacrifice a life in order to do that.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade was a political victory, accomplished by lobbyists, strategists and campaign professionals over the course of decades. But it was also a cultural battle, fought by activists across the country including those in the exact demographic that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates warn have the most to lose in the new American landscape: young women.\par \par \uc2 Often pointed to by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders as the face of the movement, a new generation of activists say they are poised to continue the fight in a post-Roe nation.\par \par \uc2 Many, but not all of them, are Christian conservatives, the demographic that has long formed the core of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. Others are secular and view their efforts against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a progressive quest for human rights. All have grown up with once unthinkable access to images from inside the womb, which has helped convince them that a fetus is a full human being long before it is viable.\par \par \uc2 Many believe the procedure should be banned at conception -- that even the earliest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effectively murder. But they embrace the mainstream anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  view that women are victims of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''industry'' and should not be prosecuted, putting them at odds with the rising ''abolitionist'' wing of the movement calling for women to be held legally responsible for their abortions.\par \par \uc2 And overwhelmingly, these young women reject the notion that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to their own -- or any woman's -- success.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Purvey said she supported a legal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception. But she is increasingly uncomfortable with using the term ''pro-life'' to describe herself, because it evokes an emphasis on preventing abortions at any cost, rather than on helping women. She prefers ''life-affirming,'' and she works at a pregnancy-resource clinic in Dallas that uses the same term to describe the free and low-cost prenatal care, postpartum doula services, lactation consulting and other services offered to its primarily Black, low-income clientele.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Purvey was born in a Mexican community in South Texas. Her mother was poor and in an unstable marriage, she said, and received prenatal care from Planned Parenthood. The family later received financial and emotional support from their church, which inspired Ms. Purvey to provide help to women like her mother.''At this point in my life, I hold the rights of pre-born children and women equally, but I consider myself a little more women-forward and women-centered,'' she said. ''That's where a lot of the change happens.''\par \par \uc2 A clear majority of Americans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal with few or no exceptions, according to a Pew survey taken in March. Women ages 18 to 29 are significantly likelier than older women to say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be generally legal, and that it is morally acceptable. Just 21 percent of young women say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be broadly illegal, Pew found.\par \par \uc2 The movement's minority status is part of its appeal, said the historian Daniel K. Williams, who has written about the history of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy.\par \par \uc2 ''The pro-life movement up until now has had the best of both worlds in terms of attracting young people,'' Mr. Williams said. It positions itself as a countercultural alternative to mainstream conventional wisdom but also champions broadly popular beliefs about the importance of justice and equality for the vulnerable. Historical touchstones -- commonplace within the movement and much-disputed outside it -- include the Civil Rights movement and 19th and early 20th century suffragists.\par \par \uc2 For the majority of American women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, other women's enthusiasm for stripping away their own constitutional rights can be baffling and enraging, a profound betrayal. But overwhelmingly, young anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  women view themselves as human rights activists -- happy warriors on the right side of history.\par \par \uc2 ''It's always been a movement of youth,'' said Kristan Hawkins, who became the president of Students for Life of America in 2006, when she was 21. She recalled a line she heard from the conservative activist Alveda King, a niece of \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . who is a frequent presence at anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  events: ''When young people join your movement, you know victory is on its way.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawkins's organization -- which supports a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting at conception and opposes oral contraceptives -- now claims 1,250 groups on campuses across the country, from middle schools to graduate schools. Its signs reading ''I Am the Pro-Life Generation'' are ubiquitous at anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawkins says the contemporary anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement offers a more empowering vision to young women than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights feminism does.\par \par \uc2 ''This is 2022, not 1962,'' she said, observing that women's legal rights to do things like secure loans have advanced dramatically since the pre-Roe era.\par \par \uc2 If feminism tells young women they need to be able to end their pregnancies in order to achieve their educational and career goals, she said, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement tells them they can have it all.\par \par \uc2 Young people have been part of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement since the 1970s. The annual March for Life in Washington, held around the anniversary of the Roe v. Wade decision, now draws buses of students from around the country to what has transformed over the years into a festive youth-driven rally.\par \par \uc2 Clare Fletcher, 26, a teacher at a Catholic school in Illinois, has attended the March for Life at least 10 times. She grew up in a strongly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  home, influenced by the understanding that her adopted younger sister's birth mother had pursued an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before giving birth.\par \par \uc2 The event, and the movement it represents, have always been ''a source of joy and celebration of life and fun and community,'' Ms. Fletcher said.\par \par \uc2 When she was a teenager, her father led a caravan of buses from Louisiana that she described as raucous road trips involving matching hats, flash mobs, tourist stops and silly songs. She can still sing from memory an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  parody of the Taio Cruz hit ''Dynamite'': ''Just wanna celebrate and be pro-life saying ayo, gotta pray-o!''\par \par \uc2 As a teenager active online, Lauren Marlowe had a hazy understanding that supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was what ''nice'' people did. But she was drawn to think differently in part because of advancements in ultrasound images. ''Back then, when they looked at ultrasounds and thought it was a clump of cells, that was all they could see,'' she said, referring to a phrase used by the philosopher Judith Jarvis Thomson in a famous 1971 defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Marlowe, 22 and the social media coordinator for Students for Life of America, launched a small line of ''trendy pro-life clothes'' as an undergraduate at \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 . The line touts a T-shirt with the word ''pro-life'' spelled out in the ''Friends'' font, and a hoodie with the cheeky slogan ''Just a clump of cells.''\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, Kailey Cornett, 28, said she anticipated that her work as chief executive of Hope Clinic for Women, a ''life-affirming'' center that provides services and support to pregnant women, would grow busier in a post-Roe landscape. Tennessee has a trigger law that is expected to go into effect by mid-August and will ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly all cases, including rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cornett received what she experienced as a life calling from God while attending a Christian youth convention as a teenager: to ''love on'' young women facing unplanned pregnancies. She volunteered at a pregnancy resource center in Arizona in high school, and pursued a degree in nonprofit management with the goal of leading one.\par \par \uc2 Reading the progressive Christian writer Sarah Bessey's book ''Jesus Feminist'' showed her that her faith and her care for women did not have to be in tension. ''Oh my gosh, I can be both,'' she recalled. ''It turns out I was a feminist the whole time, but I had this wrong definition of it.''\par \par \uc2 Hers is one of the rare pregnancy resource centers that provides some forms of birth control to clients. Though the clinic does not engage in politics, she is cautiously in favor of the state's coming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, including its lack of exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm a firm believer that trauma leads to trauma,'' she said. A woman ''ending the life of that child will not make her pain go away.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Nashville police said they were investigating an arson attempt at Hope Clinic, part of a rash of vandalism incidents at pregnancy resource centers across the country. The police said the building was spray-painted with the words ''Janes Revenge,'' the name of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group that has claimed responsibility for some incidents.\par \par \uc2 Young women whose activism is not connected to religious belief are relative newcomers to the movement, where they make up a small but boisterous niche.\par \par \uc2 Kristin Turner started a chapter of a youth climate group in her hometown, Redding, Calif. Her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  bio includes her pronouns (she/they) and support for \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . She describes herself as a feminist, an atheist and a leftist.\par \par \uc2 At 20, she is also the communications director for Progressive Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Uprising, whose goals include educating the public about ''the exploitative influence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Industrial Complex through an anti-capitalist lens.''\par \par \uc2 Recently, she started a punk band called the EmbryHoez with a friend in San Francisco. One of their songs is called ''The Hotties Will Dismantle Roe'':\par \par \uc2 They say it's empowerment / They say it's women's rights / But all I see's oppression / And might makes right.\par \par \uc2 Progressive Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Uprising, founded last year, emphasizes ''direct action,'' including ''pink-rose rescues,'' in which activists enter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to distribute roses attached to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  information.\par \par \uc2 ''If someone is committing violence against another human being,'' Ms. Turner said, ''then property lines should not be respected.'' She said she has been arrested three times in activist settings, include twice performing ''rescues.''\par \par \uc2 ''The reality is, people are dying,'' she said. ''I think that whatever privilege I have, I need to use that and leverage it.''\par \par \uc2 Kristin Turner, 20, of San Francisco, describes herself as a feminist, an atheist and a leftist.; Ms. Turner wore a button featuring a gravestone with the word ''Roe'' at a protest of a Planned Parenthood fund-raiser. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY RACHEL BUJALSKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Phoebe Purvey, who works at a pregnancy-resource clinic in Dallas, prepared blankets and bibs for first-time mothers. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHELBY TAUBER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Kailey Cornett is the chief executive of Hope Clinic for Women in Nashville, which provides services to pregnant women. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STACY KRANITZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A17) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220706ei730000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
