\par\par \uc2 ON TECH NEWSLETTER\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 Her Rage Morphed Into a Viral Resource\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shira Ovide \par \uc2 1,613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Veronica Risinger made a little online spot for neighbors to share information on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Then 30,000 people joined.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade on Friday, Veronica Risinger started what she thought would be a tiny \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group for her neighbors in Kansas City., Mo., to share resources for people seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 But Risinger's phone notifications never stopped. Her little group has morphed into a 30,000-member nationwide nexus for rage, heartfelt personal stories and education among people anxious about a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 Risinger doesn't understand how her \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group grew so large. At one point, she said, there were 10,000 people waiting to join the private group, USA Camping Resource Center. (''Camping'' is a code word used in some online conversations about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 She wasn't ready for the time commitment or for the responsibility of providing people with a place to express their feelings and to find information about the fast-changing legal status of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the U.S. But she feels that she must do the best she can. ''I don't want to be doing this, but this is the world that we're living in,'' Risinger told me.\par \par \uc2 That one woman became an unwitting leader of a large forum for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters shows that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  remains a place where Americans hash out their hopes and fears. As it did for \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups that sprang up to promote the false claim of widespread 2020 election fraud, emotion can help online communities to go viral in ways that surprise their creators and the company itself.\par \par \uc2 On Friday morning, Risinger was at work, and seething. Within minutes of the Supreme Court decision, her home state of Missouri enacted an immediate ''trigger law'' banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I was filled with such rage,'' she told me this week. ''I thought, OK, I can give people a place where they can get together.''\par \par \uc2 Risinger has experience overseeing other \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups, and she started USA Camping Resource Center largely -- or so she thought -- for people in her area who shared her anger and who wanted to vent, to talk about what they could do or to offer help. ''Maybe that could have worked if it were me and 10 people in my neighborhood,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Almost immediately, it became far more than that. People have flooded the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group, telling raw personal stories about having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or being denied one. And they ask many questions about how these bans could affect them.\par \par \uc2 Risinger said that one woman in Missouri messaged the group because she was worried about her legal risk from a planned procedure for implanted birth control. (Birth control remains legal across the U.S. The Kansas City Star has more information about access in Missouri.) Women also asked whether data from period-tracking apps might be used by law enforcement to build a case against them for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Period-tracking apps can be a risk, but other data can be more incriminating.)\par \par \uc2 For those seeking information, the group directs people as much as possible to authoritative sources, including organizations experienced in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy and assistance.\par \par \uc2 People seem to find out about the group mostly by word of mouth, and the response has amazed Risinger, who now finds herself moderating posts at all hours, including minutes after running a race on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 But the group became highly active very quickly, and Risinger said she felt overwhelmed. She said she quickly shifted her plans: ''We had the group before we really knew what we were doing.''\par \par \uc2 As is done in many other \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups, Risinger decided that the best approach to keep the conversation from going off the rails was to make rules and to enforce them strictly. The top rule: ''Don't be a jerk,'' and there's no room for debate about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 People who want to join the group must first answer why they support ''camping.'' (Some people apparently believe it's a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group about the outdoors.) Each newcomer as well as each post is approved by a moderator, of which there are now about 20 whom Risinger enlisted after the group became too big for one person to handle.\par \par \uc2 To protect people from the safety risks that could come with offering rides or homes to strangers, the group started to block posts that proposed personal assistance for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointments.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's critics have said for years that groups on the site have become hubs for unchecked conspiracy theories or health misinformation. And fringe groups on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and elsewhere online have spread false ideas or calls to violence in response to the Roe ruling. After \uc2 Facebook\uc2  flagged some comments in Risinger's group for breaking the company's rules against violence and incitement, she told members to stop suggesting violence as a solution to problems. (Everything that I read in the group was respectful and nonviolent.)\par \par \uc2 I asked Risinger how people's behavior might be different on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  than in an in-person community. Are people more emotionally vulnerable, or more cruel?\par \par \uc2 ''Are people worse on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  than they are in real life? Almost always yes,'' she said. But on the other hand, the group would never have so rapidly expanded without social media, she said.\par \par \uc2 Risinger says she doesn't know what the future holds for the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  community that she created in a fit of rage. She hopes to harness people's energy into productive action. There are discussions about mobilizing around an August election in Kansas, in which voters will decide whether to remove the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''The momentum we have is something that is not lost on me,'' Risinger said. ''I'm going to do whatever I can to make sure it gets put to good use.''\par \par \uc2 Lessons from a hellish vacation plan\par \par \uc2 Hoo boy, Brian X. Chen , a consumer tech columnist for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , has a very 2022 travel horror story. And he offers advice to avoid his bad experience.\par \par \uc2 Last year I wrote a column about using tech to make travel plans in a pandemic. That advice still applies: Check your destination's travel and tourism websites for potential requirements about Covid-19 vaccines and test results, and carry a digital copy of your health data on your smartphone.\par \par \uc2 I have another hard-earned lesson from my own bad experience.\par \par \uc2 I booked plane tickets this year to fly across the country for a wedding in the fall. I used \uc2 Hopper\uc2 , a travel price comparison service, to find and book the cheapest Delta flights.\par \par \uc2 I regret it. Over the past few months, Delta changed my flight itinerary multiple times and even canceled one of my connecting flights. After I waited on hold for more than an hour to speak to a Delta representative, the company put me on a different flight. Problem solved? No.\par \par \uc2 When I didn't receive a confirmation of my new ticket, I reached out again. A Delta representative told me that \uc2 Hopper\uc2  had canceled the ticket after Delta changed it. The only way to reach \uc2 Hopper\uc2  is through email support, whose response could take up to 48 hours, unless you want to pay more.\par \par \uc2 After an email to Hopper and another call to Delta, the airline put me on a different flight again. I sent another email to Hopper, asking that the company not touch the reservation. Crisis averted. I hope.\par \par \uc2 The lesson? If you're booking travel online, simplify the process. Airlines are short-staffed, and you might face long waits for customer support. Travel booking services like \uc2 Expedia\uc2  and \uc2 Hopper\uc2  may save you money, but they may not be worth it.\par \par \uc2 Cut out the middlemen, and book directly with the airlines and hotels. That way, if you run into problems, you are dealing with one company and not two.\par \par \uc2 Read more summer travel advice from Seth Kugel, who tries to help Times readers resolve travel problems.\par \par \uc2 Before we go ...\par \par \uc2 Deleting your period tracker won't protect you. Text messages, email receipts and \uc2 Google\uc2  searches contain more data about people who seek abortions than a tracker does, my colleague Kash Hill wrote.\par \par \uc2 From Wednesday's On Tech: Our data is a curse, with or without Roe .\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  moved to restrict items and search results related to L.G.B.T.Q. people and issues on its website in the United Arab Emirates after the government pressured the company, my colleague Karen Weise reported. It's the latest example of compromises that tech companies make to operate in restrictive countries.\par \par \uc2 ''Everything happens so much.'' That odd but perfect tweet posted 10 years ago is regularly recirculated when people feel overwhelmed by what's happening around them, The Atlantic explained. There's also a mysterious back story for what seemed to be a computer-generated \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account but wasn't. (A subscription may be required.)\par \par \uc2 Hugs to this\par \par \uc2 The running (sort of) of the goats. Each summer, a park in New York City enlists goats to munch on invasive plants. They were released into the park on Wednesday, and not all of them are exactly hoofing it. (See what I did there?!)\par \par \uc2 We want to hear from you. Tell us what you think of this newsletter and what else you'd like us to explore. You can reach us at ontech@nytimes.com.\par \par \uc2 If you don't already get this newsletter in your inbox, please sign up here . You can also read past On Tech columns .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Shira Inbar FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220703ei730006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
