\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Supreme Court Overturns Block on a 1925 Law That Bans  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabella Kwai \par \uc2 504 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision, overturning a lower court ruling that had temporarily blocked the ban, allowed a 1925 law to take effect.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court allowed a 1925 law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take effect late on Friday night, overturning a lower court ruling that had temporarily blocked it.\par \par \uc2 The decision was the latest in a series of legal battles across the country following the Supreme Court's decision on June 24 to overturn Roe v. Wade, a nearly half-century-old ruling that had established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, that meant a 1925 law written before Roe that had banned abortions and punished those who performed them with possible imprisonment automatically went into effect, said \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the state's attorney general. Though the law was not enforced after Roe was decided in 1973, it had nevertheless remained on the books.\par \par \uc2 That ban was temporarily blocked by a Harris County judge after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics sued for a stay, arguing that it had effectively been repealed after the landmark Roe ruling.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday morning, Mr. Paxton called the reversal of the stay a ''pro-life victory!'' on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Our state's pre-Roe statutes banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas are 100% good law,'' he wrote. ''Litigation continues, but I'll keep winning for Texas's unborn babies.''\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court's decision on Friday partially overruled the decision of the lower district court. Both sides will continue to argue their case involving the old law on July 12 in front of that district court. The lifting of the freeze did not allow for criminal enforcement of the ban, \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  said in a news release. The group is representing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the legal fight.\par \par \uc2 ''Extremist politicians are on a crusade to force Texans into pregnancy and childbirth against their will, no matter how devastating the consequences,'' said Julia Kaye, an attorney with the A.C.L.U. Reproductive Freedom Project.\par \par \uc2 Even before the overturning of Roe, a law that was passed last year in Texas had allowed abortions only up to about six weeks of pregnancy. And when the Roe decision was overturned, a ''trigger ban'' was activated to prohibit all abortions in Texas from the moment of fertilization, with rare exceptions including to save the life of the mother. That law goes into effect at the end of July.\par \par \uc2 Texas is one of several states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have quickly taken their campaign to the courts, aiming to block or delay \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and bans from going into effect. By Friday, they had succeeded in Utah, Kentucky, Louisiana and Florida.\par \par \uc2 The Whole Woman's Health Clinic in McAllen, Texas. A state Supreme Court ruling allowed a 1925 \uc2  ban to take effect, though legal arguments on the issue will continue later this month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica G. C\u225\'00\'E1rdenas for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220703ei730003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
