\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Drives Red and Blue Americas In Opposite Directions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 2,169 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , climate change, guns and much more, two Americas -- one liberal, one conservative -- are moving in opposite directions.\par \par \uc2 Pressed by Supreme Court decisions diminishing rights that liberals hold dear and expanding those cherished by conservatives, the United States appears to be drifting apart into separate nations, with diametrically opposed social, environmental and health policies.\par \par \uc2 Call these the Disunited States.\par \par \uc2 The most immediate breaking point is on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as about half the country will soon limit or ban the procedure while the other half expands or reinforces access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the ideological fault lines extend far beyond that one topic, to climate change, gun control and L.G.B.T.Q. and voting rights.\par \par \uc2 On each of those issues, the country's Northeast and West Coast are moving in the opposite direction from its midsection and Southeast -- with a few exceptions, like the islands of liberalism in Illinois and Colorado, and New Hampshire's streak of conservatism.\par \par \uc2 Even where public opinion is more mixed, like in Ohio, Wisconsin, Georgia, North Carolina and Texas, the Republican grip on state legislatures has ensured that policies in those states conform with those of the reddest states in the union, rather than strike a middle ground.\par \par \uc2 The tearing at the seams has been accelerated by the six-vote conservative majority in the Supreme Court, which has embraced a muscular states-rights federalism. In the past 10 days the court has erased the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , narrowed the federal government's ability to regulate climate-warming pollution and blocked liberal states and cities from barring most of their citizens from carrying concealed guns outside of their homes.\par \par \uc2 ''They've produced this Balkanized house divided, and we're only beginning to see how bad that will be,'' said David Blight, a Yale historian who specializes in the era of American history that led to the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 Historians have struggled to find a parallel moment, raising the 19th-century fracturing over slavery; the clashes between the executive branch and the Supreme Court in the New Deal era of the 1930s; the fierce battles over civil rights during Reconstruction and in the 1950s and early 1960s; and the rise of armed, violent groups like the Weather Underground in the late '60s.\par \par \uc2 For some people, the divides have grown so deep and so personal that they have felt compelled to pick up and move from one America to the other.\par \par \uc2 Many conservatives have taken to social media to express thanks over leaving high-tax, highly regulated blue states for red states with smaller government and, now, laws prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Others have transited the American rift in the opposite direction.\par \par \uc2 ''I did everything I could to put my mouth where my money was, to bridge the divide with my own actions,'' said Howard Garrett, a Black, gay 29-year-old from Franklin, Tenn., who ran for alderman in recent years, organized the town's first Juneteenth celebration and worked on L.G.B.T.Q. outreach to local schools, only to be greeted with harassment and death threats.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garrett moved to Washington, D.C., last year. ''People were just sick in their heart,'' he said, ''and that was something you can't change.''\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , history seems to be riffing on itself.\par \par \uc2 Both supporters and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights see a parallel to the abolition of slavery.\par \par \uc2 As states like Illinois and Colorado vow to become ''safe harbors'' for women in surrounding states seeking to end their pregnancies, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates see an echo of past efforts by antislavery states in the North. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents see themselves as emancipating the unborn, and often compare the Roe decision's treatment of the fetus to the Dred Scott ruling in 1857 that denied Black people the rights of American citizenship.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives are not resting on their victories: The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, long predicated on returning the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  to elected representatives in the states, talks now about putting a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban before Congress.\par \par \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2 , a leading social conservative and senior official in the Trump administration, invoked the struggle of Black Americans for equality, saying the 10 years that passed between the Supreme Court's Brown v. Board of Education decision ending ''separate but equal'' segregation and Congress's passage of the Civil Rights Act in 1964 mirrored the struggle ahead on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I cannot see us living in two Americas where we have two classes of human beings in this country: some protected fully in law, some who are not protected at all,'' said Mr. Severino, now the vice president for domestic policy at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , a conservative think tank.\par \par \uc2 On climate change, the court's decision to limit federal regulatory powers has underscored the impasse in Congress over legislation expressly limiting emissions of climate-warming pollutants like carbon dioxide and methane.\par \par \uc2 But again, the states are stepping unto the breach. States from Virginia to Maine have banded together to limit carbon emissions under the Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative. In the West, California, Oregon and Washington are pursuing a Pacific Coast Collaborative to coordinate clean fuel standards and move toward zero-emission cars.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm strongly supportive of the E.P.A. having the power to regulate greenhouse gas emissions and other pollutants from fossil fuel,'' said Katie Dykes, commissioner of the \uc2 Connecticut Department of Energy and Environmental Protection\uc2  and the chairwoman of the East Coast initiative's board of directors. ''But R.G.G.I. has been in place since 2009 and has provided clear, predictable signals to the power sector and to the states in the alliance. It becomes only more relevant if we see federal authority curtailed.''\par \par \uc2 Fossil fuel states are moving in the opposite direction, pressing for more exploration and more production of coal, oil and natural gas and for fewer emissions regulations, putting local jobs and overall economic priorities ahead of the impact of climate change.\par \par \uc2 On guns, the District of Columbia and 11 states, including Delaware and Rhode Island just this week, have banned some weapons and accessories like high-capacity magazines in response to mass shootings across the country. Republican states, in contrast, have passed and continue to pass laws that allow for the carrying of concealed or unconcealed firearms with no permits necessary.\par \par \uc2 The Republican governor of Texas, Greg Abbott, signed legislation last year trying to nullify a decades-old federal ban on silencers. And a new law in New Hampshire is meant to stop state law enforcement agencies from cooperating with federal agencies to enforce federal firearms laws that do not match New Hampshire's.\par \par \uc2 ''It's the biggest problem we're facing now,'' said Sean Holihan, the state legislative director at the Giffords Law Center to Prevent Gun Violence. ''If most of the Northeast, parts of the Midwest and all of the West Coast want to pass good gun-safety legislation, that doesn't mean someone in Chicago can't go to basically any state that borders his and buy a gun.''\par \par \uc2 As conservative states move to bar gender transition therapies for people under 18, California's Legislature is considering a bill that would void any subpoena seeking information about people traveling to the state for such care. But Alabama's attorney general, invoking the Supreme Court's reasoning in its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, said this week that federal courts must allow the state's ban on gender-transition care to take effect.\par \par \uc2 And one state's banned books are another's teen summer reading list.\par \par \uc2 Jake Grumbach, a \uc2 University of Washington\uc2  political scientist who began studying the fragmentation of the nation more than a decade ago, said America was living through a ''hyper-drive of state-based dissolution,'' but he cautioned against looking regionally, instead locating the fault line between cities and their suburbs on one side and rural areas on the other. A voter in Milwaukee and one in rural Wisconsin, he said, are as different ideologically as one in Oklahoma and one in New York City.\par \par \uc2 However, gerrymandering and restrictions on voting access in Republican states have given conservatives a greater institutional advantage than the edge Democrats have in more liberal states, Mr. Grumbach said. He pointed to a gerrymandered legislative map in New York that was blocked and to similar maps that have gone forward in Louisiana, Ohio and Florida.\par \par \uc2 The tensions of the moment might be most acute in the porous borderlands of red and blue America. The St. Louis Board of Aldermen, responding to Missouri's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, is considering using $1 million in Covid relief funds to instead aid women seeking abortions across the border in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Anne Caprara, the chief of staff to the Democratic governor of Illinois, \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state used to serve a few hundred out-of-state women per week. Since the overturning of Roe a week ago, she said, it's been ''several thousand.''\par \par \uc2 ''The governor is committed to Illinois being an oasis,'' she said. ''He isn't shifting on that, but there's no question that's a burden.''\par \par \uc2 Gun rights laws like the protections for silencers in Texas ''are edging back toward the idea of nullification, that states should be able to ignore federal law, an idea that grew directly out of slavery,'' said Bethany Lacina, a \uc2 University of Rochester\uc2  political scientist who studies federalism in different countries. ''But you can imagine a day where there's a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the governor of California says, 'Eh, we're just not going to do that.' It's all very double-edged weapons.''\par \par \uc2 Conservatives might see the coming years as the moment to pivot toward amassing more national power, if they can seize Congress in November and the White House in 2024. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have always had two arguments in favor of ending Roe v. Wade: a legal case that the Constitution does not include a right to end a pregnancy, and a moral case that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is murder.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Severino, again invoking segregation, said that until the legislative and executive branches of government stepped in with the Civil Rights and Voting Rights Acts in the 1960s, recalcitrant states failed to integrate their schools after the Supreme Court ordered them to in 1954.\par \par \uc2 ''There are deep parallels here,'' he said. ''Then, it was what is the value of a human person, measured between Black and white Americans. Here it is the value of a person within the womb and outside it.''\par \par \uc2 As the political divide between the states becomes more pronounced, what political scientists call ''sorting'' may accelerate. The conservative Illinois billionaire \uc2 Kenneth Griffin\uc2  announced last week that he had moved to Miami from Chicago, and would take \uc2 Citadel\uc2 , his hedge fund, with him. He told his employees that Florida offered a better corporate environment.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Ms. Caprara said the Pritzker administration routinely boasts of the state's welcoming political environment, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are codified and companies will never find themselves in the position the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2  now occupies in Florida -- squeezed between a conservative government constraining gay and transgender rights, and liberal consumers demanding a corporate pushback.\par \par \uc2 ''Companies don't want to have to deal with people boycotting their business, or struggling to get people to move to them, especially younger workers,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Joanna Turner Bisgrove, 46, a family physician at Rush University Medical Center in Chicago, had worked her whole professional life in Oregon, Wis., a small town south of Madison, when her hospital was purchased by a Catholic health care chain, that began restricting abortions and transgender care. After the Wisconsin Legislature took up the issue of transgender girls in sports, she said, friends of her gender-fluid child became magnets for bullying so bad that it made the local news.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a year ago, the Bisgroves finally moved across the red-blue border, to Evanston, Ill., where, Dr. Bisgrove said, her children would be accepted and her medical practice could thrive.\par \par \uc2 ''In the end,'' she said, ''my morals would not square with what I could do.''\par \par \uc2 The six-vote conservative majority in the Supreme Court has accelerated and deepened the fault lines dividing the country. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Anti-\uc2  demonstrators in Kansas. About half the country will limit or ban \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY AVID ROBERT ELLIOTT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); A rally for \uc2  rights in Chicago. Illinois vowed to be a ''safe harbor'' for \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Opposing sides of a Florida law that bans instruction on sexual orientation and gender identity at a rally outside Disney World. (PHOTOGRAPH BY OCTAVIO JONES/REUTERS); At the N.R.A. convention in May. Eleven states recently banned some weapons, but others allowed firearms with no permits. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MERIDITH KOHUT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220703ei730003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
