\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Confrontation Over First Amendment May Loom in Post-Roe Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeremy W. Peters \par \uc2 1,106 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Without a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , questions about how states can regulate speech about it suddenly become much murkier.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court declared clearly last week that there is no federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But how the decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization affects the right to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains far from settled, teeing up what legal experts said was a looming confrontation over whether the First Amendment allows censoring speech about a medical procedure that will become illegal in much of the country.\par \par \uc2 In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is outlawed, for instance, how can women be informed of their options elsewhere? Will media outlets be free to publish advertisements across state lines from providers operating in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been outlawed -- as the Supreme Court long ago ruled they could? Will women be allowed to accept information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they then decide to terminate a pregnancy but don't live in a state that allows it? What if states move to make this kind of exchange of information illegal?\par \par \uc2 ''You have the right, ostensibly, to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Will Creeley, the legal director for the Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression. ''The question then becomes whether that talk can be regulated if it aids and abets or encourages others to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''That presents a First Amendment problem,'' he added. ''Will you still have the First Amendment right to speak when you no longer have the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? And that is going to get messy.''\par \par \uc2 A top anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobbying group, the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , recently proposed model legislation for states that would make it a crime to pass along information ''by telephone, the internet or any other medium of communication'' that was used to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Many states essentially did just that before Roe v. Wade was decided in 1973. And it is not clear whether courts will find that the protections afforded to speech in the Constitution still apply to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters as they look to circumvent the raft of new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Many legal scholars say such protections still should apply. It is generally not illegal to promote an activity that isn't a crime. And since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain legal in many places, offering information about how women can obtain one legally shouldn't become a crime, scholars said.\par \par \uc2 ''There will be some tougher questions,'' said Eugene Volokh, a professor of law at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2 . ''Let's say you're deliberately advertising in a Texas newspaper and say, 'Would you like an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Go to this New Mexico \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.' Can Texas prohibit that?''\par \par \uc2 One parallel is gambling. Casino operators in Las Vegas advertise all the time in places where the activity isn't allowed. But the Supreme Court has permitted limits on the practice. Mr. Volokh pointed to a 1993 decision, United States v. Edge Broadcasting Company, which upheld a federal law banning advertising about lotteries in states that don't allow them.\par \par \uc2 The last time the Supreme Court directly addressed whether these kinds of bans could apply to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was nearly 50 years ago, in Bigelow v. Virginia, when it invalidated a law that made it a misdemeanor to publish information that encouraged a woman to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or aided her in obtaining one.\par \par \uc2 The case dealt with a newspaper called The Virginia Weekly, which had run an ad from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group in New York City that helped women, many from out of state, find doctors who could legally perform the procedure. ''Abortions are now legal in New York. There are no residency requirements,'' the ad said, promising ''STRICTLY CONFIDENTIAL'' services, seven days a week.\par \par \uc2 The paper's managing editor was tried and convicted. A lower court upheld the conviction, ruling that the First Amendment didn't protect advertisements for commercial purposes.\par \par \uc2 But the Supreme Court said speech is not stripped of First Amendment protections if it happens to have a commercial aspect, and declared that one state like Virginia could not bar citizens from another like New York ''from disseminating information about an activity that is legal in that state.''\par \par \uc2 Some First Amendment experts who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said they would not be surprised to see states try again to criminalize such speech.\par \par \uc2 ''Give it three weeks,'' said Lynn Greenky, a professor at \uc2 Syracuse University\uc2  who teaches First Amendment issues.\par \par \uc2 Major First Amendment decisions from the Supreme Court in the past have started with cases involving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In those, such as McCullen v. Coakley in 2014, the court has recognized that states can set limits on speech outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics but also ruled that those limits cannot be so restrictive that they burden First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Greenky said First Amendment protections should apply not just to those offering counseling to women outside clinics but also to those offering to help women get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  where it is legal.\par \par \uc2 ''If the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  folks can speak to patients, can't pro-choice folks counsel women who seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?'' she said.\par \par \uc2 With so many issues now ripe for legislative intervention by the states, it is unclear where opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will focus their resources and whether restricting how information can be shared will be a priority.\par \par \uc2 Mark L. Rienzi, a law professor at the \uc2 Catholic University of America\uc2 , posed a hypothetical that he said could test how courts might apply the First Amendment in a post-Roe world: What if New York State bought billboards in Texas offering to help women there make the journey north for a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rienzi, who argued before the Supreme Court on behalf of Eleanor McCullen, who offered counseling and support to women outside clinics in hopes of persuading them not to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said he thought Texas would be on shaky legal ground if it tried to prosecute anyone in New York for the billboard.\par \par \uc2 ''The underlying thing is it's not a crime where it's happening,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Rienzi added that the new legal landscape was uncharted, leaving very little certain about what laws states are now free to pass. ''I think in some ways we don't really know because the political process has essentially been jammed for 50 years,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 A demonstrator outside the Supreme Court. Without a federal right to \uc2 , questions about how states can regulate speech about it have become murkier. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220702ei720004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
