\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Acts To Curb Fallout From 2 Rulings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonah E. Bromwich and Nicholas Fandos \par \uc2 1,512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers passed measures that would prohibit concealed weapons in many public places, as well as an amendment that would initiate the process of enshrining the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 A week after the Supreme Court issued monumental rulings loosening restrictions on carrying guns and overturning the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , New York enacted sweeping measures designed to blunt the decisions' effects.\par \par \uc2 In an extraordinary session convened by Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  that began Thursday and carried late into Friday evening, the State Legislature adopted a new law placing significant restrictions on the carrying of handguns and passed an amendment that would initiate the process of enshrining the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The new legislation illustrates the growing distance between a conservative-led court that has reasserted its influence in American political life and blue states such as New York -- one of the most left-leaning in the nation, where all three branches of government are controlled by Democrats and President Biden easily triumphed over \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 As Republican-led states race rightward, the New York Legislature's moves this week provided a preview of an intensifying clash between the court and Democratic states that will likely play out for years to come.\par \par \uc2 ''We're not going backwards,'' Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat, said at a news conference in Albany on Friday and who later that evening signed the gun bill into law. ''They may think they can change our lives with the stroke of a pen, but we have pens, too.''\par \par \uc2 She made remarks on the coming July 4 holiday, asking New Yorkers to remember what was being commemorated: ''the founding of a great country that cherished the rights of individuals, freedoms and liberty for all.''\par \par \uc2 ''I am standing here to protect freedom and liberty here in the state of New York,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 The state's new gun law bars the carrying of handguns in many public settings such as subways and buses, parks, hospitals, stadiums and day cares. Guns will be off-limits on private property unless the property owner indicates that he or she expressly allows them. At the last minute, lawmakers added Times Square to the list of restricted sites.\par \par \uc2 The law also requires permit applicants to undergo 16 hours of training on the handling of guns and two hours of firing range training, as well as an in-person interview and a written exam. Applicants will also be subject to the scrutiny of local officials, who will retain some discretion in the permitting process.\par \par \uc2 Enshrining the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's constitution will be more onerous. Amending the State Constitution is a yearslong process, which starts with passage by the Legislature. Then, after a general election, another session of the Legislature must pass the amendment before it is presented to voters in a ballot referendum.\par \par \uc2 But lawmakers took a first step on Friday when the legislature passed the Equal Rights Amendment, which along with guaranteeing rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and access to contraception, prohibited the government from discriminating against anyone based on a list of qualifications including race, ethnicity, national origin, disability or sex -- specifically noting sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, and pregnancy on the list of protected conditions.\par \par \uc2 Some of the protected classes in the language of the measure appeared to anticipate future rulings from the court, which also indicated last week that it might overturn cases that established the right to same-sex marriage, same-sex consensual relations and contraception.\par \par \uc2 ''We're playing legislative Whac-a-Mole with the Supreme Court,'' said Senator \uc2 Brad Hoylman\uc2 , a Manhattan Democrat. ''Any time they come up with a bad idea we'll counter it with legislation at the state level.''\par \par \uc2 ''Civil liberties are hanging in the balance,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 New York Republicans, who have little sway in either legislative chamber, split over the Equal Rights Amendment, with seven voting in favor and 13 against. But they were united in opposition against the concealed carry bill, saying Democrats had tipped the balance much too heavily in favor of restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''Instead of addressing the root of the problem and holding violent criminals accountable, Albany politicians are preventing law-abiding New Yorkers, who have undergone permit classes, background checks and a licensing process from exercising their constitutional right to keep and bear arms,'' said \uc2 Robert Ortt\uc2 , the Republican leader in the Senate, who is from Western New York.\par \par \uc2 The session in Albany took place just a week after the Supreme Court -- now fully in the control of right-leaning justices, three of whom were appointed by Mr. Trump -- moved forward on a pair of issues that have long animated conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Last Thursday, it struck down New York's century-old law that was among the strictest in the nation in regulating the public carrying of guns. The decision found that the law, which required that applicants demonstrate that they had a heightened need to carry a firearm in public, was too restrictive and allowed local officials too much discretion. The court invited states to update their laws.\par \par \uc2 The following day, the court overturned Roe v. Wade, stripping Americans of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly 50 years after it was first granted.\par \par \uc2 New York will be the first of six states directly affected by the gun ruling to pass a new law restricting the carrying of guns. Similar legislation has been proposed in New Jersey, where a top legislative leader said this week it was possible lawmakers could be called back into session this summer to respond.\par \par \uc2 Officials there have coordinated directly with their counterparts in New York, and the two laws are expected to share many features.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Hawaii have also said that they are working on new firearm legislation, while officials in California, Maryland and Massachusetts are discussing how the court's decision should be addressed in their states.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, \uc2 Andrea Stewart-Cousins\uc2 , the Senate majority leader in New York, said that Democratic leaders were adamant that New York ''model what state legislatures all over this nation can do to reaffirm the rights of their residents.''\par \par \uc2 She defended the new concealed carry restrictions as a common-sense safety measure that balanced Second Amendment interests laid out by the Supreme Court with concerns about legally carrying weapons into sensitive or crowded places, particularly in dense urban areas like New York City already facing a scourge of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 ''We didn't want an open season,'' Ms. Stewart-Cousins said. ''In the environment that we are in, it is important to make sure that we are creating a process that respects what the Supreme Court has said but allows us to keep New Yorkers as safe as possible.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans disagreed.\par \par \uc2 ''If you look at the sensitive areas, it's the entire state, it's everywhere,'' said State Senator Andrew Lanza, a member of Republican leadership from Staten Island. ''So much of New York is now considered a sensitive area for the purpose of this law that there is no such thing as a concealed permit anymore.''\par \par \uc2 Two other states, California and Vermont, have also moved closer to placing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in their constitutions. This week, lawmakers in California advanced a constitutional amendment enshrining the right, and in November, residents of both states will vote on whether to make the amendments law.\par \par \uc2 Republican-led states are charging hard in the other direction. So far, seven have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the justices' decision last week. Another half dozen, including Texas and Tennessee, are expected to quickly follow suit. And voters in states like Kentucky and Kansas will soon decide whether to ban the practice via referendum.\par \par \uc2 By pushing so quickly in New York to respond to both rulings, Ms. Hochul and Democratic legislative leaders have kept the state on a path set by her predecessor, \uc2 Andrew M. Cuomo\uc2 , during Mr. Trump's presidency. Before allegations of sexual misconduct from a number of women led to his resignation, Mr. Cuomo was explicit in juxtaposing his agenda with the priorities of the Republican president, saying in late 2018 that he was declaring New York's independence.\par \par \uc2 State Senator \uc2 Michael Gianaris\uc2  of Queens, the deputy majority leader, said New Yorkers should expect more of the same in the coming years.\par \par \uc2 ''The Supreme Court seems intent on destroying this country one decision at a time,'' he said in an interview. ''Today, we made clear that New York will stand up against this rollback of rights that we've come to expect in the United States. You can expect we will continue doing this as the court keeps issuing horrible decisions.''\par \par \uc2 Luis Ferr\u233\'00\'E9-Sadurn\u237\'00\'ED, Grace Ashford, Tracey Tully and Hurubie Meko contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Luis Ferr\u233\'00\'E9-Sadurn\u237\'00\'ED, Grace Ashford, Tracey Tully and Hurubie Meko contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lt. Gov. Antonio Delgado presiding over the State Senate on Thursday in a special session to respond to Supreme Court decisions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CINDY SCHULTZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220702ei7200041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
